// Prehistoric Life (26 November 2026), part 2: dinosaur species.
ADDART({id:"trex",t:"Tyrannosaurus rex",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦖",w:"Tyrannosaurus",r:["dino","dinobirds","triceratops","spinosaurus","kpgimpact","paleontology","carnotaurus","dilophosaurus","deinosuchus","giganotosaurus","edmontosaurus"],
p:[["The tyrant lizard king","Tyrannosaurus rex was one of the largest meat-eating land animals that ever lived."],["A bone-crushing bite","Its bite was among the strongest of any known land animal."],["Among the last dinosaurs","It lived at the very end of the age of dinosaurs, until about 66 million years ago."]],
l:"Tyrannosaurus rex, whose name means ‘tyrant lizard king’, was a giant meat-eating dinosaur that lived in western North America at the end of the Cretaceous Period, about 68 to 66 million years ago. Adults grew to about 12 meters long and weighed around 8 metric tons or more. It walked on two powerful legs, had a huge head with teeth up to about 30 centimeters long including the root, and tiny two-fingered arms. T. rex was one of the last non-bird dinosaurs, dying out when an asteroid struck Earth 66 million years ago. It is one of the best-known dinosaurs in the world, with dozens of skeletons found.",
s:[["Discovery","The fossil hunter Barnum Brown, working for the American Museum of Natural History, found the first partial skeleton in the Hell Creek area of Montana in 1902. In 1905, the museum’s president Henry Fairfield Osborn named it Tyrannosaurus rex. Most fossils since have come from Montana, South Dakota, Wyoming and the Canadian provinces of Alberta and Saskatchewan."],["Built to bite","T. rex had a broad skull with strong jaws and thick, banana-shaped teeth that could crush bone. Studies estimate its bite force at around 35,000 newtons or more, several times that of a lion or alligator. Fossil bones of Triceratops and Edmontosaurus with T. rex tooth marks show what it ate, and its dung, preserved as coprolites, contains crushed bone."],["Senses and speed","T. rex had a large brain area for smell and forward-facing eyes that gave it good depth perception. Adults were too heavy to run very fast; computer models suggest a top walking speed of roughly 20 kilometers per hour, though younger, lighter animals were probably quicker. Its tiny arms were strong for their size, but their use is still debated."],["Growing up","Growth rings in bones show that T. rex grew rapidly as a teenager, gaining hundreds of kilograms a year, and that most lived fewer than 30 years. Young tyrannosaurs had longer legs and thinner skulls than adults. Some skeletons once thought to be young T. rex are now thought by many scientists to belong to a separate, smaller tyrannosaur, Nanotyrannus."]],
k:[["Lived","About 68–66 million years ago"],["Where","Western North America"],["Length","About 12 m (40 ft)"],["Weight","About 8 metric tons or more"],["Diet","Meat"],["Named","1905, by Henry Fairfield Osborn"]],
d:[["c.68 million years ago","Tyrannosaurus rex appears"],["66 million years ago","T. rex dies out"],["1902","Barnum Brown finds the first skeleton in Montana"],["1905","T. rex is named"],["1990","‘Sue’ is discovered in South Dakota"],["2020","The skeleton ‘Stan’ sells at auction for $31.8 million"]],
s2:[["Sue and Stan","‘Sue’, found in South Dakota on August 12, 1990, by the fossil hunter Sue Hendrickson, is one of the most complete and largest T. rex skeletons. After a legal battle, it was sold at auction in 1997 to the Field Museum in Chicago for $8.36 million. Another skeleton, ‘Stan’, sold for $31.8 million in 2020 and is now displayed in Abu Dhabi."],["Feathers or scales?","Some early relatives of T. rex, such as Yutyrannus from China, had feather-like filaments. Patches of fossil skin from T. rex itself show small scales, so adults may have had mostly scaly skin, perhaps with some feathers."]],
sp:["Big, but not the biggest","T. rex was not the longest meat-eating dinosaur; Spinosaurus and perhaps Giganotosaurus were as long or longer. But T. rex was among the heaviest and had one of the most powerful bites of any land animal known."],
g:[["Theropod","A group of mostly meat-eating, two-legged dinosaurs"],["Coprolite","Fossilized dung"],["Bite force","How hard an animal can press with its jaws"]],
f:["T. rex lived closer in time to humans than to Stegosaurus.","Its largest teeth were about the size of a banana.","T. rex is the official state fossil of Montana."],
fun:"Tyrannosaurus rex lived closer in time to us than to Stegosaurus."});

ADDART({id:"triceratops",t:"Triceratops",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦏",w:"Triceratops",r:["trex","dino","stegosaurus","ankylosaurus","paleontology","pachycephalosaurus","protoceratops"],
p:[["Three-horned face","Triceratops means ‘three-horned face’."],["A giant frill","A huge bony frill spread out from the back of its skull."],["A plant-eater","It used a parrot-like beak to snip tough plants."]],
l:"Triceratops was a large, plant-eating dinosaur that lived in western North America about 68 to 66 million years ago, at the very end of the Cretaceous Period. It had a short horn on its nose, two long horns over its eyes, and a solid bony frill at the back of its skull. Adults reached about 8 to 9 meters long and weighed several metric tons. Triceratops lived at the same time and in the same places as Tyrannosaurus rex, which hunted it. It is one of the most common dinosaurs found in the rocks of the Hell Creek Formation.",
s:[["A case of mistaken identity","In 1887, a pair of large horns found near Denver, Colorado, was described by Othniel Charles Marsh as belonging to a giant extinct bison. After more complete skulls were found in Wyoming, Marsh realized his mistake and in 1889 named the animal Triceratops."],["Horns and frill","Triceratops’s skull, including the frill, could be more than 2 meters long, one of the largest skulls of any land animal. Its horns and frill may have helped in defense, but they were probably also used to show off and recognize each other, and in shoving contests between rivals. Healed injuries on some skulls fit the shape of another Triceratops’s horns."],["Eating","Triceratops had a toothless beak at the front of its mouth and rows of hundreds of shearing teeth farther back. These teeth were arranged in groups called dental batteries, with new teeth constantly replacing worn ones. It probably ate low-growing plants such as ferns, cycads and palms."],["Fighting T. rex","Fossil Triceratops bones with T. rex tooth marks show that tyrannosaurs fed on them. Some bite marks on a Triceratops horn healed over, showing that the animal survived an attack."]],
k:[["Lived","About 68–66 million years ago"],["Where","Western North America"],["Length","About 8–9 m"],["Weight","About 6–12 metric tons"],["Diet","Plants"],["Named","1889, by Othniel Charles Marsh"]],
d:[["c.68 million years ago","Triceratops appears"],["66 million years ago","Triceratops dies out"],["1887","Horns are mistaken for a bison"],["1889","Triceratops is named"],["2010","Scientists propose that Torosaurus is an adult Triceratops"]],
s2:[["Torosaurus debate","Torosaurus was a similar dinosaur with a larger frill that had holes in it. In 2010, two scientists suggested it was simply a fully grown Triceratops. Others disagree, and the question is still debated."],["Skin","Fossil skin impressions show that Triceratops had large, rounded scales. Some scientists have suggested it may have had bristle-like structures, as its relative Psittacosaurus did, but there is no direct evidence of this."]],
sp:["A state symbol","Triceratops is the state fossil of South Dakota and the state dinosaur of Wyoming."],
g:[["Frill","A bony shield at the back of the skull"],["Dental battery","Rows of tightly packed, replaceable teeth"],["Ceratopsian","A group of beaked, often horned plant-eating dinosaurs"]],
f:["The first Triceratops horns were mistaken for those of a bison.","Its skull was one of the largest of any land animal.","Triceratops lived at the same time as T. rex."],
fun:"Triceratops’s skull could be longer than a tall adult human."});

ADDART({id:"stegosaurus",t:"Stegosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦕",w:"Stegosaurus",r:["dino","allosaurus","diplodocus","ankylosaurus","paleontology","ceratosaurus","kentrosaurus"],
p:[["Plates on its back","Stegosaurus had two rows of tall bony plates along its back."],["A spiked tail","Its tail ended in four long spikes."],["Jurassic giant","It lived about 155 to 145 million years ago."]],
l:"Stegosaurus, meaning ‘roof lizard’, was a large plant-eating dinosaur of the Late Jurassic Period, about 155 to 145 million years ago, in what is now western North America, with related fossils found in Portugal. It grew to about 9 meters long and weighed around 5 metric tons. It is famous for the two alternating rows of large, kite-shaped plates along its back and the four sharp spikes at the end of its tail. Stegosaurus had a small head, a short neck and front legs much shorter than its back legs, so it carried its head close to the ground.",
s:[["Discovery","Fossil collectors working for Othniel Charles Marsh found Stegosaurus in Colorado, and he named it in 1877. Marsh first thought the plates lay flat on its back like roof tiles, which is where the name comes from. Later finds showed that the plates stood upright."],["What were the plates for?","The plates were made of bone filled with blood vessels and covered in a tough sheath. They were too fragile to be good armor. Scientists think they were used mostly for display, helping Stegosaurus recognize its own kind and attract mates, and may also have helped control body temperature."],["The thagomizer","The four tail spikes, each up to about 90 centimeters long, were serious weapons. An Allosaurus tail bone from Utah has a wound that matches a Stegosaurus spike. The spikes are nicknamed the ‘thagomizer’ after a 1982 Far Side cartoon by Gary Larson, and scientists now use the word too."],["A small brain","Stegosaurus had a brain about the size of a lemon, small for an animal its size. An old idea claimed that it had a ‘second brain’ in its hips; this was really a space in the spine that may have held a gland or nerve tissue."]],
k:[["Lived","About 155–145 million years ago"],["Where","Western North America, Portugal"],["Length","About 9 m"],["Weight","About 5 metric tons"],["Diet","Plants"],["Named","1877, by Othniel Charles Marsh"]],
d:[["c.155 million years ago","Stegosaurus appears"],["c.145 million years ago","Stegosaurus dies out"],["1877","Stegosaurus is named"],["1982","The ‘thagomizer’ cartoon is published"],["2014","‘Sophie’ goes on display in London"],["2024","The skeleton ‘Apex’ sells for $44.6 million"]],
s2:[["Sophie and Apex","‘Sophie’, at the Natural History Museum in London, is one of the most complete Stegosaurus skeletons ever found. In July 2024, another, nicknamed ‘Apex’, sold at auction in New York for about $44.6 million, the most ever paid for a dinosaur fossil at the time."],["Neighbors","Stegosaurus lived in the Morrison Formation alongside giant long-necked dinosaurs such as Diplodocus and Brachiosaurus and the predator Allosaurus."]],
sp:["State fossil","Stegosaurus is the state fossil of Colorado, where its first fossils were found."],
g:[["Thagomizer","The spiked tail of a stegosaur"],["Plate","A flat, upright bony structure on the back"],["Morrison Formation","Jurassic rocks of the western US rich in dinosaur fossils"]],
f:["The name ‘thagomizer’ comes from a cartoon.","Stegosaurus’s plates were not strong enough to be armor.","Stegosaurus had a brain about the size of a lemon."],
fun:"Scientists borrowed the word ‘thagomizer’ for Stegosaurus’s tail spikes from a 1982 cartoon."});

ADDART({id:"velociraptor",t:"Velociraptor",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦃",w:"Velociraptor",r:["dinobirds","dino","archaeopteryx","trex","birds","therizinosaurus","microraptor","deinonychus","oviraptor","protoceratops"],
p:[["Swift thief","Velociraptor means ‘swift thief’."],["Turkey-sized","It was much smaller than in films, about the size of a turkey."],["Feathered hunter","It had feathers and a big sickle-shaped claw on each foot."]],
l:"Velociraptor was a small, feathered, meat-eating dinosaur that lived in what is now Mongolia and China about 75 to 71 million years ago, during the Late Cretaceous Period. It was about 2 meters long, including its long tail, and weighed around 15 to 20 kilograms, roughly the size of a large turkey. Like its relatives, it had a large, curved claw on the second toe of each foot, which it held off the ground. Velociraptor is one of the dromaeosaurs, a group of dinosaurs closely related to birds, and fossil evidence shows that it had feathered wings.",
s:[["Discovery","Velociraptor was found in the Gobi Desert in 1923 by Peter Kaisen, a member of an American Museum of Natural History expedition led by Roy Chapman Andrews. Henry Fairfield Osborn named it in 1924. Later expeditions from Mongolia, Poland, Russia and China found many more fossils."],["The Fighting Dinosaurs","In 1971, a Polish-Mongolian team found a Velociraptor and a Protoceratops locked together in combat. The Velociraptor’s claw was in the Protoceratops’s neck, and the Protoceratops was biting the Velociraptor’s arm. They were probably buried by a collapsing sand dune. The fossil is a national treasure of Mongolia."],["Feathers","In 2007, scientists found quill knobs, small bumps where large feathers attach, on a Velociraptor forearm bone. This shows that it had wing feathers like a bird’s, even though it could not fly. The feathers may have been used for display, for keeping eggs warm, or for balance while pinning prey."],["Hunting","Velociraptor probably hunted small animals, pinning them down with its feet and sickle claws, much as hawks and eagles do today. A fossil of a pterosaur bone in a Velociraptor’s gut shows that it also scavenged."]],
k:[["Lived","About 75–71 million years ago"],["Where","Mongolia and China"],["Length","About 2 m"],["Weight","About 15–20 kg"],["Diet","Meat"],["Named","1924, by Henry Fairfield Osborn"]],
d:[["c.75 million years ago","Velociraptor lives in the Gobi region"],["1923","The first Velociraptor fossil is found"],["1924","Velociraptor is named"],["1971","The ‘Fighting Dinosaurs’ fossil is discovered"],["2007","Quill knobs show Velociraptor had feathers"]],
s2:[["Films versus fossils","Velociraptors in popular films were based partly on the larger Deinonychus, from North America, and were shown as human-sized and scaly. The real animal was much smaller, had feathers and lived in a desert-like region."],["Relatives","Close relatives include Deinonychus, Utahraptor, which grew to about 6 meters long, and the small four-winged Microraptor."]],
sp:["Not a pack hunter?","Films show raptors hunting in clever packs. Some fossil trackways suggest relatives walked in groups, but other studies of their teeth and growth suggest they may have behaved more like modern reptiles or birds of prey, so the idea of pack hunting is still debated."],
g:[["Dromaeosaur","A family of feathered predatory dinosaurs closely related to birds"],["Quill knob","A bump on bone where large feathers attached"],["Sickle claw","A large curved claw on a raptor’s second toe"]],
f:["Velociraptor was about the size of a turkey.","Velociraptor had feathered arms.","The ‘Fighting Dinosaurs’ fossil shows a Velociraptor and Protoceratops in combat."],
fun:"A real Velociraptor was about the size of a turkey, not a person."});

ADDART({id:"brachiosaurus",t:"Brachiosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦒",w:"Brachiosaurus",r:["diplodocus","argentinosaurus","dino","stegosaurus","allosaurus","plateosaurus","kentrosaurus","amargasaurus"],
p:[["Arm lizard","Brachiosaurus means ‘arm lizard’, because its front legs were longer than its back legs."],["A giraffe-like giant","It held its long neck high to reach treetops."],["Jurassic heavyweight","It lived about 154 to 150 million years ago."]],
l:"Brachiosaurus was a giant, long-necked, plant-eating dinosaur, a sauropod, that lived in western North America during the Late Jurassic Period, about 154 to 150 million years ago. Unlike most sauropods, its front legs were longer than its back legs, so its back sloped down from the shoulders like a giraffe’s. It probably reached about 18 to 22 meters long and may have stood around 9 meters tall or more with its head raised. Estimates of its weight vary widely, from about 28 to more than 50 metric tons.",
s:[["Discovery","Elmer Riggs of the Field Columbian Museum in Chicago found the first skeleton near Grand Junction, Colorado, in 1900, and named it Brachiosaurus altithorax, meaning ‘arm lizard with a deep chest’, in 1903. Only a few fossils of Brachiosaurus have been found in North America."],["A relative from Africa","Many famous ‘Brachiosaurus’ skeletons, including a mounted skeleton in Berlin, came from Tanzania and were once thought to be the same animal. In 2009, scientists showed that the African dinosaur was different enough to have its own name, Giraffatitan."],["High browser","With its long front legs and upright neck, Brachiosaurus could probably feed on leaves high up in conifers, ginkgoes and tree ferns, where other plant-eaters could not reach. It may have needed hundreds of kilograms of plants a day."],["Breathing and blood","Like birds, sauropods had air sacs connected to their lungs and hollow spaces in their bones, which made their huge necks lighter and helped them breathe. Pumping blood up to a raised head would have needed a powerful heart."]],
k:[["Lived","About 154–150 million years ago"],["Where","Western North America"],["Length","About 18–22 m"],["Weight","About 28–50 metric tons (estimates vary)"],["Diet","Plants"],["Named","1903, by Elmer Riggs"]],
d:[["c.154 million years ago","Brachiosaurus appears"],["1900","Elmer Riggs finds the first skeleton"],["1903","Brachiosaurus is named"],["1914","African fossils of Giraffatitan are first described"],["2009","Giraffatitan is given its own name"]],
s2:[["Sauropods","Brachiosaurus belonged to the sauropods, the largest land animals ever. They had small heads, long necks and tails, and pillar-like legs. Other sauropods include Diplodocus, Apatosaurus and Argentinosaurus."],["Babies","Sauropods hatched from eggs about the size of a grapefruit or smaller and grew enormously, increasing their weight thousands of times as they grew up."]],
sp:["A film star","Brachiosaurus became famous worldwide through a scene in the 1993 film Jurassic Park in which it rears up to feed from treetops."],
g:[["Sauropod","A giant, long-necked, plant-eating dinosaur"],["Air sac","A balloon-like part of the breathing system found in birds and dinosaurs"],["Browser","An animal that eats leaves from trees and shrubs"]],
f:["Brachiosaurus’s front legs were longer than its back legs.","The Berlin ‘Brachiosaurus’ is now called Giraffatitan.","Sauropods had hollow bones, like birds."],
fun:"With its head raised, Brachiosaurus could have looked into a third-floor window."});

ADDART({id:"diplodocus",t:"Diplodocus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦕",w:"Diplodocus",r:["brachiosaurus","argentinosaurus","stegosaurus","allosaurus","dino","brontosaurus","plateosaurus","ceratosaurus","amargasaurus"],
p:[["Double beam","Diplodocus means ‘double beam’, after bones under its tail."],["One of the longest","It was one of the longest dinosaurs, about 24 to 26 meters long."],["Dippy","Copies of a Diplodocus skeleton went to museums around the world."]],
l:"Diplodocus was a long-necked, plant-eating sauropod dinosaur that lived in western North America during the Late Jurassic Period, about 154 to 152 million years ago. It was one of the longest dinosaurs known, reaching about 24 to 26 meters, but relatively light for its size, at around 12 to 15 metric tons. Most of its length came from its long neck and very long tail, which tapered to a thin, whip-like tip. Its peg-like teeth sat only at the front of its jaws and were used to strip leaves from plants.",
s:[["Discovery and name","Othniel Charles Marsh named Diplodocus in 1878 from fossils found in Colorado. The name, meaning ‘double beam’, refers to the unusual two-pronged chevron bones under its tail, which may have protected blood vessels when the tail touched the ground."],["Dippy","In 1899, workers funded by the steel tycoon Andrew Carnegie found a nearly complete skeleton in Wyoming. Carnegie paid for plaster casts to be given to museums in London, Paris, Berlin, Vienna, Madrid, Moscow and other cities. The London cast, nicknamed ‘Dippy’, was displayed from 1905 and toured the United Kingdom from 2018."],["A whip tail","Diplodocus’s tail had about 80 bones and ended in a thin tip. Some scientists think it could be cracked like a whip to make a loud noise or to defend itself, though this is debated."],["Feeding","Diplodocus probably fed on ferns, horsetails and lower branches by swinging its long neck, rather than holding it high like Brachiosaurus. Its teeth wore out quickly and were replaced about every month."]],
k:[["Lived","About 154–152 million years ago"],["Where","Western North America"],["Length","About 24–26 m"],["Weight","About 12–15 metric tons"],["Diet","Plants"],["Named","1878, by Othniel Charles Marsh"]],
d:[["c.154 million years ago","Diplodocus lives in the Morrison Formation"],["1878","Diplodocus is named"],["1899","Carnegie’s team finds a near-complete skeleton"],["1905","‘Dippy’ goes on display in London"],["2018","Dippy begins a tour of the United Kingdom"]],
s2:[["Neighbors","Diplodocus shared its world with other sauropods such as Apatosaurus, Brontosaurus and Brachiosaurus, as well as Stegosaurus and the predator Allosaurus."],["Supersaurus","A close relative, Supersaurus, may have been even longer, perhaps more than 33 meters, making it one of the longest animals ever to walk on land."]],
sp:["Gifts of science","Carnegie’s gift of Diplodocus casts helped make dinosaurs famous around the world in the early 20th century, and many of the casts still stand in museums today."],
g:[["Chevron","A small bone below a tail bone"],["Sauropod","A giant, long-necked, plant-eating dinosaur"],["Cast","A copy made by pouring material into a mold"]],
f:["Diplodocus’s tail had about 80 bones.","Copies of Diplodocus were given to museums on several continents.","Diplodocus replaced its teeth about once a month."],
fun:"A single Diplodocus was about as long as two school buses parked end to end."});

ADDART({id:"spinosaurus",t:"Spinosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐊",w:"Spinosaurus",r:["trex","dino","paleontology","sahara","croc","baryonyx","giganotosaurus","sarcosuchus"],
p:[["Spine lizard","Spinosaurus had a tall sail of spines on its back."],["Fish-eater","It had crocodile-like jaws and probably ate a lot of fish."],["Longer than T. rex","It may have been the longest meat-eating dinosaur."]],
l:"Spinosaurus, meaning ‘spine lizard’, was a large meat-eating dinosaur that lived in North Africa about 99 to 93 million years ago, during the Late Cretaceous Period. It had a long, narrow skull like a crocodile’s, cone-shaped teeth for gripping slippery prey, and a tall ‘sail’ along its back supported by spines more than 1.5 meters long. It may have reached about 14 meters long, making it possibly the longest meat-eating dinosaur known. Spinosaurus lived near huge river systems, and scientists agree it ate fish, but they still debate how much time it spent in water.",
s:[["A lost fossil","The German paleontologist Ernst Stromer named Spinosaurus in 1915 from bones found in the Egyptian desert. The fossils were kept in a museum in Munich, Germany, which was destroyed by Allied bombing in 1944 during World War II. Only Stromer’s drawings and photographs survived."],["New finds","For decades, Spinosaurus was known mostly from Stromer’s records. In 2014, a team led by Nizar Ibrahim described a new partial skeleton from Morocco, and in 2020 the same team described a tall, paddle-like tail, suggesting it swam. Other scientists argue that it waded in shallow water like a giant heron instead."],["Built for fishing","Spinosaurus had nostrils set far back on its snout, so it could put its jaws in water and still breathe. Its bones were very dense, which may have helped it stay underwater. It lived alongside giant fish such as the sawfish Onchopristis and the coelacanth Mawsonia."],["A new species","In February 2026, scientists announced a new species, Spinosaurus mirabilis, from Niger in the central Sahara. Its fossils were found far from the coast, in rocks laid down by rivers, and its skull bore a curved, scimitar-shaped crest. The find suggests that spinosaurs lived across a wide area of inland Africa and hunted fish in rivers, wading like giant herons."]],
k:[["Lived","About 99–93 million years ago"],["Where","North Africa"],["Length","Up to about 14 m"],["Weight","About 7 metric tons (estimates vary)"],["Diet","Mostly fish"],["Named","1915, by Ernst Stromer"]],
d:[["c.99 million years ago","Spinosaurus lives in North Africa"],["1915","Ernst Stromer names Spinosaurus"],["1944","The original fossils are destroyed in Munich"],["2014","A new partial skeleton is described from Morocco"],["2020","Its paddle-like tail is described"],["2026","Spinosaurus mirabilis is named from Niger"]],
s2:[["The sail","Scientists are not sure what the sail was for. It may have been used for display, like a peacock’s tail, to recognize its own kind or to attract mates. Older ideas that it controlled body temperature are less popular today."],["Spinosaur relatives","Spinosaurus belongs to the spinosaurids, a family of fish-eating dinosaurs that included Baryonyx from England and Irritator from Brazil."]],
sp:["A detective story","Rebuilding Spinosaurus has been like solving a puzzle with missing pieces. Each new fossil has changed scientists’ picture of what it looked like and how it lived, and the debate is likely to continue."],
g:[["Sail","A tall fin of skin supported by long spines on the back"],["Spinosaurid","A family of fish-eating dinosaurs with long snouts"],["Holotype","The original specimen used to name a species"]],
f:["The first Spinosaurus fossils were destroyed in World War II.","Spinosaurus’s back spines were more than 1.5 meters tall.","Spinosaurus may have been longer than T. rex."],
fun:"Spinosaurus’s nostrils sat far back on its snout so it could breathe with its jaws in water."});

ADDART({id:"ankylosaurus",t:"Ankylosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐢",w:"Ankylosaurus",r:["triceratops","trex","stegosaurus","dino","paleontology","kentrosaurus"],
p:[["Armored tank","Ankylosaurus was covered in bony armor plates."],["Club tail","Its tail ended in a heavy bony club."],["Lived with T. rex","It lived in North America at the end of the age of dinosaurs."]],
l:"Ankylosaurus was a heavily armored, plant-eating dinosaur that lived in western North America about 68 to 66 million years ago, at the end of the Cretaceous Period. It was the largest known member of the ankylosaurs, reaching about 6 to 8 meters long and weighing around 5 to 8 metric tons. Its back was covered in bony plates and spikes called osteoderms, and its tail ended in a large club made of fused bone, which could swing hard enough to break bones. Its name means ‘fused lizard’.",
s:[["Discovery","Barnum Brown, who also found the first Tyrannosaurus rex, named Ankylosaurus in 1908 from fossils found in Montana. Only a few partial skeletons have been found, so much of what we know about its body comes from better-known relatives such as Euoplocephalus."],["Armor","Ankylosaurus’s armor included flat plates, rows of spikes and small bony studs set in its skin, and even its eyelids may have been protected by bone. Its wide, low body made it hard for predators to flip over."],["The tail club","The tail club was made of large bony plates fused together, and the end of the tail was stiffened by locked tendons. Scientists estimate that a swing could break the leg bones of a large predator. Fossils of a relative, Zuul, show damage on the armor of other Zuul, suggesting they also used their clubs in fights with each other."],["Eating","Ankylosaurus had a wide beak and small, leaf-shaped teeth. It probably ate low-growing ferns and shrubs, and may have had a large gut to break down tough plants."]],
k:[["Lived","About 68–66 million years ago"],["Where","Western North America"],["Length","About 6–8 m"],["Weight","About 5–8 metric tons"],["Diet","Plants"],["Named","1908, by Barnum Brown"]],
d:[["c.68 million years ago","Ankylosaurus appears"],["66 million years ago","Ankylosaurus dies out"],["1908","Barnum Brown names Ankylosaurus"],["2017","A relative is named Zuul after a film monster"],["2022","Studies show Zuul used its tail club against rivals"]],
s2:[["Borealopelta","A relative of Ankylosaurus, Borealopelta, found in a Canadian mine in 2011, is so well preserved that its armor and skin look almost like a statue. Pigment traces suggest it had reddish-brown coloring, darker on top and lighter underneath, which helped it hide."],["Relatives","Ankylosaurs lived for more than 100 million years and on almost every continent, including Antarctica."]],
sp:["Living tanks","With its low, wide body, heavy armor and club tail, Ankylosaurus is often compared to a tank, and it was well protected even from giant predators like T. rex."],
g:[["Osteoderm","A bony plate in the skin"],["Ankylosaur","A group of armored plant-eating dinosaurs"],["Tendon","Tough tissue that connects muscle to bone"]],
f:["Ankylosaurus could swing its tail club hard enough to break bone.","Even its eyelids may have been armored.","Ankylosaurus lived at the same time as T. rex."],
fun:"A relative of Ankylosaurus was named Zuul after a monster in the 1984 film Ghostbusters."});

ADDART({id:"parasaurolophus",t:"Parasaurolophus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🎺",w:"Parasaurolophus",r:["iguanodon","dino","trex","triceratops","paleontology","maiasaura","edmontosaurus"],
p:[["A musical crest","Parasaurolophus had a long, hollow, tube-like crest."],["A duck-billed dinosaur","It belonged to the hadrosaurs, or duck-billed dinosaurs."],["Herd animal","It may have used its crest to call to others."]],
l:"Parasaurolophus was a plant-eating, duck-billed dinosaur, a hadrosaur, that lived in western North America about 76 to 73 million years ago, during the Late Cretaceous Period. It is best known for its long, curved, hollow crest, which swept back from its head and could be more than a meter long. Adults reached about 9 meters long and weighed around 2.5 metric tons. Parasaurolophus could walk on two legs or four, and fed on plants using a toothless beak and hundreds of grinding teeth.",
s:[["Discovery","The Canadian paleontologist William Parks named Parasaurolophus in 1922 from a skull and partial skeleton found in Alberta, Canada. Later species were found in New Mexico and Utah. It is one of the rarer hadrosaurs, known from only a handful of good skeletons."],["What was the crest for?","The crest held tubes connected to the nose. In 1995, scientists used CT scans of a skull and a computer model to show that air blown through the crest would make a low, deep sound, like a trombone or tuba. Parasaurolophus probably used these calls to communicate. The crest may also have helped it recognize its own kind."],["Grinding plants","Hadrosaurs had some of the most complex teeth of any animal. Hundreds of teeth were packed into dental batteries that formed grinding surfaces, which could break down tough plants such as conifer needles."],["Babies","Fossils of very young Parasaurolophus show that the crest started small and grew as the animal grew. A tiny skull found in Utah, nicknamed ‘Joe’, belonged to a baby less than a year old."]],
k:[["Lived","About 76–73 million years ago"],["Where","Western North America"],["Length","About 9 m"],["Weight","About 2.5 metric tons"],["Diet","Plants"],["Named","1922, by William Parks"]],
d:[["c.76 million years ago","Parasaurolophus appears"],["1920","The first skeleton is found in Alberta"],["1922","Parasaurolophus is named"],["1995","Scientists model the sound of its crest"],["2013","A baby skull, ‘Joe’, is described"]],
s2:[["Duck-billed dinosaurs","Hadrosaurs were among the most common plant-eaters of the Late Cretaceous. They include Edmontosaurus, Lambeosaurus and Maiasaura, whose name means ‘good mother lizard’ because its nests show parents cared for their young."],["Mummies","Some hadrosaurs, including Edmontosaurus, have been found as ‘dinosaur mummies’ with skin impressions preserved, showing patterns of scales and even a fleshy crest."]],
sp:["Hear a dinosaur","Museums have built models of the Parasaurolophus crest that visitors can blow through, producing a deep, foghorn-like sound."],
g:[["Hadrosaur","A duck-billed, plant-eating dinosaur"],["Crest","A bony structure on the top of the head"],["CT scan","A scan using X-rays to see inside objects"]],
f:["Parasaurolophus’s crest could make a sound like a trombone.","Hadrosaurs had hundreds of teeth.","Its crest was hollow."],
fun:"Scientists have recreated the deep, trombone-like call that Parasaurolophus may have made through its crest."});

ADDART({id:"allosaurus",t:"Allosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦖",w:"Allosaurus",r:["stegosaurus","diplodocus","trex","brachiosaurus","dino","carnotaurus","dilophosaurus","ceratosaurus"],
p:[["Jurassic hunter","Allosaurus was the top predator of the Late Jurassic in North America."],["Different lizard","Its name means ‘different lizard’."],["Many fossils","It is one of the best-known large meat-eating dinosaurs."]],
l:"Allosaurus was a large, meat-eating theropod dinosaur that lived about 155 to 145 million years ago, during the Late Jurassic Period, in what is now the western United States and Portugal. It grew to about 8.5 to 9.7 meters long and weighed around 2 metric tons. It had a large skull with small horns above its eyes, sharp serrated teeth and strong arms with three clawed fingers. Allosaurus is one of the best-understood large predatory dinosaurs, because thousands of its bones have been found.",
s:[["Discovery","Othniel Charles Marsh named Allosaurus in 1877, the same year as Stegosaurus, during the Bone Wars. Its name, meaning ‘different lizard’, refers to its backbones, which were different from those of other dinosaurs then known."],["The Cleveland-Lloyd Quarry","At the Cleveland-Lloyd Dinosaur Quarry in Utah, more than 10,000 bones have been found, most of them from at least 46 Allosaurus. Scientists still debate why so many predators died in one place; they may have been trapped in mud or died during a drought. Allosaurus is the state fossil of Utah."],["Hunting","Allosaurus probably attacked large plant-eaters by slamming its open upper jaw down like a hatchet, slicing with its teeth. An Allosaurus tail bone with a wound from a Stegosaurus spike, and a Stegosaurus neck plate with an Allosaurus bite, show that the two fought."],["Big Al","In 1991, a Swiss team found an almost complete young Allosaurus in Wyoming, nicknamed ‘Big Al’. Its skeleton has 19 broken bones or infections, showing that life as a predator was hard and painful."]],
k:[["Lived","About 155–145 million years ago"],["Where","Western United States, Portugal"],["Length","About 8.5–9.7 m"],["Weight","About 2 metric tons"],["Diet","Meat"],["Named","1877, by Othniel Charles Marsh"]],
d:[["c.155 million years ago","Allosaurus appears"],["1877","Allosaurus is named"],["1988","Allosaurus becomes Utah’s state fossil"],["1991","‘Big Al’ is found in Wyoming"]],
s2:[["Bigger relatives","Allosaurus’s relatives include Saurophaganax, which may have been larger, and much later giants such as Giganotosaurus and Carcharodontosaurus, which grew as large as T. rex."],["Growing up","Studies of bones show that Allosaurus reached adult size by about age 15 and that few lived past their late 20s."]],
sp:["A different age","Allosaurus lived about 80 million years before Tyrannosaurus rex. The two never met; more time separates them than separates T. rex from us."],
g:[["Theropod","A group of mostly meat-eating, two-legged dinosaurs"],["Serrated","Having a saw-like edge"],["Quarry","A place where fossils or rock are dug out"]],
f:["More than 10,000 bones have been dug from Utah’s Cleveland-Lloyd Quarry.","‘Big Al’ had 19 injuries or infections.","Allosaurus is Utah’s state fossil."],
fun:"The young Allosaurus ‘Big Al’ had 19 broken bones or infections during its life."});

ADDART({id:"iguanodon",t:"Iguanodon",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"👍",w:"Iguanodon",r:["paleontology","dino","parasaurolophus","fossils","geotime","maiasaura","baryonyx"],
p:[["Iguana tooth","Iguanodon was named for teeth that looked like a giant iguana’s."],["Thumb spike","It had a sharp spike on each thumb."],["An early discovery","It was the second dinosaur ever to be scientifically named."]],
l:"Iguanodon was a large, plant-eating dinosaur that lived in Europe about 126 to 122 million years ago, during the Early Cretaceous Period. It grew to about 10 meters long and weighed around 3 to 4 metric tons. It had a horny beak, rows of grinding teeth and a cone-shaped spike on each thumb. Iguanodon could walk on all fours or on its hind legs. Named in 1825, it was the second dinosaur to receive a scientific name, after Megalosaurus, and it played a key part in the discovery of dinosaurs.",
s:[["Discovery","Around 1822, the English doctor and geologist Gideon Mantell, possibly helped by his wife Mary Ann, found large fossil teeth in Sussex, England. They looked like the teeth of an iguana but were far larger, and in 1825 Mantell named the animal Iguanodon, meaning ‘iguana tooth’."],["The thumb on its nose","Early scientists did not know what Iguanodon looked like. Mantell placed the thumb spike on its nose, like a rhinoceros horn. Life-size sculptures made for the Crystal Palace Park in London in 1854, which still stand today, show it as a huge, four-legged reptile."],["The mine at Bernissart","In 1878, coal miners in Bernissart, Belgium, found the skeletons of at least 38 Iguanodon about 322 meters underground. The scientist Louis Dollo reconstructed them standing up like kangaroos; they are displayed in Brussels. Later studies showed that Iguanodon usually walked on four legs with its back level."],["The thumb spike","The spike may have been used for defense or for breaking open fruit and seeds. Its hand was unusual: it had three middle fingers for walking, the spiked thumb, and a flexible fifth finger for grasping."]],
k:[["Lived","About 126–122 million years ago"],["Where","Europe"],["Length","About 10 m"],["Weight","About 3–4 metric tons"],["Diet","Plants"],["Named","1825, by Gideon Mantell"]],
d:[["c.126 million years ago","Iguanodon lives in Europe"],["c.1822","Gideon Mantell finds Iguanodon teeth"],["1825","Iguanodon is named"],["1854","Life-size sculptures are unveiled at Crystal Palace"],["1878","38 skeletons are found in a Belgian coal mine"]],
s2:[["Dinner in a dinosaur","On New Year’s Eve 1853, the sculptor Benjamin Waterhouse Hawkins held a dinner party for scientists inside the mold of his Iguanodon sculpture for Crystal Palace."],["Relatives","Iguanodon belonged to a group of plant-eaters called ornithopods, which later gave rise to the duck-billed hadrosaurs such as Parasaurolophus."]],
sp:["Changing pictures","Iguanodon shows how science changes with new evidence. It went from a rhino-like reptile with a horn on its nose, to an upright kangaroo-like animal, to the four-legged dinosaur scientists picture today."],
g:[["Ornithopod","A group of plant-eating dinosaurs, many walking on two legs"],["Megalosaurus","The first dinosaur to be scientifically named, in 1824"],["Reconstruction","A model of what an extinct animal looked like"]],
f:["Iguanodon’s thumb spike was first put on its nose.","At least 38 Iguanodon skeletons were found in one Belgian coal mine.","Scientists once held a dinner party inside a model Iguanodon."],
fun:"In 1853, scientists held a New Year’s Eve dinner party inside a model of an Iguanodon."});

ADDART({id:"argentinosaurus",t:"Argentinosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦕",w:"Argentinosaurus",r:["brachiosaurus","diplodocus","dino","argentinacountry","paleontology","plateosaurus","giganotosaurus","amargasaurus"],
p:[["A true giant","Argentinosaurus is one of the largest land animals ever known."],["From Argentina","It was found in Patagonia, in southern Argentina."],["Known from a few bones","Only parts of its skeleton have been found."]],
l:"Argentinosaurus was a gigantic, long-necked, plant-eating dinosaur, a titanosaur, that lived in what is now Argentina about 95 to 94 million years ago, during the Late Cretaceous Period. It is one of the largest land animals known. Because only a few bones have been found, including backbones, ribs and part of a leg bone, its size must be estimated by comparing it with relatives. Most estimates put it at about 30 to 35 meters long and 65 to 80 metric tons, roughly the weight of ten or more African elephants.",
s:[["Discovery","In 1987, a rancher named Guillermo Heredia found what he thought was a piece of petrified wood on his farm in Neuquén Province, Argentina. It turned out to be part of a huge leg bone. In 1993, the paleontologists José Bonaparte and Rodolfo Coria named the dinosaur Argentinosaurus huinculensis."],["Giant bones","Some of its backbones are more than 1.5 meters tall. A cast of a complete skeleton, based on its own bones and those of relatives, was put on display at a museum in Argentina in 2007, and another is at the Fernbank Museum in Atlanta, Georgia."],["Titanosaurs","Titanosaurs were the last great group of sauropods, living until the end of the Cretaceous on every continent. Other giant titanosaurs from Argentina include Patagotitan, named in 2017, and Dreadnoughtus."],["How it lived","Argentinosaurus probably ate huge amounts of plants each day, swallowing leaves without chewing. Its eggs were small, around the size of a coconut or smaller, and its babies would have grown enormously. Its main predators may have been large meat-eaters such as Mapusaurus."]],
k:[["Lived","About 95–94 million years ago"],["Where","Neuquén Province, Argentina"],["Length","About 30–35 m (estimated)"],["Weight","About 65–80 metric tons (estimated)"],["Diet","Plants"],["Named","1993, by José Bonaparte and Rodolfo Coria"]],
d:[["c.95 million years ago","Argentinosaurus lives in Patagonia"],["1987","A rancher finds the first bone"],["1993","Argentinosaurus is named"],["2007","A full-size skeleton cast is displayed"],["2017","Its relative Patagotitan is named"]],
s2:[["The biggest dinosaur?","It is hard to know which dinosaur was the biggest, because the largest ones are known from incomplete skeletons. Argentinosaurus, Patagotitan and some others are all contenders."],["Bigger than whales?","The blue whale, which lives today, is heavier than any dinosaur known, reaching more than 150 metric tons. Water supports a whale’s weight, while land animals must hold up their own."]],
sp:["A rancher’s discovery","Argentinosaurus is one of many important fossils found by people who were not scientists. Its discovery helped make Patagonia one of the richest places in the world for dinosaur fossils."],
g:[["Titanosaur","A group of giant sauropod dinosaurs"],["Sauropod","A giant, long-necked, plant-eating dinosaur"],["Patagonia","The southern region of Argentina and Chile"]],
f:["Argentinosaurus was found by a rancher who thought it was petrified wood.","Some of its backbones are taller than an adult person.","It may have weighed as much as ten elephants."],
fun:"The first Argentinosaurus bone was mistaken for a piece of petrified wood."});

ADDART({id:"archaeopteryx",t:"Archaeopteryx",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐦",w:"Archaeopteryx",r:["dinobirds","birds","velociraptor","darwin","evo","microraptor","deinonychus","compsognathus"],
p:[["Ancient wing","Archaeopteryx means ‘ancient wing’."],["Part bird, part dinosaur","It had feathers and wings, but also teeth, claws and a bony tail."],["A famous fossil","It was discovered in Germany just two years after Darwin’s Origin of Species."]],
l:"Archaeopteryx was a small feathered dinosaur that lived about 150 million years ago, during the Late Jurassic Period, in what is now southern Germany. It was about the size of a raven, around 50 centimeters long, with broad wings and flight feathers. But unlike modern birds, it had teeth, three clawed fingers on each wing and a long bony tail. For more than a century it was considered the oldest known bird, and it remains one of the most important fossils ever found, because it shows features of both dinosaurs and birds.",
s:[["Discovery","In 1861, a single fossil feather was described from the Solnhofen limestone of Bavaria, and the German paleontologist Hermann von Meyer named Archaeopteryx. The same year, a nearly complete skeleton was found; it was bought by the Natural History Museum in London. Another, the beautiful Berlin specimen, was found in the 1870s."],["Evidence for evolution","The London specimen was found just two years after Charles Darwin published On the Origin of Species in 1859. It seemed to show the kind of ‘in-between’ creature his theory predicted. In the 1860s, Thomas Henry Huxley pointed out how similar it was to small dinosaurs."],["Could it fly?","Archaeopteryx had asymmetric flight feathers like those of flying birds, suggesting it could fly at least short distances, probably by flapping. Studies of its wing bones in 2018 suggested it flew in short bursts, more like a pheasant than a soaring bird."],["The Solnhofen lagoons","In the Late Jurassic, southern Germany was a warm area of islands and salty lagoons. Animals that fell into the still, low-oxygen water were buried in fine mud, which became the limestone that preserved Archaeopteryx and many pterosaurs, fish and insects in amazing detail."]],
k:[["Lived","About 150 million years ago"],["Where","Southern Germany"],["Length","About 50 cm"],["Weight","About 0.5–1 kg"],["Diet","Small animals"],["Named","1861, by Hermann von Meyer"]],
d:[["c.150 million years ago","Archaeopteryx lives in Bavaria"],["1859","Darwin publishes On the Origin of Species"],["1861","A feather and the London skeleton are found"],["1874","The Berlin specimen is found"],["2025","Studies of the Chicago specimen reveal new feather details"]],
s2:[["How many are there?","Only about a dozen skeletons of Archaeopteryx are known, all from the Solnhofen area. One of the most recently studied, now at the Field Museum in Chicago, was described in 2025 and showed special feathers on the upper arm that may have helped it fly."],["Still the first bird?","Some feathered dinosaurs found in China, such as Anchiornis and Xiaotingia, may be as old or older than Archaeopteryx. Scientists still debate exactly where Archaeopteryx fits in the family tree between dinosaurs and birds."]],
sp:["A link between groups","Archaeopteryx is often called a ‘missing link’. Scientists prefer the term ‘transitional fossil’, because it shows a mix of features from two groups rather than being the single ancestor of all birds."],
g:[["Transitional fossil","A fossil that shows features of an older group and a newer one"],["Asymmetric feather","A feather with one side narrower than the other, used for flight"],["Solnhofen limestone","Fine-grained rock in Germany famous for perfect fossils"]],
f:["Archaeopteryx had teeth.","Only about a dozen skeletons of Archaeopteryx are known.","It was found two years after Darwin’s Origin of Species."],
fun:"Archaeopteryx had teeth, claws on its wings and a long bony tail, unlike any bird alive today."});

;
/* data/prehist3.js */
// Prehistoric Life (26 November 2026), part 3: sea, sky and the ages before and after the dinosaurs.
ADDART({id:"pteranodon",t:"Pteranodon",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪽",w:"Pteranodon",r:["pterosaurs","quetzalcoatlus","mosasaurus","birds","dino","rhamphorhynchus","dimorphodon"],
p:[["Wing without teeth","Pteranodon means ‘wing without tooth’."],["A great glider","Its wings spread up to about 7 meters across."],["Over an inland sea","It soared over a sea that once split North America in two."]],
l:"Pteranodon was a large flying reptile, a pterosaur, that lived about 86 to 84 million years ago, during the Late Cretaceous Period. Its fossils come mostly from the Niobrara Formation of Kansas, which formed at the bottom of the Western Interior Seaway, a shallow sea that then divided North America. Adult males had wingspans of up to about 7 meters, though their bodies were light. Pteranodon had a long, pointed, toothless beak and a backward-pointing crest on its head. It probably fed on fish, which it caught at the sea’s surface.",
s:[["Discovery","Othniel Charles Marsh and his team collected the first Pteranodon fossils in western Kansas in 1870 and 1871. In 1876, Marsh named the animal Pteranodon, noting that it lacked teeth, which made it different from the toothed pterosaurs known from Europe. More than 1,000 specimens have since been found, making it one of the best-known pterosaurs."],["Males and females","Pteranodon fossils come in two main forms. Larger individuals had big crests and narrow hips, while smaller ones had small crests and wider hips, suited to laying eggs. Scientists think the larger ones were males that used their crests for display, as many birds do today."],["Flying","Pteranodon had long, narrow wings, like those of an albatross. It was probably an expert soarer, gliding for long distances over the sea with little flapping. Its bones were hollow and very thin-walled, which kept its weight low, perhaps around 20 to 35 kilograms for a large male."],["Diet","Fossil fish remains have been found in the throat and stomach area of some Pteranodon. It may have caught fish by dipping its beak into the water while flying, or by diving from the surface, as some seabirds do."]],
k:[["Lived","About 86–84 million years ago"],["Where","Central North America (Kansas, South Dakota and nearby)"],["Wingspan","Up to about 7 m"],["Weight","About 20–35 kg"],["Diet","Fish"],["Named","1876, by Othniel Charles Marsh"]],
d:[["c.86 million years ago","Pteranodon soars over the Western Interior Seaway"],["1870","The first Pteranodon fossils are collected in Kansas"],["1876","Pteranodon is named"],["1992","A study shows males and females had different crests"]],
s2:[["The Western Interior Seaway","During much of the Late Cretaceous, a warm, shallow sea stretched from the Gulf of Mexico to the Arctic. It was home to mosasaurs, plesiosaurs, sharks, giant fish and sea turtles, many of which are found as fossils in Kansas today."],["Not a dinosaur","Pteranodon is often called a dinosaur in films and toys, but pterosaurs were a separate group of reptiles, closely related to dinosaurs."]],
sp:["Light as a feather","Even though its wings were as wide as a small plane’s, Pteranodon may have weighed less than an average 10-year-old child."],
g:[["Pterosaur","A flying reptile of the age of dinosaurs"],["Crest","A bony structure on top of the head"],["Western Interior Seaway","A Cretaceous sea that split North America"]],
f:["Pteranodon had no teeth.","More than 1,000 Pteranodon fossils have been found.","Only males had large crests."],
fun:"A large male Pteranodon had wings as wide as a giraffe is tall, yet probably weighed less than a 10-year-old child."});

ADDART({id:"quetzalcoatlus",t:"Quetzalcoatlus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦩",w:"Quetzalcoatlus",r:["pterosaurs","pteranodon","trex","birds","dino"],
p:[["A flying giant","Quetzalcoatlus was one of the largest flying animals ever."],["Wings of 10 meters","Its wingspan was about as wide as a small airplane’s."],["Named after a god","It is named after Quetzalcoatl, the feathered serpent god of the Aztecs."]],
l:"Quetzalcoatlus was a giant pterosaur that lived in what is now Texas about 68 to 66 million years ago, at the very end of the Cretaceous Period. It had a wingspan of around 10 to 11 meters, making it one of the largest flying animals of all time. Standing on the ground, it was roughly as tall as a giraffe, with a long, stiff neck and a huge, toothless beak. Quetzalcoatlus lived inland, far from the sea, and probably fed by walking on all fours and snapping up small animals, much as storks and ground hornbills do today.",
s:[["Discovery","In 1971, Douglas Lawson, a geology student at the University of Texas, found giant wing bones in Big Bend National Park, in western Texas. In 1975, he named the animal Quetzalcoatlus northropi, after the Aztec god Quetzalcoatl and after Jack Northrop, an aircraft designer who built flying-wing airplanes."],["Size","Early estimates suggested a wingspan of up to 15 meters or more. Later studies brought this down to about 10 to 11 meters. Even so, its weight is estimated at about 200 to 250 kilograms, far heavier than any flying bird today."],["Ground stalker","Quetzalcoatlus had long legs and a long, stiff neck. Scientists think it hunted on land, walking through open woodland and plains and picking up small vertebrates, perhaps including baby dinosaurs."],["Taking off","How such a heavy animal could fly was a puzzle. In 2021, a detailed study suggested that it vaulted into the air using its powerful front limbs, pushing off with a leap of about 2.5 meters and then flapping. Once in the air, it could soar long distances."]],
k:[["Lived","About 68–66 million years ago"],["Where","Texas, United States"],["Wingspan","About 10–11 m"],["Weight","About 200–250 kg"],["Diet","Small animals"],["Named","1975, by Douglas Lawson"]],
d:[["c.68 million years ago","Quetzalcoatlus lives in what is now Texas"],["66 million years ago","Quetzalcoatlus dies out"],["1971","Douglas Lawson finds its bones in Big Bend"],["1975","Quetzalcoatlus is named"],["2021","A study explains how it took off"]],
s2:[["Azhdarchids","Quetzalcoatlus belonged to the azhdarchids, a family of giant, long-necked pterosaurs. Hatzegopteryx, found in Romania, may have been just as large, with a wider, more powerful skull."],["Giants of the sky","For comparison, the largest flying bird alive today, the wandering albatross, has a wingspan of about 3.5 meters, a third of Quetzalcoatlus’s."]],
sp:["As tall as a giraffe","With its neck raised, Quetzalcoatlus could have looked a giraffe in the eye, and it could still fly."],
g:[["Azhdarchid","A family of giant, long-necked pterosaurs"],["Wingspan","The distance from one wing tip to the other"],["Quetzalcoatl","A feathered serpent god of Mesoamerica"]],
f:["Quetzalcoatlus is named after an Aztec god.","It could stand as tall as a giraffe.","Its wingspan was three times that of the largest living bird."],
fun:"Quetzalcoatlus was named partly after Jack Northrop, a designer of flying-wing airplanes."});

ADDART({id:"mosasaurus",t:"Mosasaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐉",w:"Mosasaurus",r:["marinereptiles","pteranodon","shark","megalodon","paleontology","ichthyosaurs","pliosaurus"],
p:[["Lizard of the Meuse","Mosasaurus was named after the Meuse River in the Netherlands."],["A sea monster","It was one of the largest predators in the Late Cretaceous seas."],["A giant lizard","It was related to monitor lizards and snakes."]],
l:"Mosasaurus was a giant marine reptile that lived about 82 to 66 million years ago, during the Late Cretaceous Period, in seas around the world. It was a mosasaur, a group of sea-going lizards related to today’s monitor lizards and snakes. The largest species, Mosasaurus hoffmannii, reached about 12 meters long or more and was one of the top predators of its time. It had a long, powerful body, four paddle-like flippers, a shark-like tail fin and strong jaws with large, cone-shaped teeth. It ate fish, squid-like animals, turtles and even other mosasaurs.",
s:[["An early discovery","Around 1764, workers in a limestone quarry near Maastricht, in the Netherlands, found the first known Mosasaurus skull. A second skull, found in the 1770s, was so famous that French soldiers took it to Paris when they captured Maastricht in 1795. Studies of it helped Georges Cuvier show that animals could become extinct."],["Naming","The English geologist William Conybeare named Mosasaurus in 1822, meaning ‘lizard of the Meuse’, after the river near where it was found. The species Mosasaurus hoffmannii was named in 1829 by Gideon Mantell."],["Built to hunt","Mosasaurus had double-hinged jaws that could open wide, and an extra row of teeth on the roof of its mouth to grip prey. Its tail ended in a crescent-shaped fin, like a shark’s, making it a powerful swimmer. Fossil ammonite shells with mosasaur bite marks show what some ate."],["Live birth","Fossils of mosasaurs with unborn young inside show that they gave birth to live babies at sea, rather than laying eggs on land like most reptiles."]],
k:[["Lived","About 82–66 million years ago"],["Where","Seas worldwide"],["Length","About 12 m or more"],["Weight","Several metric tons"],["Diet","Fish, turtles, ammonites, other reptiles"],["Named","1822, by William Conybeare"]],
d:[["c.82 million years ago","Mosasaurus appears"],["66 million years ago","Mosasaurus dies out"],["c.1764","The first skull is found near Maastricht"],["1795","French troops take a famous skull to Paris"],["1822","Mosasaurus is named"],["1829","Mosasaurus hoffmannii is named"]],
s2:[["Mosasaurs around the world","Mosasaurs lived in seas on every continent, including Antarctica. Fossils are especially common in Kansas, the Netherlands, Belgium, Morocco and Jordan."],["Not a dinosaur","Although it lived at the same time, Mosasaurus was a lizard, not a dinosaur. Its closest living relatives are probably monitor lizards, such as the Komodo dragon, and snakes."]],
sp:["A turning point in science","The Maastricht skull was one of the first fossils to convince scientists that species could vanish forever, an idea that changed how people understood the history of life."],
g:[["Mosasaur","A giant extinct sea-going lizard"],["Ammonite","An extinct sea animal with a coiled shell"],["Flipper","A flat limb used for swimming"]],
f:["Mosasaurus had an extra row of teeth on the roof of its mouth.","French soldiers took a famous Mosasaurus skull to Paris in 1795.","Mosasaurs gave birth to live young."],
fun:"In 1795, French soldiers seized a Mosasaurus skull as a prize of war."});

ADDART({id:"trilobites",t:"Trilobites",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪲",w:"Trilobite",r:["cambrian","fossils","massext","geotime","insects","eurypterids","arthropleura"],
p:[["Three lobes","Trilobites are named for their bodies, divided into three lobes from side to side."],["Ancient sea creatures","They lived for about 270 million years."],["Crystal eyes","Their eyes were made of the mineral calcite."]],
l:"Trilobites were sea animals with hard outer skeletons, jointed legs and segmented bodies. They were arthropods, distant relatives of insects, crabs and spiders. Trilobites first appeared about 521 million years ago, during the Cambrian Period, and lived for nearly 270 million years before dying out in the End-Permian extinction, 251.9 million years ago. More than 20,000 species have been named, ranging from tiny ones a few millimeters long to giants more than 70 centimeters long. Because they were so common and changed quickly, their fossils are used to date and match rock layers around the world.",
s:[["Body plan","A trilobite’s body had three parts from front to back: a head shield, a segmented thorax and a tail shield. It was also divided lengthwise into three lobes, a central one and two on the sides, which gives the group its name. Under the hard shell were pairs of jointed legs, each with a branch for walking and one with gills."],["Eyes of stone","Trilobites had some of the earliest complex eyes. The lenses were made of calcite, a clear mineral, and some species had hundreds of lenses in each eye. Many fossils keep their eyes, so scientists can study how ancient animals saw."],["Lifestyles","Some trilobites crawled on the seafloor, some burrowed in mud, and some swam in open water. Many could roll into a ball, like a pill bug, to protect themselves. Some had long spines, which may have helped defend against predators."],["Growing","Like crabs and insects, trilobites grew by shedding their hard outer skeletons, a process called molting. Many trilobite fossils are actually shed skins, not dead animals."]],
k:[["Lived","About 521–252 million years ago"],["Group","Arthropods"],["Species","More than 20,000"],["Size","A few millimeters to over 70 cm"],["Eyes","Lenses made of calcite"],["Extinct","End-Permian extinction"]],
d:[["c.521 million years ago","The first trilobites appear"],["c.445 million years ago","Many trilobites die in the End-Ordovician extinction"],["c.372 million years ago","Trilobites are hit hard in the Late Devonian"],["251.9 million years ago","The last trilobites die out"],["1771","The name ‘trilobite’ is first used"]],
s2:[["Famous trilobites","Isotelus rex, from Canada, is one of the largest known, at about 72 centimeters long. Walliserops, from Morocco, had a long, three-pronged fork on its head. Elrathia kingii, from Utah, is one of the most common fossils sold to collectors."],["State fossils","Trilobites are the state fossils of Ohio, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin, where their fossils are common in ancient sea rocks."]],
sp:["Fossil favorites","Because trilobites are common, hard-shelled and often beautifully preserved, they are among the most popular fossils with collectors. Millions have been found."],
g:[["Arthropod","An animal with jointed legs and a hard outer skeleton"],["Calcite","A clear mineral made of calcium carbonate"],["Molting","Shedding an outer skeleton in order to grow"]],
f:["Trilobites lived for about 270 million years.","Trilobite eyes had lenses made of crystal.","Many trilobite fossils are shed skins."],
fun:"Trilobites had eyes with lenses made of crystal."});

ADDART({id:"dimetrodon",t:"Dimetrodon",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"⛵",w:"Dimetrodon",r:["geotime","massext","evo","fossils","dino","meganeura"],
p:[["Not a dinosaur","Dimetrodon is often mistaken for a dinosaur, but it lived long before them."],["Sail-backed","It had a tall sail on its back."],["A distant cousin","It was more closely related to mammals than to dinosaurs."]],
l:"Dimetrodon was a sail-backed predator that lived about 295 to 272 million years ago, during the Early Permian Period, more than 40 million years before the first dinosaurs. Its fossils come mostly from Texas and Oklahoma in the United States, with others found in Germany. It grew to about 1.7 to 4.6 meters long, depending on the species. Dimetrodon was a synapsid, part of the group that later gave rise to mammals, so it was more closely related to humans than to dinosaurs or lizards. It was one of the top predators of its time.",
s:[["Discovery","The American paleontologist Edward Drinker Cope named Dimetrodon in 1878 from fossils found in Texas. The name means ‘two measures of teeth’, because it had teeth of two different shapes: sharp, stabbing teeth at the front and serrated, slicing teeth at the sides."],["The sail","Dimetrodon’s sail was made of long spines growing from its backbone, probably covered with skin. Scientists once thought it helped the animal warm up in the sun and cool down in the shade. Many now think it was mainly used for display, to recognize others and attract mates."],["A mammal relative","Synapsids are named for a single opening behind each eye in the skull, which mammals also have. Dimetrodon’s different kinds of teeth were an early step toward the specialized teeth of mammals, such as incisors and canines."],["Its world","In the Early Permian, much of the land was joined into the supercontinent Pangaea. Dimetrodon lived in swampy floodplains alongside amphibians such as Eryops and the strange boomerang-headed Diplocaulus, as well as sharks in rivers and ponds."]],
k:[["Lived","About 295–272 million years ago"],["Where","Texas, Oklahoma and Germany"],["Length","About 1.7–4.6 m"],["Group","Synapsid (related to mammals)"],["Diet","Meat"],["Named","1878, by Edward Drinker Cope"]],
d:[["c.295 million years ago","Dimetrodon appears"],["c.272 million years ago","Dimetrodon dies out"],["c.233 million years ago","The first dinosaurs appear"],["1878","Edward Drinker Cope names Dimetrodon"]],
s2:[["Why it isn’t a dinosaur","Dinosaurs have a particular hip structure and legs held directly under the body. Dimetrodon walked with its legs sprawled out to the sides, like a lizard, and belonged to a completely different branch of the family tree of animals with backbones."],["Toys and films","Dimetrodon is often included in sets of toy dinosaurs, which has helped make it famous, even though it is not one."]],
sp:["Closer to you than to T. rex","Dimetrodon may look like a reptile, but it was on the branch of the family tree that eventually led to mammals, including humans."],
g:[["Synapsid","A group of animals including mammals and their extinct relatives"],["Permian","The period before the Triassic, about 299–252 million years ago"],["Pangaea","A supercontinent that contained most of Earth’s land"]],
f:["Dimetrodon lived more than 40 million years before the first dinosaurs.","It was more closely related to mammals than to dinosaurs.","Its name means ‘two measures of teeth’."],
fun:"Dimetrodon is more closely related to you than to any dinosaur."});

ADDART({id:"megalodon",t:"Megalodon",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦈",w:"Otodus megalodon",r:["shark","whale","mosasaurus","wshark","extinction","livyatan","helicoprion"],
p:[["Big tooth","Megalodon means ‘big tooth’."],["The largest shark","It was the largest shark, and one of the largest fish, that ever lived."],["Known from teeth","Most of what we know comes from teeth and backbones."]],
l:"Megalodon (Otodus megalodon) was a giant shark that lived in oceans around the world from about 23 to 3.6 million years ago. It was the largest shark known, and one of the largest predatory fish ever. Because shark skeletons are made of cartilage, which rarely fossilizes, Megalodon is known mostly from its huge teeth, some about 18 centimeters long, and from a few sets of fossil backbones. A 2025 study estimated that the largest individuals may have reached about 24 meters long and weighed about 94 metric tons. Megalodon hunted whales, seals and other large marine animals.",
s:[["Teeth","Megalodon’s triangular, serrated teeth have been found on every continent except Antarctica. Like other sharks, it constantly replaced its teeth, so each shark shed thousands in its lifetime. In the 17th century, people in Europe believed these fossil teeth were ‘tongue stones’ from snakes, until the Danish scientist Nicolas Steno showed in 1667 that they were shark teeth."],["Body shape","Megalodon was long pictured as a giant great white shark. A study published in 2025, based on backbones and comparisons with many living sharks, suggested that it had a longer, more slender body, more like a lemon shark, which may have made it a more efficient swimmer. Babies may have been about 4 meters long at birth."],["Hunting","Fossil whale bones with deep Megalodon bite marks show that it fed on whales, often attacking their fins and ribs. Its bite was among the most powerful of any animal known."],["Extinction","Megalodon died out about 3.6 million years ago. Scientists think several causes worked together: the oceans cooled, its prey moved or changed, and it may have faced competition from great white sharks and whale-hunting toothed whales."]],
k:[["Lived","About 23–3.6 million years ago"],["Where","Oceans worldwide"],["Length","Up to about 24 m (estimated)"],["Weight","Up to about 94 metric tons (estimated)"],["Tooth size","Up to about 18 cm"],["Diet","Whales, seals and other large animals"]],
d:[["c.23 million years ago","Megalodon appears"],["c.3.6 million years ago","Megalodon dies out"],["1667","Nicolas Steno shows that ‘tongue stones’ are shark teeth"],["1835","Louis Agassiz names the species"],["2025","A study suggests a long, slender body"]],
s2:[["Is it still alive?","Some films and television shows have suggested that Megalodon might survive in the deep sea. Scientists agree that it is extinct: no fresh teeth have ever been found, and a shark that large could not hide in the ocean."],["Bigger than a bus","Even the most cautious estimates show that adult Megalodon were longer than a school bus, and several times longer than the largest great white sharks."]],
sp:["Not related to great whites","Megalodon was once grouped with the great white shark, but it is now placed in a separate, extinct family, the otodontids."],
g:[["Cartilage","Flexible tissue that makes up shark skeletons"],["Serrated","Having a saw-like edge"],["Otodontid","An extinct family of large sharks"]],
f:["The largest Megalodon teeth are about the length of a human hand.","Fossil Megalodon teeth were once thought to be stone snake tongues.","Megalodon was not a close relative of the great white shark."],
fun:"Megalodon’s teeth were once thought to be stone tongues of snakes."});

ADDART({id:"smilodon",t:"Smilodon",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐅",w:"Smilodon",r:["iceagemammals","mammoth","humanorigins","iceage","extinction","terrorbirds"],
p:[["Saber-toothed cat","Smilodon is the most famous saber-toothed cat."],["Giant fangs","Its upper canine teeth were up to about 28 centimeters long."],["Ice Age hunter","It lived in the Americas until about 10,000 years ago."]],
l:"Smilodon was a large saber-toothed cat that lived in North and South America from about 2.5 million to around 10,000 years ago, during the Pleistocene Epoch, or Ice Age. It had long, curved upper canine teeth, up to about 28 centimeters including the root, and a powerful, muscular body. The largest species, Smilodon populator of South America, weighed up to about 400 kilograms, heavier than any living cat. Although often called a ‘saber-toothed tiger’, it was not closely related to tigers or other living cats. It hunted large animals such as bison, horses and young mammoths and ground sloths.",
s:[["Discovery","The Danish naturalist Peter Wilhelm Lund named Smilodon in 1842 from fossils found in caves in Brazil. The name means ‘knife tooth’. Today three species are generally recognized: Smilodon gracilis, Smilodon fatalis and Smilodon populator."],["Hunting","Smilodon had very strong front legs and could probably pin down large prey. It could open its jaws to about 110 to 120 degrees, almost twice as wide as a lion. Scientists think it used its sabers to deliver a deep, precise bite to the throat, since the long teeth could break if they hit bone."],["The La Brea Tar Pits","More than 2,000 Smilodon fatalis have been found in the La Brea Tar Pits in Los Angeles, where animals were trapped in sticky asphalt. Healed injuries in these fossils suggest that injured cats survived for a long time, perhaps because others in a group shared food with them."],["Extinction","Smilodon disappeared around 10,000 years ago, along with many other large Ice Age mammals in the Americas. Climate change at the end of the Ice Age and the decline of its large prey, perhaps hastened by human hunters, were probably the main causes."]],
k:[["Lived","About 2.5 million–10,000 years ago"],["Where","North and South America"],["Weight","Up to about 400 kg"],["Saber length","Up to about 28 cm"],["Diet","Large mammals"],["Named","1842, by Peter Wilhelm Lund"]],
d:[["c.2.5 million years ago","Smilodon appears"],["c.10,000 years ago","Smilodon dies out"],["1842","Peter Wilhelm Lund names Smilodon"],["1913","Excavations begin at the La Brea Tar Pits"],["1974","Smilodon becomes California’s state fossil"]],
s2:[["Other saber-tooths","Saber-toothed teeth evolved several times in different groups of animals. Thylacosmilus, a South American marsupial, had sabers too, and the scimitar-toothed cat Homotherium lived across Europe, Asia and the Americas."],["Smilodon and humans","Smilodon was still alive when the first people reached the Americas, so early Americans may have seen these cats."]],
sp:["Not a tiger","‘Saber-toothed tiger’ is a popular name, but Smilodon belonged to a separate branch of the cat family, the machairodonts, which has no living members."],
g:[["Canine","A long, pointed tooth near the front of the mouth"],["Pleistocene","The epoch of the most recent ice ages"],["Machairodont","A group of extinct saber-toothed cats"]],
f:["Smilodon could open its jaws about twice as wide as a lion.","More than 2,000 Smilodon have been found at the La Brea Tar Pits.","Smilodon is the state fossil of California."],
fun:"Smilodon could open its mouth about twice as wide as a lion."});

ADDART({id:"dunkleosteus",t:"Dunkleosteus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐟",w:"Dunkleosteus",r:["fish","massext","shark","geotime","fossils","tiktaalik","eurypterids","helicoprion"],
p:[["Armored fish","Dunkleosteus was a huge fish with bony armor on its head."],["Blades for teeth","Instead of teeth, it had sharp, self-sharpening bony plates."],["Devonian predator","It lived about 382 to 358 million years ago."]],
l:"Dunkleosteus was a large, armored fish that lived about 382 to 358 million years ago, during the Late Devonian Period. It belonged to the placoderms, an extinct group of fish whose heads and chests were covered in thick bony plates. Instead of teeth, Dunkleosteus had sharp bony blades in its jaws that sharpened against each other as it bit. It was one of the top predators of its time. Its fossils come mainly from Ohio in the United States and from Morocco. A 2023 study estimated that the largest individuals were about 4 meters long, shorter but stockier than once thought.",
s:[["Discovery","Fossils were first described in 1873 by the American geologist John Strong Newberry, who named them Dinichthys terrelli. In 1956, the fish was given its own name, Dunkleosteus, after David Dunkle of the Cleveland Museum of Natural History. Many fine specimens come from the Cleveland Shale, near Lake Erie."],["How big?","Because only the bony armor of the head and chest fossilizes, the rest of its body must be estimated. Earlier estimates put its length at up to 6 to 10 meters. A 2023 study compared its head with those of living fish and suggested a length of about 4 meters and a weight of about 1 metric ton, with a stocky, tuna-like body."],["A fast, strong bite","Dunkleosteus could open its jaws very quickly, sucking prey into its mouth. Computer models suggest its bite was very strong, able to cut through the armor of other fish. Fossils show that it also bit other Dunkleosteus."],["The age of fishes","The Devonian Period is often called the ‘age of fishes’, because fish spread through seas and rivers in great variety. During this time, some fish began to evolve limbs and move onto land, becoming the ancestors of amphibians, reptiles and mammals."]],
k:[["Lived","About 382–358 million years ago"],["Where","Ohio (United States), Morocco and elsewhere"],["Length","About 4 m (2023 estimate)"],["Group","Placoderm (armored fish)"],["Diet","Fish and other sea animals"],["Named","1956, after David Dunkle"]],
d:[["c.382 million years ago","Dunkleosteus appears"],["c.372 million years ago","The Late Devonian extinction begins"],["c.358 million years ago","Dunkleosteus dies out"],["1873","The first fossils are described"],["1956","Dunkleosteus receives its name"],["2023","A new study revises its size"]],
s2:[["Placoderms","Placoderms were among the first fish with jaws. Some were tiny, while Dunkleosteus was one of the largest. All died out at the end of the Devonian, about 359 million years ago."],["Ancient mothers","Fossils of a related placoderm, Materpiscis, from Australia, include an unborn young connected by an umbilical cord, the oldest known example of live birth in animals with backbones."]],
sp:["Self-sharpening jaws","Dunkleosteus’s jaw blades rubbed against each other with every bite, keeping them sharp, just as a pair of scissors can sharpen itself."],
g:[["Placoderm","An extinct group of armored fish"],["Devonian","The period about 419–359 million years ago"],["Shale","A soft rock formed from mud"]],
f:["Dunkleosteus had bony blades instead of teeth.","Its jaw blades sharpened themselves.","The Devonian is called the ‘age of fishes’."],
fun:"Dunkleosteus had no teeth, just bony blades that sharpened themselves."});

;
