ADDART({id:"ediacaran",t:"The Ediacaran Biota",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪸",w:"Ediacaran biota",r:["cambrian","geotime","fossils","trilobites","evo","snowballearth"],
p:[["Before the Cambrian","The Ediacaran biota were strange soft-bodied creatures that lived before the Cambrian Explosion."],["Fronds, discs and quilts","Many looked like leaves, cushions or pancakes on the seafloor."],["Early animals?","Some were among the first large animals on Earth."]],
l:"The Ediacaran biota are a group of mysterious soft-bodied organisms that lived on the seafloor about 575 to 539 million years ago, at the end of the Precambrian, just before the Cambrian Explosion. They are named after the Ediacara Hills of South Australia, where many were found. Most had no shells, legs or mouths that we can see; instead they looked like fronds, discs, tubes and quilted mats. Scientists have long debated what they were. Some, such as Dickinsonia, are now thought to be early animals, while others may belong to groups with no living descendants.",
s:[["Discovery","Fossils of the Ediacaran biota were first found in Namibia in the early 1900s, but their age was not understood. In 1946, the Australian geologist Reg Sprigg found many in the Ediacara Hills and suggested they were ancient jellyfish. In 1957, a schoolboy named Roger Mason found a frond-like fossil, later named Charnia, in Charnwood Forest, England, in rocks known to be Precambrian."],["Strange body plans","Dickinsonia was an oval, ribbed creature up to about 1.4 meters across. Charnia looked like a feather stuck in the seafloor. Spriggina had a head-like end. Many Ediacaran organisms seem to have grown by repeating units in a quilted pattern, a body plan unlike any animal alive today."],["Animals or not?","For decades, scientists argued about whether the Ediacarans were animals, giant single cells, lichens or something else. In 2018, researchers found traces of cholesterol-like molecules preserved in Dickinsonia fossils from Russia, a chemical sign of animals."],["The Ediacaran Period","In 2004, geologists officially named the Ediacaran Period, from about 635 to 538.8 million years ago, the first new period added to the geologic time scale in more than a century. Mistaken Point in Newfoundland, Canada, is one of the best sites for Ediacaran fossils and a UNESCO World Heritage Site."]],
k:[["Lived","About 575–539 million years ago"],["Named after","Ediacara Hills, South Australia"],["Famous fossils","Dickinsonia, Charnia, Spriggina"],["Best sites","Ediacara Hills, Mistaken Point, the White Sea"],["Period named","2004"]],
d:[["c.575 million years ago","The first large Ediacaran organisms appear"],["c.539 million years ago","Most Ediacaran forms disappear as the Cambrian begins"],["1946","Reg Sprigg finds fossils in the Ediacara Hills"],["1957","A schoolboy discovers Charnia in England"],["2004","The Ediacaran Period is officially named"],["2018","Chemical traces suggest Dickinsonia was an animal"]],
s2:[["Why they vanished","Most Ediacaran organisms disappeared around the start of the Cambrian. Scientists think the appearance of burrowing and predatory animals, which disturbed the seafloor mats and ate soft creatures, may have played a part."],["Life before Ediacara","Before the Ediacaran biota, life was mostly microscopic for about three billion years. Stromatolites, layered mounds built by microbes, are among the oldest signs of life."]],
sp:["A schoolboy’s discovery","Roger Mason was 15 when he spotted the Charnia fossil while rock climbing in 1957. His find helped prove that complex life existed before the Cambrian."],
g:[["Ediacaran","The period before the Cambrian, about 635–539 million years ago"],["Biota","All the living things of a place or time"],["Stromatolite","A layered rock built by microbes"]],
f:["Dickinsonia could grow to about 1.4 meters across.","The Ediacaran was the first new geologic period added in over a century.","A 15-year-old found one of the most famous Ediacaran fossils."],
fun:"The fossil Charnia was found in 1957 by 15-year-old Roger Mason while he was rock climbing."});

ADDART({id:"tiktaalik",t:"Tiktaalik",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐟",w:"Tiktaalik",r:["evo","dunkleosteus","fish","frog","geotime","coelacanth"],
p:[["A fish with a neck","Tiktaalik was a fish that had features of land animals."],["Fins like legs","Its strong fins had bones like the arms of four-legged animals."],["A famous transitional fossil","It helps show how animals moved from water to land."]],
l:"Tiktaalik roseae was a large, flat-headed fish that lived about 375 million years ago, during the Late Devonian Period. Its fossils were discovered in 2004 on Ellesmere Island in the Canadian Arctic and described in 2006. Tiktaalik had scales, gills and fins like a fish, but also a flat, crocodile-like head, a movable neck, sturdy ribs and fins with bones similar to the upper arm, forearm and wrist of four-legged animals. It probably lived in shallow streams and could prop itself up on its fins. It is one of the best-known transitional fossils, showing how fish evolved into tetrapods, the group that includes amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals.",
s:[["A planned discovery","Scientists Neil Shubin, Edward Daeschler and Farish Jenkins searched for rocks of the right age and type, from about 375 million years ago, formed in ancient streams. They chose Ellesmere Island in Nunavut, Canada, and after several summers of searching, found the first Tiktaalik fossils in 2004."],["Part fish, part tetrapod","Tiktaalik had a fish’s scales and fin rays, but no bony plates covering its gills, which allowed it to move its head like a neck. Its front fins had a shoulder, elbow and wrist that could bend, so it could push itself up in shallow water. Its eyes sat on top of its head, like a crocodile’s."],["Breathing","Tiktaalik had gills, but openings called spiracles on top of its head suggest it could also gulp air, perhaps using simple lungs. Breathing air would have helped in warm, shallow water with little oxygen."],["The name","The name Tiktaalik was suggested by Inuit elders of Nunavut and means ‘large freshwater fish’ in Inuktitut. The species name honors a geologist who helped organize Arctic expeditions."]],
k:[["Lived","About 375 million years ago"],["Found","Ellesmere Island, Nunavut, Canada (2004)"],["Described","2006"],["Length","Up to about 2.7 m"],["Name meaning","‘Large freshwater fish’ (Inuktitut)"]],
d:[["c.375 million years ago","Tiktaalik lives in shallow streams"],["c.365 million years ago","Early tetrapods such as Acanthostega live in swamps"],["2004","The first Tiktaalik fossils are found"],["2006","Tiktaalik is described in the journal Nature"],["2014","Studies show its hips and hind fins were also unusually strong"]],
s2:[["Your inner fish","Neil Shubin wrote a popular book, Your Inner Fish, explaining how parts of the human body, such as arm bones and the structure of the ear, can be traced back to fish like Tiktaalik."],["Not the first to walk","Fossil footprints from Poland, about 395 million years old, suggest that some four-limbed animals walked even earlier than Tiktaalik lived. Tiktaalik is best seen as a close cousin of the animals that made the move to land, not a direct ancestor."]],
sp:["Searching in the right place","Tiktaalik’s discovery is a famous example of scientific prediction: the team used what they knew about evolution and rock ages to choose where to dig."],
g:[["Tetrapod","An animal with four limbs, or descended from one"],["Transitional fossil","A fossil with features of an older and a newer group"],["Spiracle","A breathing opening on the head"]],
f:["Tiktaalik could move its head like a neck, unlike most fish.","Its name comes from the Inuktitut language.","Its front fins had bones like an arm and wrist."],
fun:"Tiktaalik’s name was suggested by Inuit elders and means ‘large freshwater fish’ in Inuktitut."});

ADDART({id:"anomalocaris",t:"Anomalocaris",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦐",w:"Anomalocaris",r:["cambrian","trilobites","ediacaran","fossils","shrimp","eurypterids"],
p:[["The strange shrimp","Anomalocaris means ‘unusual shrimp’."],["A Cambrian predator","It was one of the largest animals of its time."],["A puzzle of parts","Its body parts were first mistaken for three different animals."]],
l:"Anomalocaris was a swimming predator that lived in the seas of the Cambrian Period, about 508 million years ago. It belonged to the radiodonts, early relatives of arthropods. Anomalocaris canadensis grew to about 38 centimeters long, large for its time, and had a pair of spiny grasping appendages in front of its head, a round mouth, large stalked eyes and a body lined with flaps that it rippled to swim. Its fossils are best known from the Burgess Shale in British Columbia, Canada. For decades, its parts were mistaken for separate animals.",
s:[["A case of mistaken identity","In 1892, a grasping appendage was described as the body of a shrimp-like animal and named Anomalocaris. Its round mouth was later described as a jellyfish, and part of its body as a sea cucumber. In the 1980s, the paleontologists Harry Whittington and Derek Briggs realized that all these pieces belonged to one animal."],["Hunting","Anomalocaris used its spiny front appendages to grab prey and bring it to its mouth. Scientists once thought it crunched hard trilobites, but studies of its mouthparts and appendages suggest it may have fed on softer, swimming prey."],["Swimming","Its body had overlapping flaps along each side. By rippling these flaps in waves, like a cuttlefish or a ray, Anomalocaris could swim smoothly through the water. Its tail fan probably helped it steer."],["Sharp eyes","Fossil eyes of a related animal from Australia show that radiodonts had compound eyes with thousands of lenses, among the best eyes of any Cambrian animal and well suited to spotting prey."]],
k:[["Lived","About 508 million years ago (Cambrian)"],["Length","Up to about 38 cm"],["Group","Radiodonts (early arthropod relatives)"],["Famous site","Burgess Shale, Canada"],["Name meaning","Unusual shrimp"]],
d:[["c.508 million years ago","Anomalocaris swims in Cambrian seas"],["1892","A front appendage is named Anomalocaris"],["1909","Charles Walcott begins collecting at the Burgess Shale"],["1985","Whittington and Briggs reconstruct the whole animal"],["2011","Fossil radiodont eyes from Australia reveal thousands of lenses"]],
s2:[["Giant relatives","Some radiodonts were even bigger. Aegirocassis, from Morocco, about 480 million years old, grew to about 2 meters long and filtered food from the water like a whale."],["A star of the Cambrian","Because of its size and strange looks, Anomalocaris has become a symbol of the Cambrian Explosion and appears in many museum displays and documentaries."]],
sp:["Three animals in one","The fossil pieces of Anomalocaris were once thought to be a shrimp, a jellyfish and a sea cucumber, until scientists realized they all came from the same animal."],
g:[["Radiodont","A group of early arthropod relatives with grasping front limbs"],["Compound eye","An eye made of many small lenses"],["Appendage","A limb or limb-like body part"]],
f:["Anomalocaris’s parts were once thought to be three different animals.","It swam by rippling flaps along its sides.","Its relatives had eyes with thousands of lenses."],
fun:"The mouth of Anomalocaris was once described as a fossil jellyfish."});

ADDART({id:"meganeura",t:"Meganeura",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪰",w:"Meganeura",r:["insect","geotime","dimetrodon","fossils","pterosaurs","arthropleura"],
p:[["A giant dragonfly","Meganeura was one of the largest flying insects that ever lived."],["Wings as wide as a hawk’s","Its wingspan reached about 70 centimeters."],["A world of giant bugs","It lived in swampy forests more than 300 million years ago."]],
l:"Meganeura was a giant dragonfly-like insect that lived about 300 million years ago, during the Late Carboniferous Period. With a wingspan of about 70 centimeters, it was one of the largest flying insects known. It was not a true dragonfly but belonged to an extinct group called griffenflies, or Meganisoptera. Meganeura hunted other insects and perhaps small amphibians in the steamy coal-swamp forests of what is now France. Scientists have long debated why insects could grow so large then, and many link it to higher levels of oxygen in the air.",
s:[["Discovery","Meganeura fossils were found in coal mines near Commentry in central France in the 1880s. The French paleontologist Charles Brongniart named it in 1885. Its name means ‘large-nerved’, after the network of veins in its wings."],["The coal forests","During the Carboniferous, much of the land was covered by swampy forests of giant club mosses, horsetails and tree ferns. When these plants died and were buried, they eventually turned into coal. The forests were home to giant millipedes, cockroach relatives and early reptiles."],["Why so big?","Insects breathe through tiny tubes called tracheae, which work less well in large bodies. In the Carboniferous, oxygen may have made up about 30 percent or more of the air, compared with 21 percent today, which may have allowed larger insects. Other scientists think the lack of flying predators before birds and pterosaurs was also important."],["A fierce hunter","Like modern dragonflies, Meganeura probably caught prey in flight using its spiny legs, and had large eyes and strong jaws. A relative, Meganeuropsis from the United States, may have been slightly larger."]],
k:[["Lived","About 300 million years ago (Late Carboniferous)"],["Wingspan","About 70 cm"],["Group","Griffenflies (Meganisoptera)"],["Found","Commentry, France"],["Named","1885, by Charles Brongniart"]],
d:[["c.300 million years ago","Meganeura flies over coal swamps"],["1880","Fossils are found in coal mines at Commentry, France"],["1885","Charles Brongniart names Meganeura"],["1939","The larger relative Meganeuropsis is described from Kansas"]],
s2:[["Other giants","The Carboniferous also had Arthropleura, a millipede relative that grew to about 2.6 meters long, the largest land invertebrate known."],["Today’s biggest","The largest living dragonflies have wingspans of up to about 19 centimeters, less than a third of Meganeura’s."]],
sp:["Insects before birds","Insects were the first animals to fly, more than 100 million years before pterosaurs, birds or bats took to the air."],
g:[["Griffenfly","An extinct group of giant dragonfly-like insects"],["Carboniferous","The period about 359–299 million years ago"],["Trachea","An air tube that insects use to breathe"]],
f:["Meganeura’s wingspan was about 70 centimeters.","Insects were the first animals to fly.","The air in the Carboniferous may have had more oxygen than today."],
fun:"A millipede relative from the same age, Arthropleura, grew longer than a person is tall."});

ADDART({id:"brontosaurus",t:"Brontosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦕",w:"Brontosaurus",r:["diplodocus","brachiosaurus","paleontology","stegosaurus","allosaurus"],
p:[["The thunder lizard","Brontosaurus means ‘thunder lizard’."],["Gone and back again","Scientists dropped the name in 1903 and brought it back in 2015."],["A long-necked giant","It was a sauropod about 22 meters long."]],
l:"Brontosaurus, meaning ‘thunder lizard’, was a giant long-necked, plant-eating sauropod dinosaur that lived in western North America about 156 to 146 million years ago, during the Late Jurassic Period. It grew to about 22 meters long and weighed around 15 metric tons or more. Brontosaurus is famous not only for its size but for its name’s strange history: for most of the 20th century, scientists considered it the same animal as Apatosaurus, but a large study in 2015 concluded that it was different enough to be its own genus again.",
s:[["The Bone Wars","Othniel Charles Marsh named Apatosaurus in 1877 and Brontosaurus in 1879, during his rivalry with Edward Drinker Cope. Marsh’s mounted Brontosaurus skeleton at Yale became world famous, even though it was given the wrong head, based on a Camarasaurus skull."],["Losing its name","In 1903, the paleontologist Elmer Riggs concluded that Brontosaurus was simply a grown-up Apatosaurus. Because Apatosaurus was named first, scientists used that name, but ‘Brontosaurus’ stayed popular with the public, in books, films and even a 1989 US postage stamp."],["Coming back","In 2015, Emanuel Tschopp, Octávio Mateus and Roger Benson compared hundreds of features in many sauropod skeletons. They found enough differences, such as Brontosaurus’s longer, narrower neck, to treat it as a separate genus again. Not all scientists agree, but many now use the name."],["How it lived","Like other diplodocid sauropods, Brontosaurus had a small head, peg-like teeth, an enormously long neck and a whip-like tail. It probably fed on low and mid-height plants, swinging its neck to reach a wide area without moving its body."]],
k:[["Lived","About 156–146 million years ago"],["Where","Western North America"],["Length","About 22 m"],["Weight","About 15 metric tons or more"],["Named","1879, by Othniel Charles Marsh"]],
d:[["1877","Marsh names Apatosaurus"],["1879","Marsh names Brontosaurus"],["1903","Elmer Riggs merges Brontosaurus into Apatosaurus"],["1989","The US Postal Service issues a ‘Brontosaurus’ stamp, causing debate"],["2015","A study brings Brontosaurus back as its own genus"]],
s2:[["The wrong head","For decades, many museum skeletons of Apatosaurus and Brontosaurus had blunt Camarasaurus-like skulls. In 1979, the correct, long and low head was placed on the Carnegie Museum’s Apatosaurus skeleton."],["How science changes","Brontosaurus shows that scientific names can change as new evidence and methods appear. Its return was celebrated by dinosaur fans around the world."]],
sp:["A famous comeback","When Brontosaurus was revived in 2015, newspapers around the world announced that the beloved dinosaur was ‘back’."],
g:[["Sauropod","A giant long-necked, plant-eating dinosaur"],["Genus","A group of closely related species"],["Diplodocid","A family of long, slender sauropods including Diplodocus"]],
f:["Brontosaurus means ‘thunder lizard’.","The name was dropped in 1903 and revived in 2015.","Early skeletons were given the wrong kind of head."],
fun:"Brontosaurus lost its scientific name in 1903 but got it back 112 years later."});

ADDART({id:"pachycephalosaurus",t:"Pachycephalosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪖",w:"Pachycephalosaurus",r:["triceratops","trex","ankylosaurus","dino","paleontology"],
p:[["The thick-headed lizard","Pachycephalosaurus had a dome of solid bone on top of its head."],["A bony crown","Its dome was ringed with knobs and spikes."],["Head-butter?","Scientists debate whether it butted heads with rivals."]],
l:"Pachycephalosaurus, meaning ‘thick-headed lizard’, was a plant-eating dinosaur that lived in western North America at the very end of the Cretaceous Period, about 70 to 66 million years ago, alongside Tyrannosaurus rex and Triceratops. It walked on two legs and grew to about 4.5 meters long. It is famous for the thick dome of bone on top of its skull, up to about 25 centimeters thick, surrounded by bony knobs and short spikes. Pachycephalosaurus is known mostly from skulls, so much of its body is reconstructed from relatives.",
s:[["The dome","The dome was made of dense bone. In young animals it was flatter and more spiky; as they grew, it became taller and rounder. Some scientists think the dome helped individuals recognize their own kind and attract mates."],["Did it butt heads?","Pachycephalosaurus is often shown ramming rivals like bighorn sheep. Some studies found injuries on domes that fit head-butting, and computer models suggest the dome could absorb blows. Others argue the rounded dome would slip, so the animals may have butted each other’s sides instead."],["Growing up","Two other dinosaurs, Stygimoloch and Dracorex, have spiky skulls with smaller domes. In 2009, Jack Horner and Mark Goodwin suggested they were young Pachycephalosaurus at different growth stages, but this is still debated."],["Diet","Pachycephalosaurus had small, leaf-shaped teeth suited to eating plants. Some scientists have suggested it might also have eaten fruit, seeds or even small animals."]],
k:[["Lived","About 70–66 million years ago"],["Where","Western North America"],["Length","About 4.5 m"],["Dome thickness","Up to about 25 cm"],["Named","1943"]],
d:[["1931","A dome is described under another name"],["1943","Pachycephalosaurus is named"],["2009","Scientists suggest Stygimoloch and Dracorex are young Pachycephalosaurus"],["2013","A study finds injuries on domes consistent with head-butting"]],
s2:[["Bone-headed dinosaurs","Pachycephalosaurus was the largest of the pachycephalosaurs, a group of dome-headed dinosaurs found in North America and Asia."],["Fossil domes","Because domes were so solid, they survived as fossils much more often than the rest of the skeleton. Many pachycephalosaurs are known only from their heads."]],
sp:["A natural crash helmet","The dome of Pachycephalosaurus was made of dense bone up to 25 centimeters thick, thicker than a typical brick is long."],
g:[["Pachycephalosaur","A dome-headed, plant-eating dinosaur"],["Dome","The thick, rounded top of the skull"],["Growth stage","A phase in an animal’s development"]],
f:["Pachycephalosaurus means ‘thick-headed lizard’.","Its skull dome was up to about 25 centimeters thick.","It lived alongside T. rex and Triceratops."],
fun:"Most pachycephalosaurs are known only from their skulls, because their thick domes survived when the rest of the skeleton did not."});

ADDART({id:"therizinosaurus",t:"Therizinosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦥",w:"Therizinosaurus",r:["velociraptor","dinobirds","trex","dino","paleontology"],
p:[["The scythe lizard","Therizinosaurus had giant claws on its hands."],["A plant-eating theropod","Unlike its meat-eating relatives, it probably ate plants."],["Once thought a turtle","Its first fossils were mistaken for a giant turtle-like reptile."]],
l:"Therizinosaurus, meaning ‘scythe lizard’, was a large theropod dinosaur that lived in what is now Mongolia about 70 million years ago, during the Late Cretaceous Period. It is famous for its enormous hand claws: the claw bones alone were about 52 centimeters long, and in life they were covered by even longer horny sheaths. Therizinosaurus grew to about 9 to 10 meters long and may have weighed 5 metric tons or more. Although it belonged to the same broad group as Tyrannosaurus and Velociraptor, it probably ate plants, using its claws to pull down branches.",
s:[["A puzzling discovery","A Soviet–Mongolian expedition found the first remains, including huge claws, in the Gobi Desert in 1948. In 1954, the paleontologist Evgeny Maleev named it Therizinosaurus cheloniformis, meaning ‘scythe lizard shaped like a turtle’, because he thought it was a giant turtle-like reptile that used its claws to harvest seaweed."],["A strange dinosaur","Later finds of arms and legs, and of relatives, showed that Therizinosaurus was a theropod. Therizinosaurs had small heads with leaf-shaped teeth, long necks, pot bellies for digesting plants, and four-toed feet. Feathered relatives such as Beipiaosaurus from China show that the group had feathers."],["What were the claws for?","The claws were too long and narrow for digging and probably not ideal as weapons. Most scientists think Therizinosaurus used them to hook and pull leafy branches toward its mouth, and perhaps for display or defense against predators like Tarbosaurus."],["Size","Estimates based on relatives suggest a length of 9 to 10 meters and a weight of about 5 metric tons, making it the largest known therizinosaur."]],
k:[["Lived","About 70 million years ago"],["Where","Nemegt Formation, Mongolia"],["Length","About 9–10 m"],["Claw bones","About 52 cm"],["Named","1954, by Evgeny Maleev"]],
d:[["1948","A Soviet–Mongolian expedition finds giant claws in the Gobi"],["1954","Evgeny Maleev names Therizinosaurus"],["1970s","Scientists show it was a theropod dinosaur"],["1999","The feathered relative Beipiaosaurus is described from China"]],
s2:[["Neighbors","Therizinosaurus lived in a land of rivers and forests alongside the tyrannosaur Tarbosaurus, the duck-billed Saurolophus and the giant ostrich-like Deinocheirus."],["A popular star","Therizinosaurus has become a favorite in films and games, where its claws make it look fearsome, even though it was probably a peaceful plant-eater."]],
sp:["Longest claws of any land animal","The hand claws of Therizinosaurus are among the longest of any known land animal."],
g:[["Theropod","A group of mostly meat-eating, two-legged dinosaurs"],["Therizinosaur","A group of plant-eating theropods with long claws"],["Ungual","The last bone of a finger or toe, which supports a claw"]],
f:["Therizinosaurus’s claw bones were about 52 centimeters long.","It was first thought to be a giant turtle-like reptile.","It was a plant-eating relative of T. rex."],
fun:"Therizinosaurus was first thought to be a giant turtle that cut seaweed with its claws."});

ADDART({id:"dinoeggs",t:"Dinosaur Eggs and Nests",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🥚",w:"Dinosaur egg",r:["dino","dinobirds","velociraptor","argentinosaurus","paleontology","oviraptor","maiasaura","protoceratops"],
p:[["All dinosaurs laid eggs","Every dinosaur hatched from an egg."],["Caring parents","Fossils show that some dinosaurs guarded and warmed their nests."],["Babies inside","Some fossil eggs still contain the bones of unhatched babies."]],
l:"All dinosaurs reproduced by laying eggs, and fossil eggs, nests and even embryos have been found on every continent. Dinosaur eggs ranged from smaller than a tennis ball to about 60 centimeters long, but even the biggest dinosaurs laid relatively small eggs, because thicker shells would have stopped air from reaching the baby inside. Fossil nests show that some dinosaurs laid eggs in circles or pairs, some buried them in warm soil or plants, and some sat on them like birds. Discoveries of nesting grounds have changed the picture of dinosaurs from cold, uncaring reptiles to animals that often looked after their young.",
s:[["The egg thief that wasn’t","In 1923, an American expedition led by Roy Chapman Andrews found the first widely recognized dinosaur eggs in Mongolia. A skeleton found on top of a nest was named Oviraptor, meaning ‘egg thief’, because scientists thought it was stealing the eggs. In the 1990s, fossils of Oviraptor relatives sitting on nests with their arms spread over the eggs showed that they were actually parents brooding their own eggs."],["Good mother lizard","In 1978, Jack Horner and Bob Makela found a nesting colony in Montana with nests of a duck-billed dinosaur, which they named Maiasaura, ‘good mother lizard’, in 1979. The nests held babies too big to be newborns, suggesting that the young stayed in the nest while parents brought food."],["Titanosaur nurseries","In 1997, scientists found thousands of eggs of long-necked titanosaurs at Auca Mahuevo in Patagonia, Argentina. Some contained embryos with patches of skin. The giant parents probably buried their eggs in warm ground and left them, rather than sitting on them."],["Soft and hard shells","In 2020, a study found that the earliest dinosaurs may have laid soft, leathery eggs, like turtles, and that hard shells evolved later in several groups. This may explain why eggs of some dinosaurs are so rare as fossils."]],
k:[["First widely recognized eggs","Mongolia, 1923"],["Largest eggs","About 60 cm long"],["‘Good mother lizard’","Maiasaura"],["Famous nesting site","Auca Mahuevo, Argentina"],["Brooding dinosaurs","Oviraptorosaurs"]],
d:[["1923","Roy Chapman Andrews’s expedition finds dinosaur eggs in Mongolia"],["1978","Jack Horner finds the Maiasaura nesting colony in Montana"],["1993","An oviraptorid is found sitting on its nest"],["1997","Titanosaur nesting grounds are found in Argentina"],["2020","Scientists report that early dinosaurs laid soft-shelled eggs"]],
s2:[["Baby Yingliang","In 2021, scientists described ‘Baby Yingliang’, an oviraptorosaur embryo from China curled up inside its egg in a tucked position like a bird chick about to hatch."],["Birds still do it","Birds, the living dinosaurs, show many of the same nesting behaviors found in fossils: laying eggs in clutches, sitting on them and caring for chicks."]],
sp:["Ready to hatch","Some dinosaur embryos are so well preserved that scientists can see how they were curled inside the egg, just days before hatching."],
g:[["Embryo","An unborn animal developing inside an egg"],["Brooding","Sitting on eggs to keep them warm"],["Clutch","A group of eggs laid at one time"]],
f:["Oviraptor’s name means ‘egg thief’, but it was really a caring parent.","Even the largest dinosaurs laid eggs no longer than about 60 centimeters.","Some fossil eggs contain embryos curled up ready to hatch."],
fun:"The dinosaur named Oviraptor, ‘egg thief’, turned out to have been sitting on its own eggs."});

ADDART({id:"ammonites",t:"Ammonites",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐚",w:"Ammonoidea",r:["mosasaurus","massext","fossils","octopus","nautilus"],
p:[["Spiral shells","Ammonites were sea animals with coiled, chambered shells."],["Relatives of squid","They were related to octopuses, squid and the nautilus."],["Fossils everywhere","Their beautiful fossils are found all over the world."]],
l:"Ammonites, or ammonoids, were sea animals with coiled, chambered shells that lived from about 400 million years ago until the end of the Cretaceous, 66 million years ago. They were cephalopods, related to octopuses, squid, cuttlefish and the living nautilus. An ammonite lived in the last, largest chamber of its shell, with tentacles reaching out, and used the empty chambers behind it like a submarine’s ballast tanks to control its buoyancy. Ammonites ranged from smaller than a coin to about 2 meters across. Because they evolved quickly and spread widely, their fossils are used to date rocks.",
s:[["How they lived","Ammonites swam by squirting water through a tube, as squid do. The chambers of the shell were separated by walls called septa and connected by a tube, the siphuncle, which let the animal adjust the gas and liquid inside to float higher or sink. They probably ate plankton and small animals, and were eaten by fish, marine reptiles and other cephalopods."],["Telling time with ammonites","Ammonite species appeared and disappeared quickly, and their shells are common and easy to recognize. Geologists use them as index fossils: finding a particular ammonite shows that a rock layer belongs to a particular time, sometimes to within a few hundred thousand years."],["Giants and oddballs","The largest known ammonite, Parapuzosia seppenradensis from Germany, had a shell about 1.8 meters across. Some ammonites, called heteromorphs, had uncoiled shells shaped like hooks, paper clips or tangled knots."],["Extinction","Ammonites survived several mass extinctions but disappeared in the asteroid impact 66 million years ago. Their tiny young drifted as plankton, which may have been hit hard when the oceans changed. Their relatives, the nautiluses, which lay larger eggs, survived."]],
k:[["Lived","About 400–66 million years ago"],["Group","Cephalopods"],["Living relatives","Nautilus, octopus, squid, cuttlefish"],["Largest","About 1.8 m across"],["Used as","Index fossils"]],
d:[["c.400 million years ago","The first ammonoids appear"],["251.9 million years ago","Ammonites nearly die out in the End-Permian extinction"],["66 million years ago","The last ammonites die out"],["1895","A giant ammonite about 1.8 m across is found in Germany"],["2023","Another giant Parapuzosia is found in Germany"]],
s2:[["Snakestones","In medieval England, people thought ammonites were coiled snakes turned to stone. In Whitby, sellers sometimes carved snake heads onto them. The name ‘ammonite’ comes from the Egyptian god Amun, often shown with curled ram’s horns."],["Ammolite","Some ammonite shells from Canada have changed into a shimmering, rainbow-colored gemstone called ammolite."]],
sp:["Living cousin","The chambered nautilus, which lives in the western Pacific including Philippine waters, is the closest living animal to an ammonite in shell design, though the two are only distant relatives."],
g:[["Cephalopod","A group of mollusks including squid and octopuses"],["Index fossil","A fossil used to date rock layers"],["Siphuncle","A tube linking the chambers of a shell"]],
f:["Ammonites are named after the Egyptian god Amun.","The largest ammonite shells were about 1.8 meters across.","Ammonites were used to float like submarines."],
fun:"People in medieval England believed ammonites were snakes that had been turned to stone."});

ADDART({id:"elasmosaurus",t:"Elasmosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦕",w:"Elasmosaurus",r:["marinereptiles","mosasaurus","paleontology","pteranodon","ammonites","ichthyosaurs","nothosaurus","pliosaurus"],
p:[["The longest neck","Elasmosaurus had one of the longest necks of any animal."],["A sea reptile","It was a plesiosaur that swam with four flippers."],["A famous mistake","Its first reconstruction put the head on the wrong end."]],
l:"Elasmosaurus was a plesiosaur, a marine reptile, that lived about 80 million years ago, during the Late Cretaceous, in the Western Interior Seaway that then covered central North America. It grew to about 10 meters long, and more than half of that was its neck, which had about 72 vertebrae, more than almost any other animal. Elasmosaurus had a small head with long, sharp teeth for catching fish and squid-like animals, a broad body, four large flippers and a short tail. It is also famous for a mistake: the paleontologist Edward Drinker Cope first put its head on the end of its tail.",
s:[["The head on the tail","The first Elasmosaurus skeleton was found in Kansas in 1867 and sent to Edward Drinker Cope, who named it in 1868. Cope reconstructed it with a short neck and a long tail, putting the head at the wrong end. His rival Othniel Charles Marsh, and the scientist Joseph Leidy, pointed out the error. The embarrassment is often said to have helped start the bitter Bone Wars between Cope and Marsh."],["A long, stiff neck","Studies of the neck bones show that Elasmosaurus could not raise its neck high like a swan, as old pictures showed. Its neck was fairly stiff and held forward, perhaps letting it approach schools of fish from below without being noticed."],["Swimming","Plesiosaurs swam by ‘flying’ through the water with their four flippers, like sea turtles and penguins. Elasmosaurus was probably a slow cruiser rather than a fast chaser."],["Stones in the stomach","Many elasmosaurs have been found with stones, called gastroliths, in their stomachs. They may have helped grind food or acted as ballast to control buoyancy."]],
k:[["Lived","About 80 million years ago"],["Where","Western Interior Seaway (Kansas)"],["Length","About 10 m"],["Neck bones","About 72"],["Named","1868, by Edward Drinker Cope"]],
d:[["c.80 million years ago","Elasmosaurus swims in the Western Interior Seaway"],["1867","The first skeleton is found in Kansas"],["1868","Edward Drinker Cope names Elasmosaurus"],["1870","Joseph Leidy points out that Cope put the head on the tail"],["2000s","Studies show the neck could not be raised like a swan’s"]],
s2:[["Relatives","Elasmosaurus gave its name to the elasmosaurids, a family of very long-necked plesiosaurs found worldwide, including Albertonectes from Canada, which had an even longer neck with 76 vertebrae."],["The Loch Ness legend","Elasmosaurus is often linked to stories of the Loch Ness monster, but plesiosaurs died out 66 million years ago, and Loch Ness formed only after the last ice age."]],
sp:["Head on the wrong end","Cope tried to buy back and destroy copies of the journal that showed his upside-down reconstruction, but the story became one of paleontology’s most famous blunders."],
g:[["Plesiosaur","A sea reptile with four flippers"],["Vertebra","One of the bones of the backbone"],["Gastrolith","A stone swallowed by an animal"]],
f:["Elasmosaurus had about 72 neck bones.","Cope first put its head on the end of its tail.","It could not raise its neck high like a swan."],
fun:"Edward Drinker Cope put Elasmosaurus’s head on the end of its tail and later tried to buy back the journals that showed his mistake."});

ADDART({id:"livyatan",t:"Livyatan",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐋",w:"Livyatan",r:["megalodon","whale","sharks","mosasaurus","iceagemammals","basilosaurus"],
p:[["A whale that hunted whales","Livyatan was a giant predatory relative of the sperm whale."],["Biggest bite","It had the largest biting teeth of any known animal, apart from tusks."],["A rival of megalodon","It shared the seas with the giant shark megalodon."]],
l:"Livyatan melvillei was a giant predatory whale that lived about 9.9 to 8.9 million years ago, during the Miocene Epoch, in the Pacific Ocean off what is now Peru. A relative of today’s sperm whale, it is known from a huge skull with jaws and teeth found in 2008 in the desert of the Pisco Formation and described in 2010. Its largest teeth were about 36 centimeters long, the biggest biting teeth known from any animal, excluding tusks. Estimates of its length range from about 12 to 17 meters. Livyatan probably hunted other whales, as did the giant shark megalodon, which lived at the same time.",
s:[["Discovery","The skull was found in November 2008 by Klaas Post of the Natural History Museum Rotterdam, on the last day of a field trip in the Peruvian desert. The fossil was described by a team led by Olivier Lambert in the journal Nature in 2010."],["A different kind of sperm whale","Modern sperm whales have teeth only in their lower jaws and feed mostly on squid by suction. Livyatan had large teeth in both upper and lower jaws and a powerful bite, suggesting it attacked large prey, such as baleen whales, seals and dolphins."],["The name","The team first named it Leviathan melvillei, after the biblical sea monster and Herman Melville, author of Moby-Dick. Because the name Leviathan had already been used for another animal, they changed it in 2010 to Livyatan, from the Hebrew spelling."],["Sharing the seas","Livyatan and megalodon lived in the same waters at the same time and probably competed for prey. Teeth of Livyatan-like whales have also been found in Chile, Australia, South Africa and the United States."]],
k:[["Lived","About 9.9–8.9 million years ago"],["Where","Pisco Formation, Peru"],["Length","About 12–17 m (estimates vary)"],["Largest teeth","About 36 cm"],["Named after","Leviathan and Herman Melville"]],
d:[["c.9.9 million years ago","Livyatan hunts in the Pacific off Peru"],["2008","The skull is found in the Peruvian desert"],["2010","Livyatan is described and renamed"],["2025","A new study suggests a smaller length of about 12–13 m"]],
s2:[["The Pisco Formation","The Pisco desert in Peru was once a shallow sea. Its rocks are packed with fossil whales, seals, penguins, sharks and even aquatic sloths, making it one of the richest fossil sites for ocean life."],["Today’s sperm whales","Sperm whales are the largest toothed predators alive today, diving more than 1,000 meters to hunt squid. Their ancestors included giants like Livyatan."]],
sp:["Biggest bite in history","Livyatan’s 36-centimeter teeth are longer than the largest teeth of T. rex or megalodon."],
g:[["Sperm whale","The largest living toothed whale"],["Miocene","The epoch about 23–5.3 million years ago"],["Baleen whale","A whale that filters food with baleen plates"]],
f:["Livyatan had the largest biting teeth of any known animal, excluding tusks.","It was named partly after the author of Moby-Dick.","It lived at the same time as megalodon."],
fun:"Livyatan’s name honors Herman Melville, the author of the whale novel Moby-Dick."});

ADDART({id:"woollyrhino",t:"Woolly Rhinoceros",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦏",w:"Woolly rhinoceros",r:["mammoth","iceagemammals","caveart","iceage","smilodon","paraceratherium"],
p:[["A rhino for the cold","The woolly rhinoceros was covered in thick fur to survive the Ice Age."],["A giant horn","Its front horn could be more than a meter long."],["Painted by early people","Stone Age artists drew it on cave walls."]],
l:"The woolly rhinoceros was a large, shaggy rhinoceros that lived across northern Europe and Asia during the Ice Age, from more than 450,000 years ago until about 14,000 years ago. It was about 3.2 to 3.6 meters long and up to about 1.6 meters tall at the shoulder, and it weighed around 1.5 to 2 metric tons. It had thick fur, small ears, a short tail and two horns, the front one often more than a meter long. It grazed on grasses and other plants of the cold mammoth steppe. Frozen carcasses found in Siberia and paintings in caves such as Chauvet in France show what it looked like.",
s:[["Built for the cold","Woolly rhinos had long, thick fur, a layer of fat under the skin, and small ears and tail to reduce heat loss. Their wide front horn may have been used to sweep snow away from plants, and its sides show wear from this."],["Frozen in time","In 2014, hunters in Yakutia, Siberia, found a mummified baby woolly rhino, nicknamed Sasha, with its fur, horns and soft tissues preserved. Earlier, in 1929, scientists dug up remarkably preserved rhinos from an oil-soaked pit at Starunia, in what is now Ukraine."],["In cave art","Ice Age artists painted and carved woolly rhinos more than 31,000 years ago, at Chauvet Cave in France and elsewhere. Some pictures show the hump on the shoulders and the long horns, details later confirmed by frozen specimens."],["Why it disappeared","The woolly rhino’s range shrank toward northeastern Siberia after about 17,000 years ago. Genetic studies suggest that its numbers stayed stable until shortly before it vanished, pointing to climate warming at the end of the Ice Age, which changed its grassland habitat, as the main cause, possibly with some help from human hunting."]],
k:[["Lived","More than 450,000 to about 14,000 years ago"],["Where","Northern Europe and Asia"],["Length","About 3.2–3.6 m"],["Weight","About 1.5–2 metric tons"],["Front horn","Often over 1 m"]],
d:[["c.450,000 years ago","Woolly rhinos live in Europe"],["c.31,000 years ago","Woolly rhinos are painted in Chauvet Cave"],["c.14,000 years ago","The last woolly rhinos die out in Siberia"],["1929","Preserved rhinos are excavated at Starunia"],["2014","The frozen baby rhino Sasha is found in Siberia"]],
s2:[["Living relatives","The woolly rhino’s closest living relative is the Sumatran rhinoceros, the smallest and hairiest living rhino, which is critically endangered."],["Unicorn legends","Some scientists have suggested that horns of woolly rhinos and the related Elasmotherium, found in the ground, may have inspired legends of unicorns."]],
sp:["Snowplow horn","Wear marks on woolly rhino horns suggest they swept them side to side to clear snow off the plants they ate."],
g:[["Mammoth steppe","The cold grassland of the Ice Age"],["Mummified","Preserved with skin and soft tissues"],["Megafauna","Large animals"]],
f:["The woolly rhino’s front horn could be more than a meter long.","A frozen baby rhino named Sasha was found in 2014.","Its closest living relative is the Sumatran rhino."],
fun:"Woolly rhinos may have used their long horns like snowplows to clear snow off grass."});

ADDART({id:"groundsloths",t:"Giant Ground Sloths",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦥",w:"Megatherium",r:["iceagemammals","smilodon","mammoth","darwin","sloth","glyptodon"],
p:[["Sloths the size of elephants","Giant ground sloths were huge relatives of today’s tree sloths."],["Megatherium","The largest, Megatherium, was about 6 meters long."],["Gone by the end of the Ice Age","They vanished around 12,000 years ago."]],
l:"Giant ground sloths were large, plant-eating mammals that lived in the Americas, relatives of the small tree sloths alive today. Instead of hanging in trees, they walked on the ground, some on all fours and some able to rear up on their back legs and thick tails to reach leaves. The biggest, Megatherium americanum of South America, was about 6 meters long and weighed around 4 metric tons, as much as an elephant. Ground sloths had huge claws, which they used to pull down branches and defend themselves. Most ground sloths died out around 12,000 years ago, near the end of the last Ice Age.",
s:[["Megatherium","The first Megatherium skeleton was found in 1787 near the Luján River in what is now Argentina and shipped to Madrid, Spain. In 1796, the French scientist Georges Cuvier studied drawings of it and named it Megatherium, meaning ‘great beast’. He realized it was a giant relative of sloths and used it as evidence that species could become extinct."],["Darwin’s sloths","During the voyage of the Beagle in the 1830s, Charles Darwin collected fossil bones of giant ground sloths and other extinct mammals in South America. Their resemblance to living sloths and armadillos helped him think about how species change over time."],["How they lived","Ground sloths had broad hips and thick tails that supported them when they stood up. Their claws were so large that some walked on the sides of their feet or on their knuckles. Fossilized dung found in caves shows that some ate desert plants, grasses and leaves."],["Many kinds","Ground sloths came in many sizes, from about the size of a dog to Megatherium. Some, like Eremotherium, lived as far north as the United States, and others reached the Caribbean islands, where some survived until about 4,500 years ago. One group, Thalassocnus, even swam in the sea off Peru."]],
k:[["Largest","Megatherium americanum, about 6 m long"],["Weight","About 4 metric tons"],["Where","The Americas"],["Living relatives","Tree sloths, armadillos, anteaters"],["Extinct","About 12,000 years ago (mainland)"]],
d:[["c.3.6 million years ago","The earliest Megatherium fossils"],["1787","A Megatherium skeleton is found in Argentina"],["1796","Georges Cuvier names Megatherium"],["1832","Charles Darwin collects ground sloth fossils in Argentina"],["c.12,000 years ago","Megatherium and most mainland ground sloths die out"]],
s2:[["Sloths and people","Ground sloths were still alive when people arrived in the Americas. Footprints in New Mexico show people following a giant ground sloth, and the sloth rearing up to face them."],["Island survivors","Ground sloths on Caribbean islands such as Cuba and Hispaniola survived thousands of years longer than those on the mainland, disappearing only after people reached the islands."]],
sp:["A sloth that could stand","Megatherium could rise on its back legs and lean on its tail like a tripod, raising its head about 4 meters to reach treetops."],
g:[["Ground sloth","An extinct sloth that lived on the ground"],["Megatherium","The largest ground sloth, meaning ‘great beast’"],["Xenarthra","The group including sloths, armadillos and anteaters"]],
f:["Megatherium was as heavy as an elephant.","Darwin collected ground sloth fossils on the Beagle voyage.","Some ground sloths swam in the sea."],
fun:"Fossil footprints in New Mexico show ancient people following a giant ground sloth."});

ADDART({id:"direwolf",t:"The Dire Wolf",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐺",w:"Dire wolf",r:["smilodon","iceagemammals","wolf","dog","genetics"],
p:[["An Ice Age hunter","The dire wolf was a large dog-like predator of the Americas."],["Not just a big gray wolf","DNA shows it belonged to a separate, ancient lineage."],["Made famous by fiction","Fantasy stories and a 2025 gene-editing project brought it into the news."]],
l:"The dire wolf, Aenocyon dirus, was a large, dog-like predator that lived in North and South America from about 250,000 to 13,000 years ago, during the Late Pleistocene. It was a little larger and heavier than a gray wolf, weighing about 68 kilograms, with a broad head and strong teeth for crushing bone. Dire wolves hunted large animals such as horses, bison and ground sloths. Thousands of their skeletons have been found in the La Brea Tar Pits in Los Angeles. A 2021 DNA study showed that dire wolves were not close relatives of gray wolves but the last members of a lineage that split off millions of years ago.",
s:[["La Brea","More than 4,000 dire wolves have been dug from the La Brea Tar Pits, more than any other mammal there. They were probably drawn to animals trapped in the sticky asphalt and became stuck themselves. The large numbers suggest that dire wolves lived and hunted in packs."],["A separate lineage","Dire wolves looked much like large gray wolves, so they were long classified in the same genus, Canis. In 2021, scientists who studied ancient DNA found that dire wolves split from the ancestors of wolves, coyotes and jackals about 5.7 million years ago. They were placed in their own genus, Aenocyon."],["Extinction","Dire wolves disappeared around 13,000 years ago, along with many of the large animals they hunted. Gray wolves, which could eat smaller prey and adapt to new habitats, survived."],["The 2025 ‘dire wolves’","In April 2025, the company Colossal Biosciences announced three wolf pups, named Romulus, Remus and Khaleesi, made by editing 14 genes of gray wolf cells to give them dire wolf traits such as a larger size and a light coat. Many scientists, and the IUCN’s canid experts, said the animals are modified gray wolves, not true dire wolves."]],
k:[["Scientific name","Aenocyon dirus"],["Lived","About 250,000–13,000 years ago"],["Weight","About 68 kg"],["Most found at","La Brea Tar Pits (over 4,000)"],["Split from wolves","About 5.7 million years ago"]],
d:[["1858","Joseph Leidy names the dire wolf from a fossil found in Indiana"],["1913","Excavations at La Brea begin to uncover thousands of dire wolves"],["2011","Game of Thrones makes dire wolves famous on television"],["2021","DNA shows dire wolves were a separate lineage"],["2025","A company announces gene-edited wolves with dire wolf traits"]],
s2:[["In fantasy","Dire wolves appear in many fantasy stories and games. The television series Game of Thrones, based on George R. R. Martin’s novels, made the animal widely known, though its giant fictional wolves are far bigger than the real ones."],["De-extinction debate","The 2025 announcement sparked debate about whether extinct species can truly be brought back, and whether money would be better spent protecting endangered animals alive today."]],
sp:["Pack hunters","Healed injuries on many La Brea dire wolf skeletons suggest that injured wolves survived, perhaps because pack members shared food with them."],
g:[["Lineage","A line of descent from a common ancestor"],["Ancient DNA","Genetic material recovered from old remains"],["De-extinction","Efforts to revive extinct species or their traits"]],
f:["More than 4,000 dire wolves have been found at La Brea.","Dire wolves split from the ancestors of gray wolves about 5.7 million years ago.","Scientists say the 2025 ‘dire wolves’ are modified gray wolves."],
fun:"More dire wolves have been found in the La Brea Tar Pits than any other mammal."});

;
/* data/life1.js */
// Animal Lives (27 November 2026): how animals travel, rest, hide, defend themselves, talk, think and build.
ADDART({id:"animalmig",t:"Animal Migration",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🧭",w:"Animal migration",r:["birdmigr","salmon","seaturtle","whale","animalbehavior","antelopes"],
p:[["Long journeys","Many animals make regular journeys between places where they feed and places where they breed."],["Following the seasons","Migration usually follows the seasons, food or water."],["Amazing navigators","Animals find their way using the Sun, the stars, smells and Earth’s magnetic field."]],
l:"Animal migration is the regular movement of animals from one place to another and back again, usually with the seasons. Animals migrate to find food, water, warmer weather or safe places to breed and raise their young. Some journeys are short, like frogs crossing a road to a pond, while others span the globe: Arctic terns fly from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back every year. Birds, fish, whales, sea turtles, insects, crabs and large grazing mammals all migrate. Scientists study migration with leg bands, tags and tiny satellite trackers.",
s:[["Why animals migrate","Food and weather change through the year. In the far north, summer brings long days and plenty of insects, but winter is dark and frozen, so many birds fly south. Grazing animals follow the rains that make grass grow. Salmon and sea turtles return to the rivers and beaches where they hatched to lay their own eggs."],["Record breakers","Arctic terns can fly 70,000 to 90,000 kilometers in a year. In 2022 a bar-tailed godwit flew 13,560 kilometers from Alaska to Tasmania in 11 days without stopping to land, eat or drink. Gray whales swim up to about 20,000 kilometers on their round trip between the Arctic and Mexico."],["Finding the way","Migrating animals use several senses. Birds can sense Earth’s magnetic field and use the positions of the Sun and stars. Salmon recognize the smell of their home stream. Young animals often inherit the direction and distance they need to travel, while others learn routes by following older animals."],["Insects and crabs","Monarch butterflies travel up to about 4,800 kilometers from Canada and the United States to forests in central Mexico, a trip that takes several generations to complete. On Christmas Island in the Indian Ocean, tens of millions of red crabs march from the forest to the sea each year to release their eggs."]],
k:[["Longest yearly journey","Arctic tern, up to about 90,000 km"],["Longest nonstop flight","Bar-tailed godwit, 13,560 km (2022)"],["Navigation","Sun, stars, smell, magnetic field"],["Migrating groups","Birds, fish, whales, insects, mammals"],["Studied with","Bands, tags and satellite trackers"]],
d:[["1822","A stork shot in Germany carries an African spear, proving it had flown from Africa"],["1899","Scientists in Denmark begin ringing birds"],["1975","The monarchs’ winter forests in Mexico become known to scientists"],["2010","Trackers show Arctic terns flying about 70,000 km a year"],["2022","A bar-tailed godwit sets a nonstop record of 13,560 km"]],
s2:[["The biggest migration of all","Every night, countless small sea animals rise from the deep ocean to feed near the surface, then sink again at dawn to hide from predators. This daily trip, called diel vertical migration, involves more living things than any other migration on Earth."],["Threats along the way","Migrating animals need safe stopovers. Lost wetlands, dams, fences, bright city lights and climate change all make journeys harder. Because migrants cross borders, countries work together to protect them, for example through the Convention on Migratory Species."]],
sp:["The Philippines on the flyway","The Philippines lies on the East Asian–Australasian Flyway. Every year, migrating shorebirds and ducks from Siberia, China and Japan stop at Philippine wetlands such as the Olango Island Wildlife Sanctuary in Cebu to rest and feed."],
g:[["Migration","A regular journey between places, often with the seasons"],["Flyway","A route used by many migrating birds"],["Stopover","A place where migrants rest and feed during a journey"]],
f:["Arctic terns see more daylight than any other animal because they chase summer around the world.","Monarch butterflies take several generations to complete one round trip.","A bar-tailed godwit flew 13,560 kilometers without landing."],
fun:"A white stork shot in Germany in 1822 had an African spear stuck in its neck, proving that storks fly to Africa for the winter."});

ADDART({id:"hibernation",t:"Hibernation",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐻",w:"Hibernation",r:["bear","bat","animalbehavior","frog","sleep"],
p:[["The long winter rest","Hibernation is a deep, energy-saving state that some animals enter for the cold months."],["Slowing everything down","Body temperature, heartbeat and breathing drop far below normal."],["Not just sleep","Hibernation is different from ordinary sleep."]],
l:"Hibernation is a state of very low activity that helps some animals survive winter, when food is scarce and the weather is cold. A hibernating animal’s body temperature, heart rate and breathing drop sharply, and it lives on fat stored during summer and autumn. Hedgehogs, many bats, ground squirrels, dormice and some lemurs are true hibernators. Bears enter a lighter winter dormancy. Some animals, including lungfish and some frogs and snails, enter a similar state called estivation to survive heat and drought.",
s:[["How it works","Before hibernating, animals eat a lot and build up fat. Then their bodies enter a state called torpor. A hibernating ground squirrel’s heart may beat only a few times a minute instead of hundreds, and its body becomes almost as cold as its burrow. Every few days or weeks, most hibernators briefly warm up, which uses much of their stored energy."],["The coldest hibernators","The Arctic ground squirrel of Alaska and Canada can let its body temperature fall below the freezing point of water, to about −3 °C, without its blood freezing. Wood frogs go even further: up to about two-thirds of the water in their bodies can freeze in winter, and they thaw out and hop away in spring."],["Bears","Black bears and brown bears spend months in dens without eating, drinking or passing waste. Their body temperature drops only a few degrees, and they can wake quickly. Female bears give birth to tiny cubs in the den in the middle of winter and nurse them until spring."],["Hibernation in warm places","Not all hibernators live in cold places. The fat-tailed dwarf lemur of Madagascar hibernates for several months during the dry season, storing fat in its tail. The common poorwill of North America is the only bird known to hibernate for long periods."]],
k:[["Purpose","Save energy when food is scarce"],["True hibernators","Hedgehogs, many bats, ground squirrels, dormice"],["Coldest body","Arctic ground squirrel, about −3 °C"],["Only hibernating bird","Common poorwill"],["Hot-weather version","Estivation"]],
d:[["1946","Scientists find a common poorwill hibernating in a California canyon"],["1989","Studies show Arctic ground squirrels cool below freezing"],["2004","The fat-tailed dwarf lemur is shown to hibernate in the tropics"],["2010s","Scientists study hibernation to help protect human organs and astronauts"]],
s2:[["Lessons for medicine and space","Hibernating animals avoid muscle and bone loss despite months without moving. Scientists study them to find ways to protect patients during surgery or long hospital stays, and to see whether astronauts might one day travel to Mars in a hibernation-like state."],["White-nose syndrome","Since 2006, a fungus has killed millions of hibernating bats in North America. It wakes them too often in winter, so they burn through their fat and starve."]],
sp:["Torpor in the Philippines","Some tropical bats and small mammals enter short periods of torpor, sometimes just for a day, to save energy when food is scarce, rather than hibernating through a whole winter."],
g:[["Torpor","A state of lowered body temperature and activity"],["Estivation","A dormant state during heat or drought"],["Dormancy","A period when growth and activity slow or stop"]],
f:["Wood frogs can survive with much of their body frozen.","The common poorwill is the only bird known to hibernate for weeks.","Mother bears give birth during their winter rest."],
fun:"Wood frogs can freeze solid in winter and come back to life in spring."});

ADDART({id:"camouflage",t:"Camouflage",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦎",w:"Camouflage",r:["chameleon","octopus","insect","animalbehavior","adaptation","venom"],
p:[["The art of hiding","Camouflage helps animals blend into their surroundings."],["For hunters and hunted","Prey use it to avoid being eaten, and predators use it to sneak up."],["Many tricks","Animals use color, pattern, shape and even behavior to hide."]],
l:"Camouflage is any feature that helps an animal avoid being seen. Many animals match the color and pattern of their background, like a sandy flatfish on the seafloor. Others are shaped like leaves, twigs or stones, or have bold patterns that break up their outline. Camouflage evolved because animals that were harder to see were more likely to survive and have young. Some animals, such as octopuses, cuttlefish and chameleons, can change their color in moments, while others change with the seasons, like the Arctic fox, which is white in winter and brown in summer.",
s:[["Matching the background","The simplest camouflage is to look like the surroundings. Snowy owls and polar bears are white, desert animals are sandy brown, and many tree frogs are leaf green. Some animals even decorate themselves: decorator crabs stick bits of seaweed and sponge onto their shells."],["Breaking up the outline","Bold stripes and spots can make an animal’s shape hard to recognize. This is called disruptive coloration. A tiger’s stripes blend with tall grass and shadows, and a giraffe’s patches match dappled light under trees."],["Countershading","Many animals, from sharks to deer, are darker on top and lighter underneath. Sunlight from above usually makes the underside look shadowed, so this pattern cancels out the shadow and makes the animal look flat and harder to see."],["Masters of disguise","Leaf insects, found in Southeast Asia including the Philippines, look like leaves, complete with fake veins and bite marks. Stick insects look like twigs, and some caterpillars look like bird droppings. Octopuses and cuttlefish have skin cells called chromatophores that can change their color and texture in a fraction of a second."]],
k:[["Purpose","Avoid being seen"],["Main types","Background matching, disruptive patterns, countershading, disguise"],["Fastest color change","Octopuses and cuttlefish"],["Seasonal change","Arctic fox, snowshoe hare, ptarmigan"],["Human use","Military uniforms and hunting clothes"]],
d:[["1890","Edward Poulton publishes The Colours of Animals"],["1896","Abbott Thayer describes countershading"],["1909","Gerald Thayer publishes a book on animal concealment"],["1915","Armies begin using camouflage painting in World War I"]],
s2:[["The opposite of hiding","Some animals do the opposite and show off bright colors to warn predators that they are poisonous or taste bad. This is called warning coloration. Other harmless animals copy these colors, a trick called mimicry."],["Learning from animals","Artists and scientists who studied animal camouflage helped armies disguise ships, soldiers and equipment in the World Wars. Today, engineers are developing materials that change color like octopus skin."]],
sp:["The leaf that walks","The Philippine leaf insect is so convincing that it sways gently as it walks, like a leaf moving in the breeze."],
g:[["Camouflage","Features that help an animal avoid being seen"],["Countershading","Being darker on top and lighter below"],["Chromatophore","A skin cell that can change color"]],
f:["Cuttlefish can change color even though they are probably color-blind.","Some caterpillars look like bird droppings to avoid being eaten.","Arctic foxes change color with the seasons."],
fun:"Some caterpillars avoid being eaten by looking exactly like bird droppings."});

ADDART({id:"venom",t:"Venomous Animals",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐍",w:"Venom",r:["snake","spider","jelly","platypus","seasnakes"],
p:[["Chemical weapons","Venom is a mix of toxic chemicals that an animal injects into another."],["For hunting and defense","Animals use venom to catch prey and to protect themselves."],["From poison to medicine","Some venoms have been turned into life-saving drugs."]],
l:"Venom is a mixture of toxic substances that an animal delivers into another animal’s body through a bite, sting or spine. It is different from poison, which harms an animal that touches or eats it: a snake that bites is venomous, while a frog that is toxic to eat is poisonous. Venom has evolved many times, in snakes, spiders, scorpions, bees, jellyfish, cone snails, some fish and even a few mammals, such as the male platypus. Many venoms are complex cocktails of proteins that attack nerves, blood or muscles. Scientists study them to develop new medicines.",
s:[["Venom or poison?","The difference is in how the toxin gets in. Venomous animals have fangs, stingers or spines to inject their venom. Poisonous animals, like poison dart frogs and pufferfish, are harmful only if touched or eaten. A few animals are both."],["Some of the most venomous","The box jellyfish of Australian and Indo-Pacific waters has tentacles covered in millions of stinging cells, and its venom can kill a person within minutes. The inland taipan of Australia has the most toxic venom of any land snake, but it is shy and rarely bites. Some cone snails, found on reefs including in the Philippines, can kill a person with a harpoon-like tooth."],["How venom works","Neurotoxins attack the nervous system and can stop breathing. Hemotoxins damage blood and tissues, and some prevent blood from clotting. Snakes and spiders often use venom to paralyze prey and begin digesting it before they eat."],["Medicines from venom","The blood pressure drug captopril, approved in 1981, was developed from the venom of a Brazilian pit viper. Ziconotide, a powerful painkiller approved in 2004, comes from cone snail venom; the Filipino scientist Baldomero Olivera was a pioneer of cone snail research. Exenatide, a diabetes drug approved in 2005, was based on a substance in Gila monster saliva."]],
k:[["Definition","Toxin injected by bite, sting or spine"],["Most toxic land snake","Inland taipan"],["Venomous mammals","Male platypus, some shrews, slow lorises"],["Medicines from venom","Captopril, ziconotide, exenatide"],["Treatment for bites","Antivenom"]],
d:[["1895","Albert Calmette develops one of the first antivenoms, for cobra bites"],["1981","Captopril, based on snake venom, is approved"],["2004","Ziconotide, from cone snail venom, is approved"],["2005","Exenatide, based on Gila monster saliva, is approved"],["2017","The World Health Organization lists snakebite as a neglected tropical disease"]],
s2:[["Snakebite","Snakebites kill tens of thousands of people every year, mostly farmers and children in rural Asia and Africa. Antivenom, made by injecting small amounts of venom into horses and collecting the antibodies they produce, can save lives if given quickly."],["Venom in the Philippines","The Philippines is home to venomous animals such as the Philippine cobra, sea kraits, stonefish and cone snails. Most snakes in the country are harmless, and venomous ones usually bite only when threatened or stepped on."]],
sp:["A Filipino scientist and the cone snail","Baldomero Olivera began studying cone snail venom in the Philippines in the 1970s. His work showed that each species makes hundreds of different toxins, and it led to a new kind of painkiller."],
g:[["Venom","A toxin injected by a bite, sting or spine"],["Poison","A toxin that harms when touched or eaten"],["Antivenom","A medicine that neutralizes venom"]],
f:["Male platypuses have venomous spurs on their back legs.","A painkiller made from cone snail venom is many times stronger than morphine.","Antivenom is made using antibodies from horses or sheep."],
fun:"Male platypuses have venomous spurs on their hind legs, one of the few mammals with venom."});

ADDART({id:"animalcomm",t:"How Animals Communicate",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐝",w:"Animal communication",r:["animalbehavior","bee","whale","elephant","animalintel","grasshoppers"],
p:[["Messages without words","Animals send messages with sounds, colors, movements, smells and touch."],["Why they communicate","They warn of danger, find mates, mark territory and share where food is."],["Hidden signals","Some signals are too high, too low or too faint for humans to notice."]],
l:"Animal communication is the passing of information from one animal to another. Animals use sound, sight, smell, touch and even electricity and vibrations. A bird’s song can attract a mate and warn rivals to stay away. Honeybees dance to show their nestmates where flowers are. Ants leave scent trails, fireflies flash patterns of light, and elephants rumble at frequencies too low for humans to hear. Some animals, such as vervet monkeys and prairie dogs, have different alarm calls for different predators. Communication helps animals survive, cooperate and raise their young.",
s:[["Sound","Birds, frogs, insects, whales and monkeys all communicate with sound. Male humpback whales sing long, complex songs that change from year to year and spread between groups. Elephants make low rumbles called infrasound that can travel several kilometers, and bats use sounds too high for people to hear."],["Sight","Many animals send visual signals. Peacocks spread their tails, cuttlefish ripple patterns across their skin, and fireflies use flashes of light, each species with its own code. Wolves and dogs use body language, such as wagging tails or showing teeth."],["Smell and touch","Chemical signals called pheromones carry messages about danger, mates and territory. Ants lay scent trails that lead others to food. Cats rub their faces on objects to leave scent marks. Primates groom each other to build friendships."],["The waggle dance","When a honeybee finds good flowers, it returns to the hive and performs a figure-eight ‘waggle dance’. The angle of the dance shows the direction of the food compared with the Sun, and the length of the waggle shows how far away it is. The Austrian scientist Karl von Frisch decoded the dance and shared the Nobel Prize in 1973."]],
k:[["Channels","Sound, sight, smell, touch, vibration, electricity"],["Famous dance","Honeybee waggle dance"],["Decoded by","Karl von Frisch (Nobel Prize, 1973)"],["Low-pitched calls","Elephants (infrasound)"],["Chemical signals","Pheromones"]],
d:[["1927","Karl von Frisch publishes his findings on how bees communicate"],["1967","Recordings of humpback whale songs are first studied"],["1970","Songs of the Humpback Whale becomes a best-selling album"],["1973","Von Frisch shares the Nobel Prize for his bee research"],["1980","Scientists show vervet monkeys have different alarm calls for different predators"]],
s2:[["Is it language?","Human language uses grammar to combine words into unlimited new meanings. Most animal signals have fixed meanings, but some animals, such as Japanese tits, combine calls in a particular order. Scientists are now using artificial intelligence to look for patterns in whale and bird sounds."],["Noise pollution","Ship engines, traffic and city noise can drown out animal calls. Some birds in cities sing louder or at higher pitches to be heard, and whales change their calls near busy shipping lanes."]],
sp:["Talking with light","In some parts of Southeast Asia, including the Philippines, thousands of male fireflies gather in mangrove trees and flash in rhythm together, creating a glowing display to attract females."],
g:[["Pheromone","A chemical signal that carries a message"],["Infrasound","Sound too low for humans to hear"],["Alarm call","A signal that warns others of danger"]],
f:["Humpback whale songs change from year to year.","Elephants can hear rumbles through their feet.","Each firefly species has its own flash pattern."],
fun:"Honeybees show each other where flowers are by dancing in a figure eight."});

ADDART({id:"animalintel",t:"Animal Intelligence",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐦",w:"Animal cognition",r:["animalcomm","crow","octopus","parrot","elephant","chimpanzees","pigs"],
p:[["Clever creatures","Many animals can solve problems, use tools and learn from each other."],["Different kinds of smart","Each species is clever in ways that suit its life."],["Studying animal minds","Scientists design careful tests to understand how animals think."]],
l:"Animal intelligence, or animal cognition, is the ability of animals to learn, remember, solve problems and make decisions. Scientists once thought only humans could use tools or plan ahead, but studies have shown surprising abilities in many species. Chimpanzees use sticks to fish for termites, crows bend wire into hooks, octopuses open jars, and dolphins recognize themselves in mirrors. Intelligence comes in many forms, and each animal’s abilities fit the challenges of its own life. Brain size matters less than how the brain is organized: crows and parrots have small brains packed with neurons.",
s:[["Tool use","In 1960, the scientist Jane Goodall saw a chimpanzee in Tanzania strip leaves from a twig and use it to fish termites from a mound. This changed how scientists thought about animals. Since then, tool use has been seen in sea otters, which crack shells with stones, dolphins, which carry sponges to protect their snouts, and many birds."],["Brilliant birds","New Caledonian crows make hooked tools from twigs and leaves. In 2002, a crow named Betty bent a straight piece of wire into a hook to lift a bucket of food. An African grey parrot named Alex, studied by Irene Pepperberg from 1977 until his death in 2007, learned more than 100 words and could name colors, shapes and numbers."],["Self-awareness","In the mirror test, an animal is marked with a spot it can see only in a mirror. If it touches the spot on its own body, it seems to recognize itself. Great apes, dolphins, Asian elephants and Eurasian magpies have passed versions of the test."],["Octopus minds","Octopuses are invertebrates, yet they can solve puzzles, escape from tanks, use coconut shells as portable shelters and recognize individual people. Most of their neurons are in their arms, which can taste and explore on their own."]],
k:[["Also called","Animal cognition"],["First wild tool use seen","Chimpanzees, by Jane Goodall in 1960"],["Mirror test pioneer","Gordon Gallup (1970)"],["Famous parrot","Alex, an African grey"],["Toolmaking birds","New Caledonian crows"]],
d:[["1960","Jane Goodall sees chimpanzees making tools"],["1970","Gordon Gallup develops the mirror test"],["1977","Irene Pepperberg begins her work with Alex the parrot"],["2002","Betty the crow bends wire into a hook"],["2008","Eurasian magpies pass the mirror test"]],
s2:[["Culture in animals","Some animals pass on habits by learning rather than instinct. Different groups of chimpanzees use different tools, and humpback whales spread new songs and feeding tricks. Scientists call these traditions animal culture."],["Why it matters","Understanding animal minds affects how we treat animals in farms, zoos and research, and many countries now recognize that animals can feel pain and emotions."]],
sp:["Clever cousins in Asia","Long-tailed macaques, which live in the Philippines and across Southeast Asia, use stones to crack open shellfish and nuts on beaches in Thailand."],
g:[["Cognition","The mental processes of learning and thinking"],["Mirror test","A test of whether an animal recognizes itself"],["Animal culture","Habits passed on by learning"]],
f:["Crows can recognize individual human faces and remember them for years.","Octopuses carry coconut shells to use as shelters.","Alex the parrot learned more than 100 words."],
fun:"Crows can remember a person’s face for years and warn other crows about people who treated them badly."});

ADDART({id:"animalhomes",t:"Animal Builders and Homes",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦫",w:"Beaver dam",r:["termite","swiftlets","bee","ants","animalbehavior"],
p:[["Built by animals","Many animals build nests, burrows, dams and mounds."],["Shelter and safety","Homes protect animals from weather and predators and give their young a safe start."],["Engineers of nature","Some animal buildings change whole landscapes."]],
l:"Many animals build structures to live in, store food or raise their young. Birds weave nests, rabbits and meerkats dig burrows, beavers build dams and lodges, termites raise towering mounds, and bees make combs of wax. Some of these structures are remarkably complex: termite mounds have tunnels that help control temperature and air, and beaver dams can create ponds that change entire valleys. Animals usually build by instinct, but some, such as bowerbirds, improve their skills with practice.",
s:[["Beavers","Beavers cut down trees with their teeth and pile up branches, mud and stones to dam streams. The pond that forms protects their lodge, whose entrance is underwater. The longest known beaver dam, in Wood Buffalo National Park in Canada, is about 850 meters long and can be seen in satellite images."],["Termite cities","Some African and Australian termites build mounds several meters tall from soil, saliva and dung. Inside, a network of tunnels helps air move and keeps the temperature steady. Engineers have studied termite mounds to design buildings that stay cool without air conditioning."],["Master nest builders","Weaverbirds knot grass into hanging nests. Sociable weavers in southern Africa build giant shared nests with many separate chambers, used by hundreds of birds for generations. Male bowerbirds build decorated displays from sticks and collect colorful objects to attract females."],["Nests in the Philippines","Edible-nest swiftlets build nests from their own hardened saliva on cave walls, including in Palawan. Giant honeybees build huge single combs hanging from tree branches and cliffs, and weaver ants sew leaves together using silk made by their larvae."]],
k:[["Longest beaver dam","About 850 m, Canada"],["Tallest animal-built mounds","Termite mounds, several meters"],["Shared nests","Sociable weavers"],["Decorated displays","Bowerbirds"],["Saliva nests","Edible-nest swiftlets"]],
d:[["1996","The Eastgate Centre in Zimbabwe opens with cooling inspired by termite mounds"],["1997","Janine Benyus’s book Biomimicry popularizes learning design from nature"],["2007","The longest known beaver dam is spotted in satellite images"],["2014","Beavers are seen living wild in England for the first time in centuries"]],
s2:[["Ecosystem engineers","Animals that change their surroundings by building are called ecosystem engineers. Beaver ponds create wetlands for fish, frogs, insects and birds, and abandoned burrows become homes for other species."],["Using human things","Many animals now build with or in human-made materials. Birds nest in streetlights and buildings, hermit crabs use plastic caps as shells, and some birds weave plastic and string into their nests."]],
sp:["Ants that sew","Weaver ants, common in Philippine trees, hold leaves together in long chains while others squeeze silk from larvae to glue the leaves into a nest."],
g:[["Ecosystem engineer","An animal that changes its surroundings"],["Lodge","A beaver’s dome-shaped home"],["Biomimicry","Design that copies ideas from nature"]],
f:["The longest beaver dam can be seen from space.","A sociable weaver nest can be used for over 100 years.","Weaver ants use their own larvae as glue guns."],
fun:"The longest known beaver dam, in Canada, is about 850 meters long and was discovered using satellite images."});

;
/* data/life2.js */
// Algae, fungi and microbes, and how life works (27 November 2026).
ADDART({id:"plankton",t:"Plankton",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦠",w:"Plankton",r:["algae","whale","oceancurrents","foodchains","photo","animalmig","carboncycle","krill"],
p:[["Drifters of the sea","Plankton are tiny living things that drift with ocean currents."],["Plants and animals","Phytoplankton make food from sunlight, and zooplankton eat them."],["Base of ocean life","Almost all life in the sea depends on plankton."]],
l:"Plankton are the countless small organisms that drift in oceans, lakes and rivers, unable to swim against the current. The word comes from a Greek word meaning ‘wanderer’ or ‘drifter’. Phytoplankton, such as diatoms and cyanobacteria, use sunlight to make food, like plants do, and produce roughly half of the oxygen made on Earth each year. Zooplankton, such as copepods, krill and the larvae of fish and crabs, eat phytoplankton or each other. Together they form the base of almost every food chain in water, feeding everything from small fish to the largest whales.",
s:[["Phytoplankton","Phytoplankton are mostly single cells too small to see without a microscope. Diatoms live inside beautiful glass-like shells made of silica, and dinoflagellates spin through the water with two whip-like tails. A tiny cyanobacterium called Prochlorococcus, discovered in the 1980s, is thought to be the most abundant photosynthetic organism on Earth."],["Zooplankton","Zooplankton range from single-celled creatures to jellyfish. Copepods, relatives of crabs smaller than a grain of rice, are among the most numerous animals on the planet. Antarctic krill gather in swarms so dense they can color the sea, and they feed whales, seals, penguins and fish."],["The daily journey","Each night, enormous numbers of zooplankton rise toward the surface to feed in the dark, then sink back to deep water at dawn to hide from predators. This daily movement, called diel vertical migration, is thought to be the largest migration on Earth."],["Plankton and the climate","Phytoplankton take in carbon dioxide as they grow. When they die or are eaten, some of that carbon sinks to the deep ocean, where it can stay for centuries. This ‘biological pump’ helps control the amount of carbon dioxide in the air."]],
k:[["Meaning of the name","Drifter or wanderer (Greek)"],["Two main kinds","Phytoplankton and zooplankton"],["Oxygen made","Roughly half of Earth’s yearly oxygen production"],["Most numerous animals","Copepods"],["Largest plankton","Some jellyfish"]],
d:[["1887","Victor Hensen coins the word ‘plankton’"],["1904","Ernst Haeckel publishes drawings of diatoms and radiolarians in Art Forms in Nature"],["1931","The Continuous Plankton Recorder survey begins in the North Sea"],["1988","Scientists describe Prochlorococcus"],["2009","The Tara Oceans expedition sets out to sample plankton around the world"]],
s2:[["Glowing seas","Some plankton glow when disturbed, a light called bioluminescence. On certain beaches, breaking waves sparkle blue at night because of glowing dinoflagellates."],["Plankton under pressure","Warming seas and changes in ocean chemistry affect where plankton grow and which kinds thrive. Because so much sea life depends on them, scientists monitor plankton closely."]],
sp:["Feeding giants","Whale sharks, the largest fish in the world, feed on plankton by filtering seawater. In the Philippines, whale sharks gather to feed in places such as Donsol in Sorsogon."],
g:[["Plankton","Organisms that drift with currents"],["Phytoplankton","Plankton that make food from sunlight"],["Zooplankton","Animal-like plankton that eat other plankton"]],
f:["Phytoplankton produce roughly half of the oxygen made on Earth each year.","The biggest animals on Earth, blue whales, eat some of the smallest: krill.","Some plankton make the sea glow at night."],
fun:"The largest animal ever known, the blue whale, lives on tiny shrimp-like krill."});

ADDART({id:"algae",t:"Algae",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🟢",w:"Algae",r:["seaweeds","kelp","plankton","photo","lichens","plantevolution"],
p:[["Simple food makers","Algae are living things that make food from sunlight but are not true plants."],["Tiny to giant","They range from single cells to seaweeds dozens of meters long."],["Oxygen makers","Algae and their relatives have been making oxygen for billions of years."]],
l:"Algae are a large and varied group of living things that make their own food by photosynthesis, as plants do, but lack true roots, stems and leaves. They live in oceans, lakes, rivers, damp soil, snow and even on the fur of sloths. Some are single cells, like diatoms and the green alga Chlamydomonas, while others are large seaweeds such as kelp. Algae make much of the world’s oxygen and feed countless animals. People eat algae, use them to make products from ice cream to toothpaste, and study them as a source of fuel.",
s:[["Kinds of algae","Scientists divide algae into several groups that are not all closely related. Green algae are relatives of land plants. Red algae include the seaweeds used to make agar and carrageenan. Brown algae include kelp and sargassum. Diatoms are single cells with glassy shells. Blue-green ‘algae’ are really cyanobacteria, a kind of bacteria."],["Algae and the air we breathe","More than two billion years ago, cyanobacteria began releasing oxygen into the air, transforming the planet and making animal life possible. Today, algae and cyanobacteria in the oceans produce a large share of Earth’s oxygen."],["Algae in daily life","Agar from red algae is used in desserts and in laboratories to grow bacteria. Carrageenan thickens ice cream, chocolate milk and toothpaste. Nori is used to wrap sushi, and spirulina, a cyanobacterium, is sold as a health food."],["Harmful algal blooms","When water has too many nutrients, often from fertilizers or sewage, algae can grow out of control. Some blooms make toxins. In the Philippines, blooms of the dinoflagellate Pyrodinium bahamense cause ‘red tide’, and authorities ban eating shellfish from affected waters because the toxins can cause paralysis."]],
k:[["Make food by","Photosynthesis"],["Main groups","Green, red and brown algae, diatoms"],["Not really algae","Blue-green algae (cyanobacteria)"],["Products","Agar, carrageenan, nori, spirulina"],["Harmful blooms","Red tide"]],
d:[["c.2.4 billion years ago","Oxygen from cyanobacteria builds up in the air"],["1658","Japanese tradition credits the accidental discovery of agar"],["1882","Robert Koch’s laboratory begins using agar to grow bacteria"],["1970s","Seaweed farming for carrageenan expands in the Philippines"],["1983","A major red tide hits Philippine waters"]],
s2:[["Fuel from algae","Some algae store oils that can be turned into biofuel. Scientists have grown algae in ponds and tubes, but making algae fuel cheaply enough to compete with other fuels remains difficult."],["Snow algae","Some algae live on snow and ice, turning it pink or red. This ‘watermelon snow’ is found on mountains and glaciers, and because it is darker than white snow, it can make the ice melt faster."]],
sp:["The Philippines and carrageenan","The Philippines is one of the world’s top producers of carrageenan seaweed. Coastal families in places such as Tawi-Tawi and Palawan farm red seaweeds on lines in shallow water."],
g:[["Algae","Simple living things that make food from sunlight"],["Cyanobacteria","Bacteria that photosynthesize, once called blue-green algae"],["Algal bloom","A rapid overgrowth of algae"]],
f:["Some algae live on the fur of sloths.","Agar from seaweed is used to grow bacteria in laboratories.","Blue-green algae are actually bacteria."],
fun:"Some sloths grow green algae in their fur, which may help them hide in the trees."});

ADDART({id:"slimemolds",t:"Slime Molds",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🟡",w:"Slime mold",r:["fungi","microbe","decomposers","animalintel","classification"],
p:[["Not fungi, not animals","Slime molds are strange living things that can act like single cells or a single giant blob."],["Brainless problem solvers","They can find the shortest path through a maze."],["Forest recyclers","Slime molds feed on bacteria and fungi on rotting logs and leaves."]],
l:"Slime molds are unusual living things once grouped with fungi, but now known to belong to a separate group of single-celled organisms called amoebozoans. They live on rotting wood, leaves and soil, where they feed on bacteria, yeasts and fungi. Plasmodial slime molds, such as the bright yellow Physarum polycephalum, form a single giant cell with many nuclei that can spread across a log and move slowly in search of food. Although they have no brain or nerves, slime molds can solve mazes and build efficient networks, and scientists study them to learn how simple systems make decisions.",
s:[["Two kinds","Plasmodial slime molds form one huge cell containing millions of nuclei, which oozes along like a living net. Cellular slime molds, such as Dictyostelium, live as separate amoebas that, when food runs out, gather together into a slug-like body and then a stalk that releases spores."],["Life of a slime mold","When food is plentiful, a slime mold spreads out and feeds. When it dries out or starves, it forms tough spore-producing structures, often on little stalks. The spores blow away and later hatch into new cells. Some slime molds can dry out completely and revive when water returns."],["Solving mazes","In 2000, the Japanese scientist Toshiyuki Nakagaki placed pieces of Physarum in a maze with food at the entrance and exit. The slime mold spread through the maze and then pulled back from dead ends, leaving a single tube along the shortest route."],["Designing railways","In 2010, researchers placed oat flakes on a map in the positions of cities around Tokyo. Physarum grew a network linking the flakes that looked strikingly similar to the real railway system, balancing short distances with backup routes."]],
k:[["Group","Amoebozoa (not fungi)"],["Famous species","Physarum polycephalum"],["Food","Bacteria, yeasts and fungi"],["Brain","None"],["Famous experiments","Maze solving (2000), Tokyo rail network (2010)"]],
d:[["1860s","Scientists begin to argue that slime molds are not fungi"],["1935","Kenneth Raper discovers the cellular slime mold Dictyostelium discoideum"],["2000","A slime mold solves a maze"],["2010","A slime mold recreates the Tokyo rail network"],["2019","The Paris Zoological Park displays a slime mold nicknamed ‘the blob’"]],
s2:[["Memory without a brain","Slime molds can avoid places where they met something unpleasant before, such as salt, and can anticipate regular changes in their surroundings. Scientists think they store information in the structure of their tube networks."],["The blob in space","In 2021, pieces of Physarum were sent to the International Space Station so that astronaut Thomas Pesquet could run experiments alongside students on Earth."]],
sp:["Look closely","Slime molds are common in damp forests, including in the Philippines. After rain, look on rotting logs for bright yellow, orange or white blobs and tiny stalked spore cases."],
g:[["Slime mold","A single-celled organism that can form a large moving mass"],["Plasmodium","A giant single cell with many nuclei"],["Spore","A tiny cell that can grow into a new organism"]],
f:["A slime mold can solve a maze without a brain.","Some slime molds can dry out and come back to life.","The slime mold Physarum polycephalum has more than 700 sexes."],
fun:"The slime mold Physarum polycephalum has more than 700 mating types, so you could say it has hundreds of sexes."});

ADDART({id:"yeast",t:"Yeast",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🍞",w:"Yeast",r:["fungi","microbe","wheat","cheese","dna"],
p:[["A helpful fungus","Yeasts are tiny single-celled fungi."],["Bread and drinks","Yeast makes bread rise and turns sugar into alcohol."],["A star of science","Yeast is one of the most studied living things in the world."]],
l:"Yeasts are single-celled fungi, each cell far too small to see without a microscope. There are about 1,500 known species, living on fruit, flowers, soil and even inside animals. The best known is baker’s yeast, Saccharomyces cerevisiae, which people have used for thousands of years to make bread rise and to brew beer and wine. Yeast feeds on sugar and, without oxygen, turns it into alcohol and carbon dioxide gas, a process called fermentation. Because yeast cells work much like human cells, scientists use yeast to study genes, aging and disease, and to make medicines.",
s:[["How fermentation works","When yeast eats sugar without enough oxygen, it releases carbon dioxide and ethanol. In bread dough, the gas forms bubbles that make the dough rise, and the alcohol evaporates during baking. In brewing, the alcohol stays, and the gas makes drinks fizzy."],["Discovering yeast","People used yeast for thousands of years without knowing what it was. In 1680, Antonie van Leeuwenhoek saw yeast cells through his microscope. In the 1850s and 1860s, Louis Pasteur showed that fermentation is carried out by living yeast. In 1897, Eduard Buchner showed that juice from crushed yeast could ferment sugar, leading to the discovery of enzymes, and he won the Nobel Prize in 1907."],["A model for science","In 1996, baker’s yeast became the first organism with a nucleus to have its entire genome sequenced. Many yeast genes have human counterparts, so yeast helps scientists understand cell division, aging and cancer. Several Nobel Prizes have been awarded for discoveries made with yeast."],["Medicines from yeast","Scientists can insert human genes into yeast so that it produces useful proteins. The first vaccine made this way, against hepatitis B, was approved in 1986, and yeast is also used to produce insulin for people with diabetes."]],
k:[["Kingdom","Fungi"],["Known species","About 1,500"],["Baker’s yeast","Saccharomyces cerevisiae"],["Fermentation makes","Alcohol and carbon dioxide"],["First genome of a cell with a nucleus","Yeast, 1996"]],
d:[["c.7000 BC","Traces of fermented drinks are made in China"],["1680","Antonie van Leeuwenhoek sees yeast cells"],["1857","Louis Pasteur shows fermentation is caused by living cells"],["1897","Eduard Buchner ferments sugar with yeast juice"],["1986","A hepatitis B vaccine made in yeast is approved"],["1996","The yeast genome is sequenced"]],
s2:[["Yeast in the Philippines","Traditional Filipino drinks and foods rely on yeasts and other microbes. Tapuy, a rice wine of the Cordillera, and basi, an Ilocano sugarcane wine, are made using starter cultures. In the Cordillera, the starter for tapuy is called bubod."],["Harmful yeasts","Most yeasts are harmless, but some, such as Candida, can cause infections in people, especially when the body’s defenses are weak."]],
sp:["The bubbles in your bread","The holes in a slice of bread are bubbles of carbon dioxide made by millions of yeast cells feeding on the sugars in the flour."],
g:[["Yeast","A single-celled fungus"],["Fermentation","Breaking down sugar without oxygen"],["Genome","All of an organism’s genetic material"]],
f:["A gram of baker’s yeast contains billions of cells.","Yeast was the first cell with a nucleus to have its genome sequenced.","Some insulin used by people with diabetes is made by yeast."],
fun:"About a third of yeast genes have close relatives in the human body."});

ADDART({id:"adaptation",t:"Adaptation",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐫",w:"Adaptation",r:["evo","camouflage","darwin","mendel","desert","naturalselection"],
p:[["Fitting in","An adaptation is a feature that helps a living thing survive where it lives."],["Built by evolution","Adaptations develop over many generations through natural selection."],["Everywhere you look","Camels’ humps, cactus spines and polar bear fur are all adaptations."]],
l:"An adaptation is an inherited feature that helps a living thing survive and reproduce in its environment. Adaptations can be parts of the body, such as a giraffe’s long neck or a duck’s webbed feet; ways the body works, such as a camel’s ability to go days without water; or behaviors, such as birds migrating south for winter. Adaptations arise through natural selection: individuals with helpful traits are more likely to survive and pass those traits on, so over many generations the traits become common. Charles Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace described this process in 1858.",
s:[["Body adaptations","Polar bears have thick fur and a layer of fat to stay warm. Fennec foxes have huge ears that release heat in the desert. Woodpeckers have strong beaks and shock-absorbing skulls. Cactuses store water in thick stems and have spines instead of leaves to reduce water loss."],["Adaptations at work","Some adaptations are about how the body works. Camels can lose a large share of their body water and refill quickly by drinking. Deep-diving seals store extra oxygen in their blood and muscles. Some Antarctic fish make antifreeze proteins that stop their blood from freezing."],["Watching evolution happen","On the Galápagos island of Daphne Major, Peter and Rosemary Grant measured finches for decades. After a drought in 1977, when only large, hard seeds were left, finches with bigger beaks survived better, and the next generation had larger beaks on average."],["Human adaptations","People have adaptations too. Many populations who have kept dairy animals for thousands of years can digest milk as adults. Tibetans and Andeans have adaptations for life at high altitudes. A 2018 study found that the Sama-Bajau sea nomads of Southeast Asia, including the Philippines, have larger spleens, which may help them dive longer."]],
k:[["Definition","An inherited feature that helps survival"],["Kinds","Body, function and behavior"],["Caused by","Natural selection over generations"],["Described by","Charles Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace (1858)"],["Famous study","Darwin’s finches on Daphne Major"]],
d:[["1858","Darwin and Wallace present natural selection"],["1859","Darwin publishes On the Origin of Species"],["1950s","Bernard Kettlewell studies peppered moths in polluted England"],["1977","A drought on Daphne Major changes finch beaks"],["2018","Scientists report large spleens in Sama-Bajau divers"]],
s2:[["The peppered moth","Before the Industrial Revolution, most peppered moths in England were pale and hard to see on lichen-covered trees. As soot darkened tree trunks, dark moths survived better and became common. When air pollution was reduced, pale moths returned."],["Not perfect","Adaptations are not perfect designs. Evolution can only work with what already exists, so many features are compromises, and an adaptation that helps in one place can be harmful in another."]],
sp:["Island adaptations","Animals on islands often evolve unusual sizes. The Philippines has small island animals such as the Philippine mouse-deer and the tamaraw, a small wild buffalo found only on Mindoro."],
g:[["Adaptation","A feature that helps a living thing survive"],["Natural selection","When helpful traits are passed on more often"],["Trait","A feature that can be inherited"]],
f:["Some Antarctic fish have antifreeze proteins in their blood.","Fennec foxes use their large ears to cool down.","Scientists watched finch beaks change after a single drought."],
fun:"Some Antarctic fish have natural antifreeze in their blood so they do not freeze in icy water."});

ADDART({id:"lifecycles",t:"Life Cycles",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🔄",w:"Biological life cycle",r:["metamorph","seeds","frog","salmon","celldivision","hibernation"],
p:[["From birth to birth","A life cycle is the series of stages a living thing goes through as it grows and reproduces."],["Many patterns","Some animals simply grow bigger, while others change shape completely."],["Short and long lives","Lifespans range from a day to thousands of years."]],
l:"A life cycle is the series of changes a living thing goes through from the start of its life until it produces offspring of its own, which begin the cycle again. Many animals, including mammals, simply grow larger after birth. Others go through dramatic changes called metamorphosis: a caterpillar becomes a butterfly, and a tadpole becomes a frog. Plants grow from seeds into adults that make flowers and new seeds. Life cycles vary enormously in length, from mayflies whose adult lives last only a day to bristlecone pines that live for nearly 5,000 years.",
s:[["Animals","Mammals give birth to young that look like small adults, and birds and most reptiles hatch from eggs. Insects such as butterflies, beetles and flies have complete metamorphosis with four stages: egg, larva, pupa and adult. Grasshoppers and dragonflies have incomplete metamorphosis: the young, called nymphs, look like small wingless adults."],["Plants","A flowering plant begins as a seed, which sprouts into a seedling and grows into an adult plant. Its flowers are pollinated, produce seeds inside fruits, and the seeds are spread by wind, water or animals. Some plants, called annuals, complete their whole life cycle in one year, while trees may take decades to flower."],["Strange cycles","Periodical cicadas in North America spend 13 or 17 years underground as nymphs, then emerge together by the billions. Pacific salmon hatch in rivers, grow at sea, and return to spawn and die. Some bamboo species flower all at once only every several decades, then die."],["The longest and shortest lives","Adult mayflies may live less than a day. Mice live about two years. Greenland sharks may live for about 270 to 400 years, and some bristlecone pines in California are nearly 5,000 years old. The ‘immortal jellyfish’ Turritopsis dohrnii can turn back into an earlier stage of its life cycle when stressed."]],
k:[["Definition","Stages from birth to reproduction"],["Complete metamorphosis","Egg, larva, pupa, adult"],["Longest-lived animal with a backbone","Greenland shark"],["Oldest living trees","Bristlecone pines, nearly 5,000 years"],["Shortest adult life","Some mayflies, less than a day"]],
d:[["1669","Jan Swammerdam shows that insect metamorphosis is a gradual change"],["1705","Maria Sibylla Merian publishes paintings of insect life cycles in Suriname"],["1957","A bristlecone pine called Methuselah is found to be over 4,700 years old"],["2016","Scientists estimate that Greenland sharks live for centuries"]],
s2:[["Alternating generations","Ferns and mosses have life cycles with two forms that take turns. A fern plant releases spores, which grow into tiny heart-shaped plants that make eggs and sperm, which then grow into a new fern."],["Why cycles matter","Knowing an animal’s life cycle helps protect it. For example, protecting sea turtles means protecting both the beaches where they nest and the seas where they feed."]],
sp:["Sea turtles in the Philippines","Female sea turtles return to the beaches where they hatched, sometimes decades later, to lay their eggs. Turtle Islands in Tawi-Tawi is one of the most important green sea turtle nesting sites in Southeast Asia."],
g:[["Life cycle","The stages of a living thing’s life"],["Metamorphosis","A dramatic change in body form"],["Nymph","A young insect that resembles the adult"]],
f:["Periodical cicadas spend 13 or 17 years underground.","Some adult mayflies live less than a day.","The immortal jellyfish can turn itself back into a younger stage."],
fun:"The ‘immortal jellyfish’ can turn itself back into a young polyp and start its life over again."});

ADDART({id:"celldivision",t:"Cell Division",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🧫",w:"Cell division",r:["cell","dna","mendel","cancer","lifecycles"],
p:[["One becomes two","Cell division is how one cell splits into two new cells."],["Growth and repair","It lets living things grow, heal wounds and replace worn-out cells."],["Two kinds","Mitosis makes body cells, and meiosis makes egg and sperm cells."]],
l:"Cell division is the process by which a parent cell divides into two or more daughter cells. It is how a single fertilized egg grows into a human body of tens of trillions of cells, and how the body replaces skin, blood and gut cells every day. Before dividing, a cell copies its DNA so that each new cell gets a full set of instructions. In mitosis, one cell becomes two identical cells. In meiosis, used to make eggs and sperm, one cell divides twice to make four cells, each with half the usual number of chromosomes. Bacteria divide by a simpler process called binary fission.",
s:[["Mitosis","Mitosis has several stages. In prophase, the chromosomes coil up and become visible. In metaphase, they line up in the middle of the cell. In anaphase, the copies are pulled to opposite ends. In telophase, new nuclei form, and then the cell splits in two, a step called cytokinesis."],["Meiosis","Meiosis makes reproductive cells. It mixes up genes from the two parents and halves the number of chromosomes, so when an egg and sperm join, the new cell has a full set. This mixing is one reason brothers and sisters look different from each other."],["How often cells divide","Some cells, like those lining the gut, are replaced every few days. Others, like most nerve cells, rarely divide after birth. In 1961, Leonard Hayflick found that normal human cells grown in a laboratory divide only about 40 to 60 times before stopping."],["When division goes wrong","Cells normally divide only when the body needs them. If mutations damage the controls, cells can divide out of control and form a tumor. This is how cancer begins, and many cancer treatments work by stopping cells from dividing."]],
k:[["Main types","Mitosis and meiosis"],["Bacteria divide by","Binary fission"],["Cells in a human body","About 37 trillion (estimate)"],["Hayflick limit","About 40–60 divisions"],["Named","Mitosis, by Walther Flemming (1882)"]],
d:[["1855","Rudolf Virchow states that every cell comes from another cell"],["1882","Walther Flemming describes and names mitosis"],["1951","HeLa cells, the first human cells to keep dividing in a laboratory, are grown"],["1961","Leonard Hayflick discovers the limit on cell divisions"],["2001","A Nobel Prize honors discoveries about how cell division is controlled"]],
s2:[["HeLa cells","In 1951, cells taken from Henrietta Lacks, a patient with cervical cancer, kept dividing in a laboratory. They were used to develop the polio vaccine and in countless experiments, but she and her family were not asked for permission, raising important questions about medical ethics."],["Stem cells","Stem cells can divide to make more stem cells or to become specialized cells, such as muscle or blood. Scientists hope to use them to repair damaged organs."]],
sp:["Your body’s daily renewal","Your body makes billions of new blood cells every day through cell division in the bone marrow."],
g:[["Mitosis","Cell division that makes two identical cells"],["Meiosis","Cell division that makes eggs and sperm"],["Chromosome","A packaged strand of DNA"]],
f:["Your body makes billions of new blood cells every day.","Bacteria can divide as often as every 20 minutes.","Most nerve cells in the brain stop dividing after birth."],
fun:"Some bacteria can divide every 20 minutes, so one cell could become millions in a few hours."});

;
/* data/life3.js */
// Ecosystems, conservation and forests (27 November 2026). Figures that change were checked by web search on this date.
ADDART({id:"carboncycle",t:"The Carbon Cycle",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"♻️",w:"Carbon cycle",r:["photo","climchange","nitrogencycle","plankton","fossilfuels","peatlands"],
p:[["Carbon on the move","Carbon constantly moves between the air, living things, oceans and rocks."],["Life is made of carbon","Every living thing is built from carbon compounds."],["Out of balance","Burning fossil fuels adds carbon to the air faster than nature removes it."]],
l:"The carbon cycle is the movement of carbon atoms between the atmosphere, oceans, living things, soils and rocks. Plants, algae and some bacteria take carbon dioxide from the air and turn it into sugars by photosynthesis. Animals eat plants, and living things release carbon dioxide back into the air when they breathe or decay. Oceans absorb and release huge amounts of carbon, and over millions of years, carbon is stored in rocks, coal, oil and natural gas. Since the Industrial Revolution, burning fossil fuels and clearing forests have added carbon dioxide to the air faster than plants and oceans can remove it, warming the planet.",
s:[["The fast cycle","Carbon moves quickly between the air and living things. Each year, plants take in enormous amounts of carbon dioxide in spring and summer and release it as leaves fall and decay. Measurements in Hawai‘i show the level of carbon dioxide rising and falling each year with the seasons of the Northern Hemisphere’s forests."],["The slow cycle","Over millions of years, carbon moves between rocks, oceans and the air. Shells of sea creatures settle on the seafloor and become limestone. Ancient plants buried in swamps became coal, and tiny sea organisms became oil and gas. Volcanoes release carbon from deep inside the Earth."],["Oceans and forests","The oceans hold about 50 times more carbon than the atmosphere. They absorb roughly a quarter of the carbon dioxide released by human activities, and forests and other land ecosystems absorb roughly another quarter to a third. But absorbing carbon dioxide makes seawater more acidic, harming corals and shellfish."],["The Keeling Curve","In 1958, the scientist Charles David Keeling began measuring carbon dioxide on Mauna Loa in Hawai‘i. The level was about 315 parts per million. Before the Industrial Revolution it was about 280. In May 2026, the yearly peak reached about 432 parts per million, the highest in millions of years."]],
k:[["Main stores","Atmosphere, oceans, living things, soils, rocks"],["Taken in by","Photosynthesis"],["Released by","Breathing, decay, fires, burning fuels"],["Before industry","About 280 ppm of CO₂"],["Peak in May 2026","About 432 ppm (Mauna Loa)"]],
d:[["1896","Svante Arrhenius calculates that more carbon dioxide would warm the Earth"],["1938","Guy Callendar links rising carbon dioxide to warming"],["1958","Charles David Keeling begins measurements on Mauna Loa"],["2013","Carbon dioxide passes 400 parts per million at Mauna Loa"],["2026","The yearly peak reaches about 432 parts per million"]],
s2:[["Blue carbon","Mangroves, seagrass meadows and salt marshes store large amounts of carbon in their soils. Protecting and restoring them, including the mangrove forests of the Philippines, helps keep carbon out of the air."],["Removing carbon","Besides cutting emissions, people are trying to remove carbon dioxide from the air by planting forests, improving soils and building machines that capture carbon, though these methods are still small compared with emissions."]],
sp:["Breathing with the seasons","The Keeling Curve rises and falls each year like a slow breath: carbon dioxide drops in the northern summer, when forests are green, and rises in winter."],
g:[["Carbon cycle","The movement of carbon through Earth’s systems"],["ppm","Parts per million, a measure of concentration"],["Ocean acidification","Seawater becoming more acidic as it absorbs CO₂"]],
f:["The oceans hold about 50 times as much carbon as the air.","Carbon dioxide in the air has risen by more than half since before the Industrial Revolution.","Coal is made from ancient plants that grew in swamps."],
fun:"The carbon atoms in your body may once have been part of a dinosaur, a tree or the air over an ancient ocean."});

ADDART({id:"decomposers",t:"Decomposers",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🍂",w:"Decomposer",r:["fungi","microbe","soil","foodchains","eco","slimemolds","yeast"],
p:[["Nature’s recyclers","Decomposers break down dead plants and animals."],["Returning nutrients","They return nutrients to the soil so new plants can grow."],["Without them","Without decomposers, the world would be buried in dead material."]],
l:"Decomposers are living things that break down dead plants, animals and waste into simple substances. Bacteria and fungi are the main decomposers, releasing chemicals that digest dead material and absorbing the nutrients. They are helped by detritivores, animals such as earthworms, millipedes, woodlice, termites and dung beetles, which eat and shred dead matter. Decomposition returns carbon, nitrogen and other nutrients to the soil, water and air, where plants can use them again. Without decomposers, nutrients would stay locked up in dead things, and life could not continue.",
s:[["Fungi and bacteria","Fungi spread thread-like hyphae through dead wood and leaves, releasing enzymes that break down tough materials. Some fungi are among the few organisms able to digest lignin, the substance that makes wood hard. Bacteria break down softer materials and work fast in warm, moist places."],["Detritivores","Earthworms swallow soil and dead leaves, mixing and enriching the soil. Charles Darwin studied them for decades and published a book about them in 1881. Termites digest wood with the help of microbes in their guts. Dung beetles roll and bury animal dung, recycling it and feeding their young."],["The speed of decay","Decay is fastest when it is warm and damp. In a tropical rainforest, a fallen leaf may disappear within weeks, while in cold or dry places it can take years. In waterlogged, airless places like peat bogs, decay almost stops, which is why bog bodies thousands of years old can survive."],["Scavengers","Larger animals such as vultures, hyenas, crabs and some beetles feed on dead animals, speeding up decay. Vultures can eat rotten meat that would sicken other animals, helping to stop the spread of disease."]],
k:[["Main decomposers","Bacteria and fungi"],["Detritivores","Earthworms, millipedes, woodlice, termites, dung beetles"],["Job","Recycle nutrients"],["Fastest decay","Warm, wet places"],["Slowest decay","Cold, dry or waterlogged places"]],
d:[["1881","Charles Darwin publishes his book on earthworms"],["1965","Australia begins importing dung beetles to clear cattle dung"],["1981","The first ‘body farm’ for studying human decomposition opens in Tennessee"],["2012","A study links the rise of wood-rotting fungi to the end of the coal age, an idea still debated"]],
s2:[["Australia’s dung problem","Australia’s native dung beetles were used to the small, dry droppings of kangaroos and could not cope with cow pats. From 1965 to 1985, scientists brought in dozens of dung beetle species from Africa and Europe to bury the dung and reduce flies."],["Composting","Gardeners and farmers use decomposers to turn food scraps and garden waste into compost, a rich soil improver. Worm farms, or vermicomposting, are popular in the Philippines for making organic fertilizer."]],
sp:["Termite farmers","Some termites grow gardens of fungus inside their mounds. The fungus breaks down plant material that the termites collect, and the termites eat the fungus."],
g:[["Decomposer","A living thing that breaks down dead material"],["Detritivore","An animal that eats dead plant and animal matter"],["Compost","Decomposed material used to enrich soil"]],
f:["Darwin spent decades studying earthworms.","Some termites farm fungus inside their mounds.","Australia imported dung beetles to deal with cattle dung."],
fun:"Australia imported dozens of species of dung beetles to clean up the cow pats that its own beetles could not handle."});

ADDART({id:"endangered",t:"Endangered Species",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦏",w:"Endangered species",r:["extinction","conservation","tamaraw","eagle","biodivloss","dodo"],
p:[["At risk of disappearing","Endangered species are in danger of dying out completely."],["The Red List","The IUCN Red List tracks the risk of extinction for species worldwide."],["Comebacks are possible","Protection has helped some species recover."]],
l:"An endangered species is a kind of plant or animal at serious risk of extinction. The main global record is the IUCN Red List of Threatened Species, run by the International Union for Conservation of Nature since 1964. It places species in categories from Least Concern to Extinct. In its July 2026 update, the Red List included 175,909 species, of which 49,505 were threatened with extinction. The biggest threats are habitat loss, overhunting and overfishing, pollution, invasive species, disease and climate change. Laws, protected areas, breeding programs and international agreements help protect species at risk.",
s:[["The Red List categories","Species are placed in categories: Least Concern, Near Threatened, Vulnerable, Endangered, Critically Endangered, Extinct in the Wild and Extinct. ‘Threatened’ means Vulnerable, Endangered or Critically Endangered. The decision is based on evidence such as population size, how fast numbers are falling and how small the range is."],["Who is at risk","According to the Red List, more than 40 percent of amphibians, about a quarter of mammals, more than a third of reef-building corals and about one in nine bird species are threatened. Many plants, insects and fungi have not yet been assessed, so the true number is higher."],["Protecting species","International trade in endangered species is controlled by CITES, an agreement that took effect in 1975. Many countries have their own laws, such as the United States Endangered Species Act of 1973 and the Philippines’ Wildlife Resources Conservation and Protection Act of 2001."],["Success stories","The American bald eagle recovered after the pesticide DDT was banned and was removed from the US endangered list in 2007. The giant panda was moved from Endangered to Vulnerable in 2016, and many humpback whale populations have grown since commercial whaling was banned."]],
k:[["Main record","IUCN Red List (since 1964)"],["Species assessed (July 2026)","175,909"],["Threatened (July 2026)","49,505"],["Trade agreement","CITES (in force 1975)"],["Philippine law","Wildlife Act, RA 9147 (2001)"]],
d:[["1964","The IUCN Red List is first published"],["1973","The United States passes the Endangered Species Act"],["1975","CITES enters into force"],["2001","The Philippines passes the Wildlife Resources Conservation and Protection Act"],["2016","The giant panda is moved from Endangered to Vulnerable"],["2026","The Red List reaches 175,909 species assessed"]],
s2:[["Endangered in the Philippines","The Philippines has many species found nowhere else, and many are threatened. The Philippine eagle and the Philippine cockatoo are critically endangered. In April 2026, rangers counted 425 tamaraws in Mounts Iglit-Baco Natural Park in Mindoro, up from 380 the year before."],["Why every species matters","Each species plays a part in its ecosystem. Losing one can affect many others, such as the plants that rely on an animal to spread their seeds. Species also have value in themselves and are part of the world’s natural heritage."]],
sp:["Counting tamaraws","Every year, teams spread out across the grasslands of Mounts Iglit-Baco to count tamaraws at the same time from hilltop vantage points, so that no animal is counted twice."],
g:[["Endangered","At high risk of extinction"],["Threatened","Vulnerable, Endangered or Critically Endangered"],["CITES","A treaty that controls trade in wild species"]],
f:["More than 40 percent of amphibian species are threatened.","The bald eagle was removed from the US endangered list in 2007.","The Red List assessed its first 1,000 fungi in 2025."],
fun:"Some species thought extinct have been rediscovered, such as the coelacanth fish in 1938, which was known only from fossils."});

ADDART({id:"keystone",t:"Keystone Species",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦦",w:"Keystone species",r:["eco","foodchains","wolf","kelp","rewilding","animalhomes","figtrees"],
p:[["Holding it all together","A keystone species has an unusually large effect on its ecosystem."],["Like an arch’s keystone","Remove it, and the whole system can change or collapse."],["Big effects","Sea otters, wolves, elephants and fig trees are keystone species."]],
l:"A keystone species is a plant or animal that has a much bigger effect on its ecosystem than its numbers would suggest. The name comes from the keystone at the top of a stone arch, which holds the other stones in place. In 1969, the American ecologist Robert Paine coined the term after removing ochre sea stars from tide pools in Washington State: without the sea stars, mussels took over and many other species disappeared. Keystone species include predators that control prey, such as wolves and sea otters, and species that create habitats or feed many others, such as beavers, elephants and fig trees.",
s:[["Predators","Predators keep plant-eaters in check. Sea otters eat sea urchins; where otters were hunted out, urchins multiplied and ate whole kelp forests. When otters returned, the kelp forests recovered, along with the fish and other animals that live in them."],["Ecosystem engineers","Some keystone species shape their surroundings. Beavers build dams that create wetlands. African elephants knock down trees and keep savannas open for grazing animals. Corals build reefs that shelter a quarter of all marine species."],["Food for many","Some species provide food when little else is available. Fig trees fruit at different times through the year, feeding monkeys, birds, bats and insects in tropical forests. Salmon carry nutrients from the ocean into rivers and forests, feeding bears, eagles and even trees."],["Wolves of Yellowstone","After wolves were reintroduced to Yellowstone National Park in 1995 and 1996, elk changed where and how they grazed. Scientists have linked the wolves’ return to changes in willows, aspens and other species, although how large these effects are is still debated."]],
k:[["Coined by","Robert Paine (1969)"],["Classic example","Ochre sea star"],["Predator examples","Sea otters, wolves, sharks"],["Engineer examples","Beavers, elephants, corals"],["Food-provider examples","Fig trees, salmon"]],
d:[["1963","Robert Paine begins removing sea stars from tide pools"],["1969","Paine introduces the term ‘keystone species’"],["1974","Studies show sea otters protect kelp forests"],["1995","Wolves are reintroduced to Yellowstone"]],
s2:[["Not just big animals","Keystone species can be small. Prairie dogs dig burrows used by many animals, and some bees and bats are the main pollinators of important plants."],["Why it matters","Protecting a keystone species helps protect many others. Conservationists often focus on them to keep whole ecosystems healthy."]],
sp:["Fruit bats of the Philippines","Fruit bats, including the giant golden-crowned flying fox found only in the Philippines, spread seeds and pollinate flowers across forests, helping keep them healthy."],
g:[["Keystone species","A species with an unusually large effect on its ecosystem"],["Ecosystem engineer","A species that physically changes its habitat"],["Trophic cascade","Changes that ripple down a food chain"]],
f:["Removing one sea star species let mussels take over a whole shore.","Sea otters protect kelp forests by eating sea urchins.","Fig trees feed animals when little other fruit is available."],
fun:"The idea of keystone species began with an ecologist pulling sea stars off rocks and tossing them into the ocean."});

ADDART({id:"rewilding",t:"Rewilding",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐺",w:"Rewilding (conservation biology)",r:["keystone","conservation","wolf","protectedareas","yellowstone"],
p:[["Letting nature return","Rewilding means restoring natural processes and bringing back missing species."],["Less management","Instead of tightly controlling land, rewilding lets nature take the lead."],["Bringing animals home","Wolves, beavers, bison and jaguars have been returned to places they once lived."]],
l:"Rewilding is a kind of conservation that aims to restore ecosystems by letting natural processes take over and bringing back species that have disappeared. Rather than managing every detail, rewilding projects remove barriers such as dams and fences, let forests regrow, and reintroduce animals like wolves, beavers, bison and wild horses that shape their surroundings. Famous examples include the return of wolves to Yellowstone National Park in the United States, European bison to forests in Poland and other countries, and jaguars to the Iberá wetlands of Argentina. Rewilding can bring back wildlife, store carbon and reduce flooding, but it can also cause conflict with farmers.",
s:[["Wolves in Yellowstone","Gray wolves had been wiped out from Yellowstone by the 1920s. In 1995 and 1996, 31 wolves from Canada were released in the park. Their return changed how elk behaved and has been linked to changes in plants, rivers and other animals, though scientists still debate the size of the effects."],["Bison in Europe","European bison, or wisent, were hunted to extinction in the wild by 1927. A few survived in zoos, and from 1952 they were returned to the Białowieża Forest on the Poland–Belarus border. Today thousands live wild across several European countries."],["Beavers and rivers","Beavers were hunted out of Britain centuries ago. They have returned to rivers in Scotland and England, where their dams slow floods, filter water and create wetlands. In 2025, England approved licensed releases of beavers into the wild."],["Big projects","In Argentina’s Iberá wetlands, conservationists have reintroduced giant anteaters, pampas deer, green-winged macaws and, since 2021, jaguars. At the Knepp Estate in England, a former farm left to rewild since 2001 now hosts rare birds, butterflies and white storks."]],
k:[["Goal","Restore natural processes and missing species"],["Famous reintroduction","Wolves in Yellowstone (1995–1996)"],["European example","European bison in Białowieża Forest"],["South American example","Jaguars in Iberá, Argentina"],["Challenges","Conflicts with farmers, disease, cost"]],
d:[["1952","European bison are returned to Białowieża Forest"],["1995","Wolves are reintroduced to Yellowstone"],["2001","The Knepp Estate in England begins rewilding"],["2021","Jaguars are released in the Iberá wetlands of Argentina"],["2025","England approves licensed wild releases of beavers"]],
s2:[["Debates","Some people worry that reintroduced predators will kill livestock, or that rewilding takes land away from farming. Projects work best when local communities are involved and benefit, for example from tourism."],["Rewilding the seas","Ocean rewilding includes protecting areas from fishing so fish and corals can recover, and restoring oyster reefs, seagrass meadows and kelp forests."]],
sp:["Restoring forests in the Philippines","Philippine projects that replant native trees, rather than fast-growing foreign species, aim to rebuild forests that support native wildlife such as hornbills and the Philippine eagle."],
g:[["Rewilding","Restoring ecosystems by letting nature take the lead"],["Reintroduction","Returning a species to a place it once lived"],["Wisent","The European bison"]],
f:["European bison were extinct in the wild by 1927.","Thirty-one wolves were brought to Yellowstone in 1995 and 1996.","White storks now nest at England’s Knepp Estate."],
fun:"Every European bison alive today descends from just 12 animals that survived in zoos."});

ADDART({id:"protectedareas",t:"National Parks and Protected Areas",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🏞️",w:"Protected area",r:["yellowstone","conservation","endangered","rewilding","coral"],
p:[["Places set aside for nature","Protected areas are lands and waters managed to conserve nature."],["From Yellowstone to the sea","The idea began with national parks and now includes marine reserves."],["The 30 by 30 goal","Countries have agreed to protect 30 percent of land and sea by 2030."]],
l:"A protected area is a clearly defined space of land or water that is managed to conserve nature over the long term. Protected areas include national parks, wildlife sanctuaries, nature reserves and marine protected areas. Yellowstone, created in the United States in 1872, is often called the world’s first national park. According to the Protected Planet Report 2024, protected and conserved areas covered 17.6 percent of the world’s land and inland waters and 8.4 percent of its oceans and coasts. In 2022, nearly 200 countries agreed to protect 30 percent of the planet’s land and sea by 2030, a target known as ‘30 by 30’.",
s:[["Kinds of protected areas","The IUCN groups protected areas into categories, from strict nature reserves where few people may enter, to national parks for nature and visitors, to areas where people live and use resources sustainably. Some areas are managed by Indigenous peoples and local communities."],["The largest","The Northeast Greenland National Park, created in 1974, is the largest national park on land, covering about 972,000 square kilometers. Some marine protected areas are even bigger, such as Papahānaumokuākea in the Pacific Ocean around the northwestern Hawaiian Islands."],["Protection in the sea","Marine protected areas can help fish populations recover, protect coral reefs and support fishing nearby. ‘No-take’ zones, where fishing is banned, often see fish grow larger and more numerous, and fish spill over into surrounding waters."],["30 by 30","The Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework, adopted in December 2022, set the goal of conserving 30 percent of land and sea by 2030. Reaching it would mean almost doubling the protected share of land and more than tripling protection in the ocean."]],
k:[["First national park","Yellowstone, 1872"],["Land protected (2024 report)","17.6%"],["Ocean protected (2024 report)","8.4%"],["Global target","30% by 2030"],["Largest national park","Northeast Greenland, about 972,000 km²"]],
d:[["1872","Yellowstone becomes the first national park"],["1974","Northeast Greenland National Park is created"],["1992","The Philippines passes the National Integrated Protected Areas System Act"],["2018","The Philippines expands its protected areas with the E-NIPAS Act"],["2022","Countries adopt the 30 by 30 target"],["2024","The Protected Planet Report finds 17.6% of land protected"]],
s2:[["Protected areas of the Philippines","The Philippines protects its natural areas under the NIPAS Act of 1992 and the Expanded NIPAS Act of 2018. Its protected areas include Tubbataha Reefs Natural Park, Puerto Princesa Subterranean River National Park, Mount Apo Natural Park and Mounts Iglit-Baco Natural Park, home of the tamaraw."],["Paper parks","Some protected areas exist on maps but lack rangers, money or enforcement, so illegal logging, hunting or fishing continue. Conservationists call these ‘paper parks’ and say quality matters as much as size."]],
sp:["Tubbataha","Tubbataha Reefs Natural Park in the Sulu Sea is a no-take marine park and a UNESCO World Heritage Site. Rangers live on a small station in the middle of the sea to guard the reefs."],
g:[["Protected area","A place managed to conserve nature"],["Marine protected area","A protected area in the sea"],["No-take zone","An area where fishing is not allowed"]],
f:["Yellowstone was created in 1872.","Northeast Greenland National Park is larger than most countries.","Rangers at Tubbataha live on a station in the middle of the Sulu Sea."],
fun:"Northeast Greenland National Park is bigger than all but about 30 countries in the world."});

ADDART({id:"rainforests",t:"Tropical Rainforests",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🌴",w:"Tropical rainforest",r:["amazon","trees","deforestation","giantrees","phbio","carboncycle","treerings","orangutans","titanoboa","cloudforests"],
p:[["Forests of rain","Tropical rainforests grow near the equator, where it is warm and wet all year."],["Treasure houses of life","They are home to more kinds of plants and animals than any other land habitat."],["Under threat","Logging, farming and fires are destroying rainforests."]],
l:"Tropical rainforests are dense forests that grow near the equator, where temperatures are warm all year and rain is heavy, often more than 2,000 millimeters a year. They cover only a small part of Earth’s land, yet scientists estimate that they hold more than half of all land plant and animal species. The largest are in the Amazon Basin of South America, the Congo Basin of Central Africa and Southeast Asia, including the Philippines. Rainforests store vast amounts of carbon, help make rain and provide food, medicines and homes for millions of people, but large areas are cut down each year.",
s:[["Layers of the forest","A rainforest has layers. The tallest trees, called emergents, rise above the rest, sometimes over 60 meters tall. Below is the canopy, a thick roof of leaves where most animals live. Under it is the understory of small trees and shrubs, and at the bottom is the dark forest floor, which receives very little sunlight."],["Life everywhere","A single hectare of rainforest can contain hundreds of tree species. Plants grow on other plants: orchids, ferns and bromeliads perch on branches, and vines climb toward the light. Animals include monkeys, sloths, jaguars, hornbills, frogs, snakes and countless insects."],["Poor soils","Rainforest soils are usually thin and poor, because heavy rain washes nutrients away and decomposers recycle dead material very quickly. Most nutrients are held in the living plants, so when forests are cleared, the land often becomes infertile within a few years."],["Rainmakers","Rainforest trees release huge amounts of water vapor from their leaves. In the Amazon, this moisture forms clouds and rain that move across the continent, watering farms and cities far away."]],
k:[["Location","Near the equator"],["Rainfall","Often more than 2,000 mm a year"],["Largest","Amazon rainforest"],["Layers","Emergent, canopy, understory, forest floor"],["Species","More than half of land species (estimate)"]],
d:[["1960s","Large-scale clearing of rainforests accelerates"],["1988","The murder of Brazilian rubber tapper Chico Mendes draws world attention to the Amazon"],["2004","Brazil launches a plan that later cuts Amazon deforestation sharply"],["2021","More than 100 countries pledge to end deforestation by 2030"]],
s2:[["Rainforests of the Philippines","The Philippines was once mostly covered in rainforest, dominated by tall dipterocarp trees. Much was logged in the 20th century, but large forests survive in places such as the Sierra Madre, Palawan and Mindanao, home to the Philippine eagle and many species found nowhere else."],["Temperate rainforests","Not all rainforests are tropical. Cool, wet temperate rainforests grow along coasts such as the Pacific Northwest of North America, southern Chile and New Zealand."]],
sp:["Life in the canopy","Many rainforest animals spend their whole lives in the canopy and never touch the ground. Scientists use towers, cranes and rope walkways to study this hidden world."],
g:[["Canopy","The dense upper layer of leaves in a forest"],["Emergent","A very tall tree rising above the canopy"],["Epiphyte","A plant that grows on another plant"]],
f:["Some rainforest animals never touch the ground.","Rainforest soils are usually poor in nutrients.","Rainforest trees help make their own rain."],
fun:"Less than 2 percent of the sunlight that hits a rainforest canopy reaches the forest floor."});

ADDART({id:"figtrees",t:"Fig Trees",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🌳",w:"Ficus",r:["trees","keystone","symbiosis","rainforests","fruits"],
p:[["An amazing family","Fig trees belong to the genus Ficus, with about 850 species."],["A partnership with wasps","Every fig species depends on tiny wasps to pollinate its hidden flowers."],["Food for the forest","Figs feed more kinds of animals than almost any other fruit."]],
l:"Fig trees are plants of the genus Ficus, which includes about 850 species of trees, shrubs and vines, most of them in the tropics. They include the edible fig, the banyan, the sacred bodhi tree, the rubber plant and the strangler figs that grow around other trees. Figs have a remarkable partnership with tiny fig wasps: the fig’s flowers are hidden inside the fruit-like structure, and only a female wasp that crawls inside can pollinate them. Because figs fruit all year round, they feed monkeys, birds, bats and insects when other fruit is scarce, making them keystone species in tropical forests.",
s:[["Flowers on the inside","What we call a fig is actually a hollow structure lined with hundreds of tiny flowers. A female fig wasp enters through a narrow opening, often losing her wings, and lays eggs inside while spreading pollen. Her young hatch inside the fig, mate, and the females fly off carrying pollen to another fig."],["Strangler figs","Strangler figs begin life as seeds dropped by birds high on another tree. Their roots grow down to the ground and wrap around the host tree. Over many years, the host may die and rot away, leaving a hollow lattice of fig roots standing on its own."],["Banyans","Banyan trees send down roots from their branches that thicken into new trunks. One banyan can spread into what looks like a small forest. The Thimmamma Marrimanu banyan in India covers about 19,000 square meters."],["Sacred and useful","The Buddha is said to have reached enlightenment under a bodhi tree, a kind of fig, and fig trees are sacred in many cultures. The edible fig was one of the first plants grown by people: figs found in the Jordan Valley may have been cultivated over 11,000 years ago."]],
k:[["Genus","Ficus"],["Species","About 850"],["Pollinators","Fig wasps"],["Famous kinds","Edible fig, banyan, bodhi tree, strangler fig"],["Role","Keystone food source in tropical forests"]],
d:[["c.9400 BC","Figs are grown in the Jordan Valley"],["c.500 BC","The Buddha is said to reach enlightenment under a bodhi tree"],["1989","The Thimmamma Marrimanu banyan enters Guinness World Records"],["2006","Scientists report the ancient cultivated figs from Gilgal"]],
s2:[["Balete in the Philippines","In the Philippines, strangler figs are called balete. Many Filipinos believe balete trees are homes of spirits and creatures from folklore, such as the kapre and engkanto, and some people avoid cutting them. The hollow trunks of old balete can be large enough to climb inside."],["Invasive figs","When fig trees are planted far from home without their wasps, they cannot make seeds. But in places like Florida, where wasps arrived too, some figs have spread as invasive plants."]],
sp:["A partnership 75 million years old","Fig trees and fig wasps have depended on each other for tens of millions of years. Most fig species have their own special wasp, and neither can survive without the other."],
g:[["Ficus","The genus that includes all fig trees"],["Fig wasp","A tiny wasp that pollinates figs"],["Strangler fig","A fig that grows around a host tree"]],
f:["A fig is really a cluster of hidden flowers.","Banyan trees grow new trunks from their branches.","Each fig species usually has its own kind of wasp."],
fun:"Every fig species relies on its own tiny wasp, and the wasp relies on the fig: neither can survive without the other."});

ADDART({id:"treerings",t:"Tree Rings",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪵",w:"Dendrochronology",r:["trees","carbondating","climate","giantrees","redwoods"],
p:[["A tree’s diary","Many trees add a ring of wood every year."],["Counting the years","Counting rings tells a tree’s age, and their widths record the weather."],["Clocks for history","Tree rings help date ancient buildings and track past climates."]],
l:"Tree rings are the layers of wood that many trees add each year as they grow. In places with distinct seasons, a tree grows quickly in spring, making light, wide cells, and slowly in late summer, making darker, denser wood. Each light and dark pair makes one ring. Counting rings reveals a tree’s age, and their widths record whether a year was wet or dry, warm or cold. The science of dating with tree rings is called dendrochronology. By matching patterns between living trees, old timbers and ancient wood, scientists have built records stretching back more than 12,000 years.",
s:[["How dating works","Because weather affects all trees in a region in similar ways, they share patterns of wide and narrow rings. By overlapping the patterns from living trees with those in older wood, such as beams in old buildings or logs preserved in bogs, scientists build long continuous timelines. A piece of wood can then be dated to the exact year it grew."],["The founder","The American astronomer Andrew Ellicott Douglass began studying tree rings in Arizona in the early 1900s, hoping to link them with sunspots. He used them to date ancient Pueblo ruins in the American Southwest and founded the Laboratory of Tree-Ring Research in 1937."],["Ancient trees","In 1957, Edmund Schulman found that a bristlecone pine in California, later named Methuselah, was more than 4,700 years old. Scientists take thin cores with a hollow drill, so they can count rings without harming the tree."],["Records of disaster","Narrow or damaged rings record droughts, frosts, fires and insect attacks. Large volcanic eruptions cool the climate and leave frost rings. A sudden jump in carbon-14 in rings from the year 774–775, discovered in 2012, revealed a massive burst of radiation from space."]],
k:[["Science","Dendrochronology"],["Founder","Andrew Ellicott Douglass"],["Longest record","More than 12,000 years (Europe)"],["Oldest known tree","Bristlecone pines, nearly 5,000 years"],["Tool","Increment borer"]],
d:[["1904","Andrew Ellicott Douglass begins tree-ring studies"],["1937","The Laboratory of Tree-Ring Research is founded in Arizona"],["1957","Methuselah is found to be over 4,700 years old"],["1964","A bristlecone pine called Prometheus is cut down and found to be about 4,900 years old"],["2012","Tree rings reveal a radiation burst in AD 774–775"]],
s2:[["Checking radiocarbon dates","Radiocarbon dating assumes the amount of carbon-14 in the air has stayed the same, but it has varied. Tree rings, whose exact years are known, are used to calibrate radiocarbon dates and make them accurate."],["Rings in the tropics","Many tropical trees, including most in the Philippines, grow all year and do not form clear yearly rings. Scientists use other methods, such as chemical changes in the wood, to study their ages and past climates."]],
sp:["The tree that was cut down","In 1964, a researcher cut down a bristlecone pine in Nevada to count its rings and found it was about 4,900 years old, then the oldest known tree. The loss led to stronger protection for ancient trees."],
g:[["Dendrochronology","Dating events using tree rings"],["Increment borer","A hollow drill for taking tree cores"],["Frost ring","A damaged ring caused by freezing"]],
f:["A tree core can be taken without harming the tree.","Tree rings revealed a burst of radiation from space in AD 774–775.","Many tropical trees do not have clear yearly rings."],
fun:"Tree rings can show the exact year a beam in an old building was cut, even centuries later."});

;
/* data/agencies1.js */
// Space agencies (27 November 2026). Figures that change were checked by web search on this date.
ADDART({id:"nasa",t:"NASA",c:"Space",i:"🚀",w:"NASA",r:["spaceagencies","apollo","artemis","iss","jwst","esa","roscosmos","cnsa","csa"],
p:[["America’s space agency","NASA is the United States government agency for space exploration and aeronautics."],["From the Moon to Mars","It landed the first people on the Moon and has sent robots to every planet."],["Back to the Moon","Its Artemis program flew astronauts around the Moon in 2026."]],
l:"NASA, the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, is the agency of the United States government responsible for space exploration, space science and aeronautics research. It was created on July 29, 1958, in response to the Soviet Union’s launch of Sputnik, and began operating on October 1, 1958. NASA ran the Mercury, Gemini and Apollo programs, which landed 12 astronauts on the Moon between 1969 and 1972, and later built the Space Shuttle and led construction of the International Space Station. Its robotic missions have explored every planet, and its space telescopes, including Hubble and the James Webb Space Telescope, have transformed astronomy. In April 2026, its Artemis II mission carried four astronauts around the Moon.",
s:[["Beginnings","NASA grew out of an older agency, the National Advisory Committee for Aeronautics, founded in 1915 to research airplanes. After Sputnik in 1957, President Dwight D. Eisenhower signed a law creating NASA. In 1961, Alan Shepard became the first American in space, and President John F. Kennedy set the goal of landing a person on the Moon before the decade was out."],["The Apollo era","On July 20, 1969, Apollo 11 astronauts Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin walked on the Moon. Six Apollo missions landed between 1969 and 1972. At its peak in the mid-1960s, NASA’s budget was more than 4 percent of all US government spending, compared with less than half a percent today."],["Shuttles, stations and telescopes","The Space Shuttle flew 135 missions from 1981 to 2011, launching the Hubble Space Telescope and helping build the International Space Station. Two shuttles, Challenger in 1986 and Columbia in 2003, were lost with their crews. Since 2020, NASA has paid private companies, beginning with SpaceX, to carry astronauts to the station."],["Artemis","NASA’s Artemis program aims to return people to the Moon’s surface and prepare for Mars. Artemis I flew an uncrewed Orion capsule around the Moon in 2022. Artemis II launched on April 1, 2026, carrying Reid Wiseman, Victor Glover, Christina Koch and Canada’s Jeremy Hansen around the Moon. They traveled 252,756 miles from Earth, farther than any humans before, and splashed down on April 10."]],
k:[["Founded","July 29, 1958 (began work October 1, 1958)"],["Headquarters","Washington, D.C."],["Administrator","Jared Isaacman (since December 2025)"],["Famous programs","Apollo, Space Shuttle, Artemis"],["Famous telescopes","Hubble, James Webb"]],
d:[["1958","NASA is created"],["1969","Apollo 11 lands on the Moon"],["1981","The first Space Shuttle flies"],["1990","The Hubble Space Telescope is launched"],["2021","The James Webb Space Telescope is launched"],["2026","Artemis II carries four astronauts around the Moon"]],
s2:[["NASA science","NASA’s robots have landed on Mars, orbited Jupiter and Saturn, flown past Pluto and touched an asteroid. Voyager 1, launched in 1977, is the most distant human-made object. NASA satellites also study Earth’s climate, oceans and weather."],["Spinoffs","Technologies developed for NASA have found uses on Earth, including improvements to memory foam, scratch-resistant lenses, water filters and the tiny camera sensors used in phones."]],
sp:["Budget battles","NASA’s budget is set each year by the US Congress. In January 2026, Congress approved full funding for NASA for the 2026 fiscal year, rejecting a proposal to cut its science budget nearly in half."],
g:[["Aeronautics","The science of flight within the atmosphere"],["Orion","NASA’s crew capsule for deep-space missions"],["SLS","Space Launch System, NASA’s giant Moon rocket"]],
f:["NASA was created because of Sputnik.","Artemis II astronauts traveled farther from Earth than any people before.","Voyager 1 is the most distant spacecraft ever launched."],
fun:"In April 2026, the Artemis II crew broke the record for the farthest distance humans have traveled from Earth, set by Apollo 13 in 1970."});

ADDART({id:"esa",t:"The European Space Agency",c:"Space",i:"🇪🇺",w:"European Space Agency",r:["spaceagencies","ariane","rosetta","gaia","nasa"],
p:[["Europe in space","The European Space Agency (ESA) brings European countries together to explore space."],["Science and satellites","ESA builds science missions, rockets, weather and navigation satellites."],["A record budget","In 2025, its members agreed to the largest budget in its history."]],
l:"The European Space Agency, or ESA, is an intergovernmental organization of 23 member states that cooperate on space research, technology and exploration. Founded in 1975, it has its headquarters in Paris and its main launch site at Kourou in French Guiana, South America. ESA built the Ariane family of rockets, landed the Huygens probe on Saturn’s moon Titan, and put the Philae lander on a comet with the Rosetta mission. It runs the Copernicus Earth-observing satellites with the European Union and the Galileo navigation system. In November 2025, member states pledged a record €22.3 billion for its future programs.",
s:[["How ESA works","Each member country pays into ESA’s mandatory science and basic programs according to the size of its economy, and chooses which optional programs to join. Much of the money returns to companies in the paying countries as contracts. ESA is separate from the European Union, though the two work closely together, and some members, such as the United Kingdom, Norway and Switzerland, are not in the EU."],["Science missions","ESA’s Rosetta orbited a comet from 2014 to 2016, and its Philae lander made the first landing on a comet. Gaia mapped the positions of nearly two billion stars. The JUICE mission, launched in 2023, is on its way to Jupiter’s icy moons, and ESA provided key parts of the James Webb Space Telescope."],["Rockets","ESA developed the Ariane rockets, launched from Kourou. The new Ariane 6 made its first flight in July 2024. ESA also flies the smaller Vega rockets."],["Astronauts","ESA astronauts fly to the International Space Station on American and Russian spacecraft. ESA built the Columbus laboratory on the station and the service module that powers NASA’s Orion capsule, which carried the Artemis II crew around the Moon in 2026."]],
k:[["Founded","1975"],["Members","23 countries"],["Headquarters","Paris, France"],["Spaceport","Kourou, French Guiana"],["Director General","Josef Aschbacher"]],
d:[["1975","ESA is founded"],["1979","The first Ariane rocket is launched"],["2005","The Huygens probe lands on Titan"],["2014","Philae lands on a comet"],["2024","Ariane 6 makes its first flight"],["2025","Members pledge a record €22.3 billion"]],
s2:[["Watching Earth","ESA’s Sentinel satellites, part of the European Union’s Copernicus program, monitor forests, oceans, ice, air pollution and disasters, and their data are free for anyone to use."],["Galileo","Galileo is Europe’s own satellite navigation system, like GPS. Its signals are used by phones, cars and ships around the world, including in the Philippines."]],
sp:["A spaceport in the jungle","ESA launches from Kourou in French Guiana because it is close to the equator. Earth’s spin gives rockets an extra boost there, saving fuel."],
g:[["Intergovernmental","Run jointly by several governments"],["Copernicus","Europe’s Earth-observing satellite program"],["Galileo","Europe’s satellite navigation system"]],
f:["Philae made the first landing on a comet in 2014.","Gaia has mapped nearly two billion stars.","ESA built the module that powers NASA’s Orion spacecraft."],
fun:"ESA’s Philae lander bounced twice when it landed on a comet in 2014 before coming to rest in a shadowy spot."});

ADDART({id:"roscosmos",t:"Russia’s Space Program",c:"Space",i:"🛰️",w:"Roscosmos",r:["sputnik","gagarin","soyuz","mir","iss"],
p:[["First in space","The Soviet Union launched the first satellite and the first person into space."],["Roscosmos today","Russia’s space activities are run by the state corporation Roscosmos."],["Soyuz","Russia’s Soyuz spacecraft have carried astronauts for more than 50 years."]],
l:"Russia’s space program continues the work of the Soviet space program, which achieved many of history’s firsts: the first satellite, Sputnik 1, in 1957; the first person in space, Yuri Gagarin, in 1961; the first woman in space, Valentina Tereshkova, in 1963; and the first spacewalk, by Alexei Leonov, in 1965. Today Russian space activities are managed by Roscosmos, a state corporation. Russia launches crews on Soyuz spacecraft from the Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakhstan and runs the Russian part of the International Space Station. It plans to start building its own Russian Orbital Station in 2028.",
s:[["The Soviet space program","Led by the chief designer Sergei Korolev, whose identity was kept secret until his death in 1966, the Soviet program raced the United States. Soviet probes made the first landings on the Moon and Venus, and the Lunokhod rovers drove on the Moon in the 1970s. The Salyut stations and then Mir, which orbited from 1986 to 2001, made the Soviets leaders in long-duration spaceflight."],["Soyuz","The Soyuz spacecraft first flew in 1967 and, in updated versions, is still used today. After the Space Shuttle retired in 2011, Soyuz was the only way to reach the International Space Station until American commercial spacecraft began flying crews in 2020. Russia and the United States now swap seats so that each country has astronauts on the other’s spacecraft."],["Roscosmos","After the Soviet Union broke up in 1991, Russia formed a space agency, which became the state corporation Roscosmos in 2015. Its head since 2025 is Dmitry Bakanov. Russia builds the Soyuz, Proton and Angara rockets and is developing a new spaceport, Vostochny, in its Far East."],["Recent years","In 2023, Russia’s Luna 25 crashed while trying to land near the Moon’s south pole. Russia has said it will stay on the International Space Station until it is deorbited around 2030, and plans a smaller Russian Orbital Station that would begin assembly in 2028."]],
k:[["Agency","Roscosmos (state corporation)"],["Head","Dmitry Bakanov (since 2025)"],["Main spaceports","Baikonur (Kazakhstan), Vostochny, Plesetsk"],["Crew spacecraft","Soyuz"],["Famous firsts","First satellite, first person and first woman in space"]],
d:[["1957","Sputnik 1 becomes the first artificial satellite"],["1961","Yuri Gagarin becomes the first person in space"],["1965","Alexei Leonov makes the first spacewalk"],["1986","The Mir space station is launched"],["2015","Roscosmos becomes a state corporation"],["2023","Luna 25 crashes on the Moon"]],
s2:[["Baikonur","The Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakhstan, from which Sputnik and Gagarin were launched, is the world’s first and largest spaceport. Russia leases it from Kazakhstan."],["Space and politics","After Russia’s invasion of Ukraine in 2022, European and other partners ended many joint projects with Russia, such as launching satellites on Soyuz rockets and the ExoMars rover partnership. Cooperation on the space station continued."]],
sp:["Gagarin’s words","As his rocket lifted off on April 12, 1961, Yuri Gagarin called out ‘Poyekhali!’, meaning ‘Let’s go!’. The phrase is still famous in Russia."],
g:[["Cosmonaut","A Russian or Soviet astronaut"],["Cosmodrome","A Russian name for a spaceport"],["Soyuz","A Russian crew spacecraft and rocket"]],
f:["The Soyuz spacecraft first flew in 1967.","Baikonur is in Kazakhstan, not Russia.","The chief designer Sergei Korolev was kept secret during his life."],
fun:"Sergei Korolev, the genius behind Sputnik and Gagarin’s flight, was known in public only as the ‘Chief Designer’ until he died."});

ADDART({id:"cnsa",t:"China’s Space Program",c:"Space",i:"🇨🇳",w:"China National Space Administration",r:["tiangong","shenzhou","changeprogram","tianwen","spaceagencies"],
p:[["A rising space power","China has become one of the world’s leading space nations."],["Its own space station","China operates the Tiangong space station."],["Moon and Mars","Chinese robots have landed on the far side of the Moon and on Mars."]],
l:"China’s space program has grown rapidly to become one of the most active in the world. The China National Space Administration (CNSA) manages civil space projects, while crewed missions are run by the China Manned Space Agency. China launched its first satellite in 1970, sent its first astronaut, Yang Liwei, into space in 2003, and completed its Tiangong space station in 2022. Its Chang’e robots made the first landing on the far side of the Moon in 2019 and, in 2024, brought back the first samples from the far side. China’s Tianwen-1 put a rover on Mars in 2021. China plans to land astronauts on the Moon by 2030.",
s:[["Beginnings","China’s rocket program began in the 1950s under the scientist Qian Xuesen. Its first satellite, Dong Fang Hong 1, launched in 1970 and broadcast the song ‘The East Is Red’ from orbit. The Long March family of rockets has carried hundreds of missions since."],["Astronauts","China’s astronauts, sometimes called taikonauts, fly on Shenzhou spacecraft. Yang Liwei orbited Earth in 2003, making China the third country able to launch people into space on its own. Since 2021, Chinese crews have lived aboard the Tiangong station in rotations of about six months."],["The Moon","The Chang’e program, named after a moon goddess, sent orbiters, landers and rovers. Chang’e 4 landed on the far side in 2019, Chang’e 5 returned samples in 2020, and Chang’e 6 brought back the first far-side samples in 2024. China is building the Long March 10 rocket, the Mengzhou crew capsule and the Lanyue lander for crewed landings."],["Mars and beyond","Tianwen-1 reached Mars in 2021 and landed the Zhurong rover, making China the second country to operate a rover on Mars. Tianwen-2, launched in 2025, is on its way to collect samples from a near-Earth asteroid and then study a comet."]],
k:[["Civil space agency","China National Space Administration (founded 1993)"],["First satellite","Dong Fang Hong 1 (1970)"],["First astronaut","Yang Liwei (2003)"],["Space station","Tiangong (completed 2022)"],["Goal","Astronauts on the Moon by 2030"]],
d:[["1970","China launches its first satellite"],["2003","Yang Liwei becomes China’s first astronaut"],["2019","Chang’e 4 lands on the far side of the Moon"],["2021","The Zhurong rover lands on Mars"],["2022","The Tiangong station is completed"],["2024","Chang’e 6 returns samples from the Moon’s far side"]],
s2:[["International partners","Because of a US law passed in 2011, China is excluded from the International Space Station. China has invited other countries to fly experiments on Tiangong and is planning an International Lunar Research Station with partners including Russia."],["Commercial space","Many private Chinese companies now build rockets and satellites, and China is launching large satellite constellations to compete with services like Starlink."]],
sp:["Seen from the Philippines","Spent stages of Chinese Long March rockets have occasionally fallen into seas near the Philippines, and the Philippine Space Agency warns fishers and ships to stay away from the drop zones announced before launches."],
g:[["Taikonaut","A popular name for a Chinese astronaut"],["Shenzhou","China’s crew spacecraft"],["Long March","China’s family of rockets"]],
f:["China’s first satellite played the song ‘The East Is Red’ from orbit.","Chang’e 6 brought back the first samples from the far side of the Moon.","China is the third country to launch astronauts on its own rockets."],
fun:"China’s first satellite, launched in 1970, broadcast the song ‘The East Is Red’ back to Earth."});

ADDART({id:"csa",t:"The Canadian Space Agency",c:"Space",i:"🍁",w:"Canadian Space Agency",r:["spaceagencies","nasa","iss","artemis","spaceshuttle","artemisii"],
p:[["Canada in space","The Canadian Space Agency leads Canada’s space activities."],["The robot arms","Canada is famous for building the Canadarm robotic arms."],["To the Moon","A Canadian astronaut flew around the Moon on Artemis II in 2026."]],
l:"The Canadian Space Agency (CSA) is the Canadian government agency responsible for space. It was founded in 1989 and has its headquarters near Montreal, Quebec. Canada was the third country, after the Soviet Union and the United States, to design and build its own satellite, Alouette 1, launched in 1962. It is best known for the Canadarm robotic arms used on the Space Shuttle and the Canadarm2 arm on the International Space Station. Canadian astronauts have flown on the Shuttle, Soyuz and the space station, and in April 2026, Jeremy Hansen became the first non-American to fly around the Moon, on NASA’s Artemis II.",
s:[["Alouette","Alouette 1, launched in 1962, studied the upper atmosphere and worked for 10 years, far longer than planned. It made Canada the third nation to build a satellite, although it was launched on an American rocket."],["Canadarm","The first Canadarm flew on the Space Shuttle in 1981 and was used on 90 missions to launch and catch satellites, including repairs to the Hubble Space Telescope. Canadarm2, installed on the International Space Station in 2001, helped assemble the station and catches visiting cargo ships. Canada is building Canadarm3 for NASA’s planned Gateway station near the Moon."],["Canadian astronauts","Marc Garneau became the first Canadian in space in 1984. Roberta Bondar, a neurologist, flew in 1992 as Canada’s first woman astronaut. Chris Hadfield commanded the International Space Station in 2013 and became famous for recording a version of David Bowie’s ‘Space Oddity’ in orbit."],["To the Moon","Because of Canada’s contribution of Canadarm3, NASA agreed to fly Canadian astronauts on Artemis missions. Jeremy Hansen, a former fighter pilot, flew around the Moon on Artemis II from April 1 to 10, 2026, alongside three NASA astronauts."]],
k:[["Founded","1989"],["Headquarters","Longueuil, Quebec (near Montreal)"],["First satellite","Alouette 1 (1962)"],["Famous technology","Canadarm robotic arms"],["First Canadian around the Moon","Jeremy Hansen (2026)"]],
d:[["1962","Alouette 1 is launched"],["1981","The first Canadarm flies on the Space Shuttle"],["1984","Marc Garneau becomes the first Canadian in space"],["1989","The Canadian Space Agency is founded"],["2013","Chris Hadfield commands the International Space Station"],["2026","Jeremy Hansen flies around the Moon on Artemis II"]],
s2:[["Watching Earth","Canada’s RADARSAT satellites use radar to see through clouds and darkness, tracking sea ice in the Arctic, floods and oil spills."],["Space and the North","Canada uses satellites to provide communications to remote northern communities and to watch over its vast Arctic territory."]],
sp:["Space Oddity","Chris Hadfield’s 2013 music video of ‘Space Oddity’, filmed aboard the International Space Station, was watched millions of times and is often called the first music video made in space."],
g:[["Canadarm","Canada’s robotic arms used in space"],["Gateway","A planned space station orbiting the Moon"],["RADARSAT","Canadian radar satellites for watching Earth"]],
f:["Canada was the third country to build its own satellite.","Canadarm was used on 90 Space Shuttle missions.","Jeremy Hansen was the first non-American to fly around the Moon."],
fun:"Chris Hadfield recorded a music video of David Bowie’s ‘Space Oddity’ while living on the International Space Station."});

;
/* data/online1.js */
// Growing Up Online (27 November 2026).
ADDART({id:"digitalfootprint",t:"Your Digital Footprint",c:"Science and Technology",i:"👣",w:"Digital footprint",r:["dataprivacy","socialmedia","passwords","cyberbullying","onlinesafety","netiquette"],
p:[["Traces you leave online","A digital footprint is the trail of information you leave when you use the internet."],["Hard to erase","Posts, photos and searches can stay online for years."],["Think before you post","A little care now protects your privacy and reputation later."]],
l:"A digital footprint is the record of everything a person does online: the posts, photos, comments and videos they share, the websites they visit, the things they search for and buy, and the information that apps and websites collect about them. Some of it is shared on purpose, called an active footprint, and some is gathered without people noticing, called a passive footprint. Because online information can be copied, saved and searched, it can last for years and may be seen by future schools, employers or strangers. Understanding your digital footprint helps you protect your privacy, safety and reputation.",
s:[["Active and passive footprints","Your active footprint is what you choose to share, like a selfie, a game review or a comment. Your passive footprint is collected in the background: your location, the videos you watch, how long you look at a post and which ads you click. Companies use this data to recommend content and show ads."],["Why it lasts","Even when you delete a post, someone may already have taken a screenshot or shared it, and copies may remain on servers or in web archives. A post meant only for friends can spread far beyond them. This is why people say that the internet never forgets."],["Who looks at it","Universities, scholarship committees and employers sometimes search for applicants online. A kind, thoughtful online presence can help, while cruel or embarrassing posts can cause problems years later."],["Shrinking your footprint","You can make your accounts private, turn off location sharing, check app permissions, use strong passwords and think before posting. Search for your own name now and then to see what others can find."]],
k:[["Definition","The trail of data you leave online"],["Active footprint","What you share on purpose"],["Passive footprint","Data collected without you noticing"],["Philippine law","Data Privacy Act of 2012"],["Best habit","Think before you post"]],
d:[["1996","The Internet Archive begins saving copies of websites"],["2012","The Philippines passes the Data Privacy Act"],["2014","A European court recognizes a ‘right to be forgotten’ in search results"],["2018","Europe’s General Data Protection Regulation takes effect"]],
s2:[["The right to be forgotten","In 2014, the European Union’s top court ruled that people can ask search engines to remove links to outdated or irrelevant information about them. Other countries have different rules."],["Sharenting","Parents who post photos and stories about their children start a child’s digital footprint before the child can decide. Many experts suggest asking children before posting about them once they are old enough."]],
sp:["The grandmother test","A simple rule: before posting, ask yourself whether you would be comfortable if your grandmother, your teacher or a future boss saw it."],
g:[["Digital footprint","The record of a person’s online activity"],["Privacy settings","Options that control who sees your information"],["Data broker","A company that collects and sells personal data"]],
f:["Deleted posts can survive as screenshots.","Apps can collect your location even when you are not posting.","The Internet Archive has saved hundreds of billions of web pages."],
fun:"The Internet Archive’s Wayback Machine has saved hundreds of billions of web pages, so old versions of many websites can still be viewed."});

ADDART({id:"netiquette",t:"Online Etiquette",c:"Science and Technology",i:"🤝",w:"Etiquette in technology",r:["socialmedia","cyberbullying","misinformation","digitalfootprint","onlinelearning","copyrightonline","onlinecommunities"],
p:[["Good manners online","Netiquette means being polite, kind and respectful on the internet."],["Real people","Behind every screen is a real person with feelings."],["Simple rules","Think before you post, respect privacy and do not spread rumors."]],
l:"Online etiquette, often called netiquette, is the set of manners and good habits for behaving on the internet. Because people cannot see each other’s faces or hear their tone of voice online, messages are easily misunderstood, and some people say things they would never say in person. Netiquette encourages people to be respectful, to think before posting, to respect others’ privacy, to give credit when sharing someone else’s work and to avoid spreading rumors or false information. The word combines ‘network’ or ‘internet’ with ‘etiquette’.",
s:[["Remember the human","The most important rule is to treat people online the way you would treat them face to face. Insults, mockery and pile-ons can hurt deeply and may count as cyberbullying. If you disagree, criticize the idea, not the person."],["Tone and clarity","Text can seem colder or angrier than intended. Typing in ALL CAPITALS looks like shouting. Emojis can show tone, but they can also be read differently by different people. In group chats, think about whether a message needs to go to everyone."],["Respect privacy","Do not share other people’s photos, screenshots of private chats or personal details without permission. Tagging someone in an embarrassing photo or posting their location can cause real harm."],["Share responsibly","Check facts before sharing news, especially shocking stories. Give credit to artists and creators, and do not pass off others’ work as your own. In online classes, mute your microphone when not speaking and stay on topic in chats."]],
k:[["Also called","Netiquette"],["Golden rule","Treat others as you would in person"],["Shouting online","Typing in all capitals"],["Sharing tip","Check facts and give credit"],["Classic guide","Virginia Shea’s Netiquette (1994)"]],
d:[["1982","Scott Fahlman proposes the :-) smiley to show a joke online"],["1994","Virginia Shea publishes the book Netiquette"],["1995","An internet standards document, RFC 1855, sets out netiquette guidelines"],["2020","Online classes during the COVID-19 pandemic make video-call manners important worldwide"]],
s2:[["Trolls","Internet trolls post deliberately provocative or offensive messages to upset people. The usual advice is ‘do not feed the trolls’: ignore, block and report rather than reply."],["Different places, different rules","A gaming chat, a class forum and a family group chat all have different expectations. Many online communities post their own rules, and moderators remove posts that break them."]],
sp:["Online manners in the Philippines","The Philippines has some of the world’s most active social media users. Campaigns by schools and the government encourage students to be ‘responsible digital citizens’ who think before they click, share and comment."],
g:[["Netiquette","Good manners on the internet"],["Troll","Someone who posts to provoke or upset others"],["Moderator","A person who enforces rules in an online community"]],
f:["Typing in all capitals is seen as shouting.","The :-) smiley was proposed in 1982 so people could mark jokes.","Many online communities have moderators who enforce their rules."],
fun:"The sideways smiley :-) was invented in 1982 by computer scientist Scott Fahlman so that people could show when they were joking."});

ADDART({id:"chatbots",t:"AI Chatbots",c:"Science and Technology",i:"💬",w:"Chatbot",r:["ai","llm","aihistory","misinformation","onlinelearning"],
p:[["Computers you can talk to","AI chatbots are programs that answer questions and hold conversations in ordinary language."],["Powered by language models","Modern chatbots learn patterns from huge amounts of text."],["Helpful but not perfect","Chatbots can explain and create, but they can also make mistakes."]],
l:"An AI chatbot is a computer program that people can talk with in everyday language, by typing or speaking. Early chatbots followed simple rules, but modern ones, such as ChatGPT, Claude and Gemini, are built on large language models trained on vast amounts of text. They can answer questions, explain ideas, help write and translate, summarize documents, write computer code and more. Chatbots became widely popular after ChatGPT was released in November 2022. They are powerful tools, but they can make up false information, reflect biases in their training data and raise questions about privacy, honesty in schoolwork and jobs.",
s:[["From ELIZA to ChatGPT","In 1966, Joseph Weizenbaum at MIT created ELIZA, a program that imitated a therapist by turning users’ statements into questions. Many users felt it understood them, even though it followed simple rules. Later assistants such as Siri, launched in 2011, answered spoken questions. ChatGPT, released on November 30, 2022, reached a huge audience within weeks and started a race among technology companies."],["How they work","A large language model learns statistical patterns from billions of sentences, then predicts which words are likely to come next in a response. Companies then train the models further, using human feedback, to be more helpful and to refuse harmful requests. Many chatbots can also search the web, read images and use other tools."],["Mistakes and hallucinations","Chatbots sometimes state false facts confidently, invent quotes or cite sources that do not exist. This is called hallucination. It is important to double-check important information, especially about health, money or the law, with reliable sources or trusted adults."],["Using chatbots well","Chatbots can be good study partners: they can explain a hard idea in simpler words or quiz you before a test. But copying their answers as your own work is cheating, and many schools have rules about using AI. Avoid sharing private information, such as passwords, addresses or photos of others, with any chatbot."]],
k:[["First chatbot","ELIZA (1966)"],["Breakthrough","ChatGPT, released November 30, 2022"],["Built on","Large language models"],["Main risk","Hallucinations (made-up facts)"],["Good habit","Check important answers with reliable sources"]],
d:[["1950","Alan Turing proposes a test of whether a machine can imitate human conversation"],["1966","Joseph Weizenbaum creates ELIZA"],["2011","Apple introduces the voice assistant Siri"],["2017","Google researchers describe the transformer, the design behind modern language models"],["2022","ChatGPT is released"]],
s2:[["Who makes them","Chatbots are made by companies including OpenAI (ChatGPT), Anthropic (Claude), Google (Gemini), Meta and others, as well as by researchers who share open models. Most require users to be at least 13 years old, with parents’ permission for younger teenagers."],["Big questions","People debate how chatbots should be used in schools and workplaces, whether training them on books and artwork without permission is fair, how much energy they use, and how to keep them safe and honest as they become more capable."]],
sp:["The ELIZA effect","Even people who knew ELIZA was a simple program sometimes asked to be left alone with it to talk privately. The tendency to believe a computer understands us is now called the ELIZA effect."],
g:[["Chatbot","A program that converses in ordinary language"],["Large language model","An AI trained on huge amounts of text to predict words"],["Hallucination","When an AI states false information as fact"]],
f:["The first chatbot, ELIZA, was created in 1966.","Chatbots can confidently invent facts that are not true.","Most chatbots require users to be at least 13."],
fun:"When Joseph Weizenbaum’s secretary tried his 1966 chatbot ELIZA, she asked him to leave the room so she could talk to it privately."});

ADDART({id:"onlinegaming",t:"Gaming Online",c:"Science and Technology",i:"🎮",w:"Online game",r:["videogames","esports","gameconsoles","screentime","onlinesafety","onlinecommunities"],
p:[["Playing together","Online games let people play with friends and strangers around the world."],["Fun and community","Gaming can build teamwork, creativity and friendships."],["Playing smart","Safe gaming means guarding your account, your money and your time."]],
l:"Online gaming means playing video games over the internet with or against other people. Popular online games include battle royales, team strategy games, building games like Minecraft and Roblox, and mobile games such as Mobile Legends: Bang Bang, which is hugely popular in the Philippines and Southeast Asia. Online games can be social and creative and can teach teamwork and problem-solving. But they also bring risks: contact with strangers, toxic chat, scams that steal accounts, spending real money on in-game items, and playing so long that sleep, schoolwork or health suffer.",
s:[["Kinds of online games","Massively multiplayer online games put thousands of players in one shared world. MOBAs, or multiplayer online battle arenas, such as Mobile Legends and Dota 2, pit two teams of five against each other. Battle royales drop many players onto a shrinking map until one is left. Sandbox games like Minecraft and Roblox let players build worlds and create their own games."],["Free-to-play and in-game purchases","Many games are free to download but make money by selling skins, characters, battle passes and in-game currency. Loot boxes, which give random rewards for money, have been compared to gambling; Belgium banned paid loot boxes in 2018, and other countries require games to show the odds of winning items."],["Staying safe","Never share your password, home address or school with other players. Be wary of offers of free items or currency, which are often scams to steal accounts. Use privacy settings, mute or block abusive players, and tell a trusted adult if someone asks for photos or tries to move the chat elsewhere."],["Healthy gaming","Taking breaks, keeping devices out of the bedroom at night and balancing games with sports, friends and schoolwork help keep gaming fun. In 2019, the World Health Organization recognized ‘gaming disorder’ for the small number of people whose gaming seriously harms their lives."]],
k:[["Popular in the Philippines","Mobile Legends: Bang Bang"],["Common genres","MOBA, battle royale, sandbox, MMO"],["Main risks","Scams, strangers, overspending, too much screen time"],["Loot boxes","Banned in Belgium (2018)"],["Recognized by WHO","Gaming disorder (2019)"]],
d:[["1978","MUD1, an early online multiplayer text game, is created in England"],["1997","Ultima Online helps popularize massively multiplayer games"],["2009","Minecraft is first released"],["2016","Mobile Legends: Bang Bang is launched"],["2019","The WHO recognizes gaming disorder"]],
s2:[["The Philippines and mobile gaming","Because smartphones are much more common than gaming PCs and consoles, mobile games dominate in the Philippines. Filipino Mobile Legends teams have won major world championships, and esports was included in the Southeast Asian Games hosted by the Philippines in 2019."],["Games as social spaces","For many young people, games are where they meet friends after school. Some games host concerts, movie screenings and art shows inside their virtual worlds."]],
sp:["Too good to be true","Messages offering free diamonds, skins or currency usually lead to fake login pages that steal accounts. Real games never ask for your password through chat."],
g:[["MOBA","Multiplayer online battle arena, a team strategy game"],["Loot box","A paid in-game item with random rewards"],["Free-to-play","A game that is free but sells optional items"]],
f:["Belgium banned paid loot boxes in 2018.","Esports was a medal event at the 2019 Southeast Asian Games in the Philippines.","Minecraft was first released in 2009."],
fun:"Some online games have hosted live concerts inside their virtual worlds, watched by millions of players at once."});

ADDART({id:"onlinesafety",t:"Staying Safe Online",c:"Science and Technology",i:"🛡️",w:"Internet safety",r:["passwords","onlinescams","cyberbullying","phishing","digitalfootprint","onlinegaming","chatbots"],
p:[["Safety on the internet","Online safety means protecting yourself and others while using the internet."],["Know the risks","Scams, strangers, bullying and harmful content are real dangers."],["Simple habits","Strong passwords, privacy settings and telling a trusted adult go a long way."]],
l:"Online safety, or internet safety, is the knowledge and habits that protect people from harm when they use the internet. The main risks include scams and phishing that steal money or accounts, strangers who try to win children’s trust for harmful reasons, cyberbullying, harmful or upsetting content, and sharing too much personal information. Good habits include using strong, unique passwords with two-factor authentication, keeping accounts private, thinking before sharing, checking whether messages and links are genuine, and talking to a trusted adult when something online feels wrong. Many countries now also have laws to protect young people online.",
s:[["Protect your accounts","Use long, unique passwords or passkeys and turn on two-factor authentication, which asks for a code from your phone. Never share passwords, even with friends, and log out on shared devices. Keep apps and devices updated, because updates fix security holes."],["Scams and phishing","Phishing messages pretend to be from banks, games, delivery companies or friends and try to trick you into clicking links or giving codes. In the Philippines, scams by text message have been so common that a law requiring SIM card registration was passed in 2022. Never share one-time passwords (OTPs), and be suspicious of urgent messages and prizes."],["Strangers online","Most people online are who they say they are, but some adults pretend to be young to gain children’s trust, a crime called grooming. Warning signs include asking for secrecy, gifts, private photos or to meet in person. Children should tell a trusted adult right away, and such cases can be reported to the police."],["Harmful content","The internet has violent, frightening or false content. Reporting tools, content filters and parental controls can help, and it is always all right to close an app and talk to someone about what you saw."]],
k:[["Main risks","Scams, strangers, bullying, harmful content, oversharing"],["Account protection","Strong passwords and two-factor authentication"],["Never share","Passwords and one-time PINs (OTPs)"],["Philippine law","SIM Registration Act (2022)"],["Best advice","Tell a trusted adult"]],
d:[["2004","Safer Internet Day is first held in Europe"],["2012","The Philippines passes its Cybercrime Prevention Act"],["2022","The Philippines passes the SIM Registration Act"],["2025","Australia’s ban on social media accounts for children under 16 takes effect"]],
s2:[["Laws to protect children","Countries are passing laws to make online services safer for children, such as requiring privacy-protective settings by default or age checks. In December 2025, Australia began banning children under 16 from having accounts on major social media platforms."],["Getting help in the Philippines","Online abuse and scams can be reported to the Philippine National Police Anti-Cybercrime Group and the National Bureau of Investigation’s Cybercrime Division. Schools also have child protection officers."]],
sp:["Safer Internet Day","Every February, more than 100 countries mark Safer Internet Day with lessons and campaigns to make the internet safer and kinder, especially for young people."],
g:[["Phishing","A trick to steal information using fake messages"],["Two-factor authentication","A second check, such as a code, when logging in"],["Grooming","When an adult builds a child’s trust in order to harm them"]],
f:["Two-factor authentication blocks most attempts to break into accounts with stolen passwords.","Real banks never ask for your one-time PIN by text or call.","Safer Internet Day is marked in more than 100 countries."],
fun:"Safer Internet Day began in Europe in 2004 and is now celebrated each February in more than 100 countries."});

;
/* data/money1.js */
// Money and Work (27 November 2026). Figures that change were checked by web search on this date.
ADDART({id:"budgeting",t:"Saving and Budgeting",c:"How We Live",i:"🐷",w:"Budget",r:["financialliteracy","money","interestdebt","inflation","digitalpay","retirement"],
p:[["Planning your money","A budget is a plan for how to use the money you have."],["Saving for later","Saving means setting money aside for goals and emergencies."],["Needs and wants","Good budgets put needs first and leave room for wants."]],
l:"A budget is a plan that shows how much money a person, family, business or government expects to receive and how it will be spent or saved. Saving means keeping part of your money for the future instead of spending it now. Budgeting helps people pay for what they need, avoid running out of money, prepare for emergencies and reach goals like buying a bicycle, paying for school or starting a business. A simple rule is to save first, then spend: put some money aside as soon as you receive it, before buying anything else.",
s:[["Making a budget","Start by writing down all your income, such as an allowance, gifts or wages. Then list your expenses, separating needs, like food, transport and school supplies, from wants, like snacks, games and toys. Subtract spending from income. If the result is negative, cut back on wants or find ways to earn more."],["Rules of thumb","One popular guide, the 50/30/20 rule, suggests spending about half of income on needs, 30 percent on wants and saving 20 percent. It is only a starting point: what matters most is to spend less than you earn and to save regularly, even small amounts."],["Where to save","A piggy bank, or alkansya, is a fun start, but money in a bank or a digital savings account is safer and can earn interest. Over time, interest earns interest of its own, which is called compound interest. Saving early, even a little, can grow into a lot."],["Emergency funds","Experts advise building an emergency fund, money set aside for unexpected costs such as illness, losing a job or repairs after a typhoon. A common goal for adults is enough to cover three to six months of basic expenses."]],
k:[["Budget","A plan for income, spending and saving"],["Key rule","Save first, then spend"],["Popular guide","50/30/20 rule"],["Emergency fund","About 3–6 months of expenses (adults)"],["Filipino piggy bank","Alkansya"]],
d:[["1810","Henry Duncan opens one of the first savings banks for ordinary people, in Ruthwell, Scotland"],["2005","Elizabeth Warren popularizes the 50/30/20 rule in the book All Your Worth"],["2015","The Philippines launches a National Strategy for Financial Inclusion"],["2020","Digital wallets and online savings accounts spread quickly in the Philippines during the pandemic"]],
s2:[["Paluwagan","In the Philippines, a paluwagan is a savings group where members contribute a fixed amount regularly and take turns receiving the whole pot. Similar groups exist around the world. They build discipline but depend on trust, since members can lose money if someone stops paying."],["Government budgets","Governments make budgets too. In the Philippines, the president proposes a national budget each year, and Congress debates and passes it as the General Appropriations Act."]],
sp:["The bamboo alkansya","Many Filipino children save coins in an alkansya made from a section of bamboo with a slot cut in it. To get the money out, the bamboo has to be split open, which makes it harder to spend the savings early."],
g:[["Budget","A plan for using money"],["Compound interest","Interest earned on both savings and earlier interest"],["Emergency fund","Savings kept for unexpected costs"]],
f:["Saving small amounts regularly can grow into large sums through compound interest.","A paluwagan is a Filipino group savings arrangement.","Governments, businesses and families all make budgets."],
fun:"A traditional bamboo alkansya has to be cut open to get the coins out, which makes it hard to give in to temptation."});

ADDART({id:"interestdebt",t:"Interest, Loans and Debt",c:"How We Live",i:"💳",w:"Interest",r:["money","creditcards","budgeting","inflation","financialliteracy","imfworldbank"],
p:[["The price of money","Interest is what it costs to borrow money, or what you earn for saving it."],["Borrowing wisely","Loans help people buy homes, study and start businesses."],["When debt grows","Debt with high interest can grow faster than people can repay it."]],
l:"Interest is the price of using someone else’s money. When you borrow money in a loan, you repay the amount borrowed, called the principal, plus interest. When you save money in a bank, the bank pays you interest because it can lend your money to others. Interest is usually given as a yearly percentage rate. Loans let people buy homes, pay for education, start businesses and handle emergencies, but debt can become a heavy burden if interest is high or payments are missed. Central banks, such as the Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas, influence interest rates across the economy.",
s:[["Simple and compound interest","Simple interest is charged only on the original amount. Compound interest is charged on the principal plus interest already added. For savers, compounding helps money grow; for borrowers, it can make unpaid debts grow quickly. Credit card balances that are not paid in full often grow this way."],["Kinds of loans","Mortgages are loans for buying homes, repaid over many years with the house as security. Student loans pay for education. Business loans help companies grow. Personal loans and salary loans cover everyday needs. Microloans are small loans to people, often women, who run small businesses and cannot get bank loans."],["Interest rates and the economy","Central banks set key interest rates. Raising them makes borrowing more expensive, which slows spending and helps fight inflation. Lowering them encourages borrowing and spending. The Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas, founded in 1993, sets the policy rate for the Philippines."],["Dangerous debt","Some lenders charge extremely high interest. In the Philippines, informal ‘5-6’ lenders lend five pesos and collect six, often within weeks, which can add up to very high yearly rates. Online lending apps have been criticized for harassing borrowers. Borrowing only what you can repay, and comparing rates, helps avoid debt traps."]],
k:[["Interest","The cost of borrowing or reward for saving"],["Principal","The amount borrowed or saved"],["Central bank of the Philippines","Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas (1993)"],["Informal lending","‘5-6’ lending"],["Small loans for small businesses","Microfinance"]],
d:[["c.1750 BC","The Code of Hammurabi sets limits on interest in Babylon"],["1694","The Bank of England is founded, partly to lend money to the government"],["1983","Muhammad Yunus founds the Grameen Bank in Bangladesh"],["1993","The Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas is established"],["2006","Yunus and the Grameen Bank win the Nobel Peace Prize"]],
s2:[["Usury","Charging excessive interest is called usury. Many religions and societies have limited or banned interest. Islamic banking avoids interest by using profit-sharing and leasing arrangements instead."],["Government debt","Governments borrow too, by selling bonds to investors. The Philippine government, like most, borrows to pay for roads, schools and other projects, and must pay interest on its debt from taxes."]],
sp:["Borrowing smart","Before borrowing, ask: Do I really need this? Can I afford the payments every month? What is the total cost, including interest and fees? Is the lender registered with the Securities and Exchange Commission or the Bangko Sentral?"],
g:[["Interest","Money paid for the use of borrowed money"],["Principal","The original amount of a loan or deposit"],["Usury","Charging very high interest"]],
f:["The Code of Hammurabi limited interest about 3,700 years ago.","Raising interest rates helps central banks fight inflation.","‘5-6’ lenders lend five pesos and collect six."],
fun:"Rules about interest are among the oldest laws we know, written in the Code of Hammurabi almost 4,000 years ago."});

ADDART({id:"unemployment",t:"Jobs and Unemployment",c:"How We Live",i:"💼",w:"Unemployment",r:["labor","phecon","gdp","minimumwage","gigeconomy","remittances","smallbiz","unions"],
p:[["Looking for work","Unemployment means wanting a job but not having one."],["A key measure","The unemployment rate shows how healthy an economy is."],["Underemployment","Many people have work but need more hours or better jobs."]],
l:"Unemployment is when people who are able and looking for work cannot find a job. The unemployment rate is the share of the labor force, people who are working or actively seeking work, who are unemployed. It is one of the most watched signs of an economy’s health: when businesses grow, they hire more people, and when the economy slows, jobs are lost. In the Philippines, the Philippine Statistics Authority measures jobs every month through the Labor Force Survey. In September 2026, it reported an unemployment rate of 5.4 percent, or about 2.86 million Filipinos without jobs, and an underemployment rate of 11 percent.",
s:[["Kinds of unemployment","Frictional unemployment happens while people move between jobs or start looking after school. Structural unemployment occurs when workers’ skills do not match the jobs available, for example when machines replace certain tasks. Cyclical unemployment rises during recessions. Seasonal unemployment affects farm, fishing and tourism workers between busy seasons."],["Underemployment","Some people have work but want more hours or a job that uses their skills better. In the Philippines, underemployment is often a bigger issue than unemployment: many workers have part-time, low-paid or informal jobs, such as street vending or day labor, without contracts or benefits."],["Why it matters","Unemployment means lost income for families and lost production for the economy. Long periods without work can harm health and confidence. Young people often face the highest rates, and fresh graduates may take months to find jobs that match their studies."],["What helps","Governments try to create jobs by encouraging investment, building infrastructure and supporting small businesses. Training programs, such as those of the Technical Education and Skills Development Authority (TESDA), help workers learn new skills. Some countries pay unemployment benefits to support people while they look for work."]],
k:[["Definition","Wanting work but not having a job"],["Philippine survey","Labor Force Survey (PSA)"],["Unemployment rate, September 2026","5.4% (about 2.86 million people)"],["Underemployment rate, September 2026","11.0%"],["Skills training agency","TESDA"]],
d:[["1929","The Great Depression begins; US unemployment later reaches about 25 percent"],["1956","The Philippines begins its regular Labor Force Survey"],["1994","TESDA is created in the Philippines"],["2020","Philippine unemployment jumps to 17.6 percent during pandemic lockdowns in April"],["2026","The Philippine unemployment rate is 5.4 percent in September"]],
s2:[["Working abroad","When jobs at home are scarce or low-paid, many Filipinos seek work overseas as nurses, seafarers, engineers and household workers. Their remittances support families and the economy."],["Machines and jobs","New technology destroys some jobs and creates others. Today, people debate how artificial intelligence will change work, and many experts say lifelong learning will be important."]],
sp:["Counting jobs","Each month, PSA interviewers visit sample households across the Philippines and ask about everyone’s work in the past week. From these answers, the agency estimates employment for the whole country."],
g:[["Labor force","People working or actively looking for work"],["Underemployment","Having work but needing more hours or better jobs"],["Informal sector","Work without contracts, benefits or registration"]],
f:["Philippine unemployment reached 17.6 percent in April 2020 during lockdowns.","The PSA measures jobs through a monthly Labor Force Survey.","Underemployment is often a bigger problem than unemployment in the Philippines."],
fun:"To be counted as unemployed, a person must be looking for work; someone who has given up searching is not counted in the unemployment rate."});

ADDART({id:"remittances",t:"Remittances",c:"How We Live",i:"💸",w:"Remittance",r:["ofw","phecon","digitalpay","money","migration"],
p:[["Money sent home","Remittances are money that people working abroad send to their families."],["A lifeline","For millions of families, remittances pay for food, school and homes."],["A pillar of the economy","The Philippines receives some of the largest remittances in the world."]],
l:"Remittances are transfers of money from people working in another country, or another part of their own country, to family members back home. Worldwide, migrants send hundreds of billions of dollars every year, more than the total of all foreign aid. The Philippines is one of the largest receivers: according to the Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas, cash remittances from overseas Filipinos reached a record 35.63 billion US dollars in 2025. Remittances help families pay for food, schooling, health care and housing, and they support the country’s economy, though they also reflect the lack of well-paid jobs at home.",
s:[["How money is sent","In the past, workers sent money by mail, through banks or by asking travelers to carry it. Today, money moves through banks, money transfer companies, pawnshops and digital wallets, often arriving within minutes. Fees can be high, so groups such as the United Nations aim to lower the cost of sending money."],["Who sends money","Overseas Filipino workers include nurses, caregivers, seafarers, engineers, construction workers and household helpers. The largest amounts come from the United States, which has a large Filipino community, followed by countries such as Singapore, Saudi Arabia, Japan and the United Kingdom."],["Effects on families","Remittances lift many families out of poverty and pay for children’s education. But long separations are hard: children may grow up with a parent abroad, and workers can face abuse, illegal recruitment or debt. The Philippine government created a Department of Migrant Workers in 2021 to protect them."],["Effects on the economy","Remittances are a major source of foreign currency for the Philippines, equal to several percent of the economy. They boost household spending, which drives growth, and they tend to rise in December, when workers send extra money for Christmas."]],
k:[["Definition","Money sent home by workers away from home"],["Philippine cash remittances, 2025","US$35.63 billion (record)"],["Largest source","United States"],["Peak month","December"],["Government department","Department of Migrant Workers (2021)"]],
d:[["1974","The Philippine Labor Code begins a formal overseas employment program"],["1982","The Philippine Overseas Employment Administration is created"],["1995","The Migrant Workers and Overseas Filipinos Act is passed"],["2021","The Department of Migrant Workers is created"],["2025","Philippine cash remittances reach a record $35.63 billion"]],
s2:[["Balikbayan boxes","Besides money, many overseas Filipinos send balikbayan boxes, large cartons packed with food, clothes, toys and gifts, shipped home by sea. The boxes have become a symbol of Filipino families’ love across distance."],["Mga bagong bayani","Overseas Filipino workers are often called ‘mga bagong bayani’, or modern-day heroes, because of the sacrifices they make for their families and the country."]],
sp:["Remittances around the world","India, Mexico, China, the Philippines and Egypt are among the countries receiving the most remittances. In some small countries, remittances are equal to more than a quarter of the whole economy."],
g:[["Remittance","Money sent by a worker to family elsewhere"],["OFW","Overseas Filipino worker"],["Balikbayan box","A box of gifts sent home by overseas Filipinos"]],
f:["Philippine cash remittances reached a record $35.63 billion in 2025.","Remittances usually peak in December.","Worldwide, remittances are larger than all foreign aid combined."],
fun:"Every December, remittances to the Philippines jump as overseas workers send extra money home for Christmas."});

ADDART({id:"smallbiz",t:"Small Businesses and the Sari-sari Store",c:"How We Live",i:"🏪",w:"Sari-sari store",r:["startups","phecon","trade","interestdebt","digitalpay"],
p:[["The backbone of the economy","Small businesses make up most businesses in the Philippines and many other countries."],["The corner store","The sari-sari store is the neighborhood shop found almost everywhere in the Philippines."],["Big jobs from small firms","Small businesses employ millions of people."]],
l:"Small businesses are companies with relatively few workers and modest sales, from family stores and eateries to repair shops and online sellers. In the Philippines, micro, small and medium enterprises, or MSMEs, are the backbone of the economy: according to the Department of Trade and Industry, they made up 99.63 percent of all business establishments in 2023 and provided about 67 percent of jobs. The most familiar small business is the sari-sari store, a tiny neighborhood shop, often run from the front of a home, that sells snacks, drinks, soap, cooking oil and phone load in small amounts.",
s:[["The sari-sari store","‘Sari-sari’ means ‘various’ or ‘assorted’ in Filipino. These stores sell goods in tiny packets called tingi, such as single-use sachets of shampoo or a few pieces of garlic, so that families with little cash can buy what they need each day. Many store owners also let trusted neighbors buy on credit, recorded in a notebook, and the store is often a gathering place for the community."],["Micro, small and medium","In the Philippines, businesses are grouped by their assets or number of workers. Micro enterprises have fewer than 10 workers, small enterprises 10 to 99, and medium enterprises 100 to 199. In 2023, micro enterprises alone made up more than 90 percent of all businesses."],["Starting a small business","Many people start businesses to earn extra income or be their own boss, selling food, clothes, crafts or services. They need some money to start, called capital, a product people want, a good location or online shop, and careful records of costs and sales. Businesses must register with the government and pay taxes."],["Challenges","Small businesses often struggle to get loans, compete with large companies and survive disasters or economic slowdowns. Government programs offer training, small loans and help with selling online, and cooperatives let small producers pool resources."]],
k:[["MSME share of businesses (2023)","99.63%"],["MSME share of jobs (2023)","About 67%"],["Micro enterprise","Fewer than 10 workers"],["Iconic small business","Sari-sari store"],["Buying in small amounts","Tingi"]],
d:[["1991","The Magna Carta for Small Enterprises is passed in the Philippines"],["2002","The Barangay Micro Business Enterprises Act offers tax breaks to the smallest firms"],["2008","The Magna Carta is expanded to cover micro, small and medium enterprises"],["2020","Many small businesses move online during pandemic lockdowns"]],
s2:[["The sachet economy","Selling products in small, cheap sachets lets low-income families buy goods they could not afford in large bottles. But the billions of plastic sachets used each year are a major source of plastic waste in the Philippines’ rivers and seas."],["Online sellers","Social media and shopping apps have helped many Filipinos, especially women and young people, start businesses from home, selling clothes, food and crafts across the country."]],
sp:["More than a store","A sari-sari store is often the first place neighbors hear the news, leave messages, charge phones or ask for directions, making it a hub of community life."],
g:[["MSME","Micro, small and medium enterprise"],["Tingi","Buying or selling in small amounts"],["Capital","Money used to start or run a business"]],
f:["MSMEs made up 99.63 percent of Philippine businesses in 2023.","‘Sari-sari’ means ‘assorted’.","Many sari-sari stores let neighbors buy on credit, recorded in a notebook."],
fun:"Many sari-sari stores sell through a small window with metal bars, and customers often call the owner by name to get served."});

ADDART({id:"unions",t:"Labor Unions",c:"How We Live",i:"✊",w:"Trade union",r:["labor","minimumwage","childlabor","indrev","unemployment"],
p:[["Workers together","A labor union is an organization of workers who join together to improve their jobs."],["Bargaining for better","Unions negotiate with employers over pay, hours and safety."],["Hard-won rights","Many rights workers have today, like the weekend, came from union struggles."]],
l:"A labor union, or trade union, is an organization of workers who join together to protect their interests, such as fair pay, reasonable hours, safe workplaces and job security. Instead of each worker bargaining alone, the union negotiates with employers for all its members, a process called collective bargaining. If talks fail, workers may go on strike, refusing to work until an agreement is reached. Unions grew during the Industrial Revolution, when factory workers faced long hours, low pay and dangerous conditions, and they helped win many rights that workers now take for granted, such as the eight-hour day, weekends and safety laws.",
s:[["Origins","As factories spread in the 1700s and 1800s, workers began forming unions. At first they were often illegal, and strikes were met with arrests and violence. Over time, many countries recognized workers’ right to organize. The International Labour Organization, founded in 1919, set international standards for workers’ rights."],["What unions do","Unions negotiate collective bargaining agreements that set wages, benefits and working conditions. They represent workers in disputes, campaign for laws such as minimum wages and safety rules, and sometimes support political parties."],["May Day","International Workers’ Day on May 1 remembers workers’ struggles, including the 1886 Haymarket affair in Chicago during a campaign for the eight-hour day. In the Philippines, Labor Day on May 1 is a national holiday, often marked by rallies calling for higher wages."],["Unions in the Philippines","The Philippine Constitution protects workers’ right to form unions, bargain collectively and strike, in line with law. The Union Obrera Democrática Filipina, founded in 1902 under Isabelo de los Reyes, was one of the first labor federations in the country. Labor issues today include contractualization, short-term contracts that deny workers regular status and benefits."]],
k:[["Also called","Trade union"],["Main method","Collective bargaining"],["Last resort","Strike"],["International body","International Labour Organization (1919)"],["Labor Day","May 1"]],
d:[["1886","The Haymarket affair in Chicago, during the campaign for an eight-hour day"],["1902","Isabelo de los Reyes founds the Union Obrera Democrática Filipina"],["1919","The International Labour Organization is founded"],["1974","The Labor Code of the Philippines is issued"],["1980","Poland’s Solidarity union is founded"]],
s2:[["Solidarity","In 1980, shipyard workers in Gdańsk, Poland, led by Lech Wałęsa, founded Solidarity, the first independent union in the Soviet bloc. It grew into a mass movement that helped end communist rule in Poland in 1989."],["Debates","Supporters say unions raise wages and protect workers. Critics argue they can make businesses less flexible or protect members at the expense of other workers. The share of workers in unions has fallen in many countries since the 1970s."]],
sp:["The weekend","The two-day weekend became common in many countries in the 20th century after campaigns by unions and workers for shorter working weeks."],
g:[["Labor union","An organization of workers"],["Collective bargaining","Negotiation between a union and an employer"],["Strike","When workers stop working to press demands"]],
f:["Labor Day on May 1 is a public holiday in the Philippines.","The International Labour Organization was founded in 1919.","Poland’s Solidarity union helped end communist rule."],
fun:"The idea of an eight-hour day was summed up in an old slogan: ‘Eight hours for work, eight hours for rest, eight hours for what we will.’"});

;
/* data/life4.js */
// Life, part 4 (27 November 2026): microbes, how bodies work and how animals live.
ADDART({id:"bacteria",t:"Bacteria",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦠",w:"Bacteria",r:["microbe","antibiotics","germtheory","cell","immune","yeast","originoflife"],
p:[["Tiny single cells","Bacteria are living things made of just one simple cell."],["Everywhere on Earth","They live in soil, water, ice, hot springs, rocks and inside our bodies."],["Mostly helpful","A few cause disease, but most are harmless or useful."]],
l:"Bacteria are tiny living things, each made of a single cell without a nucleus. They are among the oldest forms of life, with fossil traces more than 3 billion years old, and they live almost everywhere: in soil, oceans, hot springs, Antarctic ice, deep rocks and on and inside plants and animals. Most bacteria are only a few thousandths of a millimeter long, so they can be seen only with a microscope. Some cause diseases such as tuberculosis and cholera, but most are harmless, and many are essential. They recycle dead matter, make nutrients for plants, help us digest food and are used to make foods and medicines.",
s:[["What a bacterium is like","A bacterium is a single cell with a tough wall, a thin membrane and a jelly-like inside, but no nucleus. Its DNA usually sits in one loop. Many bacteria swim with whip-like tails called flagella. They come in a few main shapes: balls (cocci), rods (bacilli) and spirals."],["Multiplying fast","Bacteria reproduce by splitting in two, a process called binary fission. In ideal conditions, some can divide every 20 minutes, so one cell could become millions within a few hours. They can also swap small pieces of DNA with each other, which helps them adapt quickly."],["Our bacterial partners","A human body carries roughly as many bacterial cells as human cells, about 38 trillion, most of them in the gut. These gut bacteria help break down food, make some vitamins and train the immune system. Together with other microbes, they form the microbiome."],["Germs and medicine","Harmful bacteria are often called germs. Diseases caused by bacteria include tuberculosis, cholera, typhoid and strep throat. Antibiotics can kill bacteria or stop them growing, but overuse has led some bacteria to become resistant. Handwashing, clean water and vaccines help prevent bacterial infections."]],
k:[["Type of life","Single cells without a nucleus"],["Typical size","About 1–5 thousandths of a millimeter"],["Oldest traces","More than 3 billion years old"],["In a human body","About 38 trillion bacterial cells"],["First seen","1676, by Antonie van Leeuwenhoek"]],
d:[["1676","Antonie van Leeuwenhoek describes tiny living things seen through his microscope"],["1876","Robert Koch shows that a bacterium causes anthrax"],["1882","Koch identifies the bacterium that causes tuberculosis"],["1928","Alexander Fleming discovers penicillin, which kills bacteria"],["1977","Carl Woese shows that archaea are a separate group from bacteria"]],
s2:[["Bacteria that changed the world","About 2.4 billion years ago, cyanobacteria, which make food from sunlight, filled Earth’s air with oxygen. This Great Oxidation Event made animal life possible. Even today, tiny ocean cyanobacteria produce a large share of the oxygen we breathe."],["Useful bacteria","Bacteria turn milk into yogurt and cheese and help make vinegar, soy sauce and fermented vegetables. Bacteria in plant roots turn nitrogen from the air into fertilizer. Scientists use bacteria to make insulin and other medicines, and to clean up oil spills and wastewater."]],
sp:["Life in extreme places","Some bacteria thrive where almost nothing else can. Thermus aquaticus lives in the hot springs of Yellowstone National Park, and an enzyme from it made possible PCR, the method used to copy DNA in laboratories. Deinococcus radiodurans can survive radiation thousands of times stronger than would kill a person."],
g:[["Bacterium","One single-celled living thing (plural: bacteria)"],["Binary fission","Splitting in two to reproduce"],["Antibiotic","A medicine that kills bacteria or stops them growing"]],
f:["A human body carries about 38 trillion bacterial cells.","Some bacteria can divide every 20 minutes.","Cyanobacteria filled Earth’s air with oxygen billions of years ago."],
fun:"Some bacteria glow in the dark. Bobtail squid keep glowing bacteria in a special organ, which helps hide their shadow from predators below on moonlit nights."});

ADDART({id:"viruses",t:"Viruses",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🧬",w:"Virus",r:["microbe","bacteria","immune","pandemic","covid19","germtheory"],
p:[["Smaller than bacteria","Viruses are tiny particles, far smaller than bacteria."],["Need a host","A virus can multiply only inside the cells of another living thing."],["Causes of disease","Viruses cause colds, flu, measles, dengue and COVID-19."]],
l:"A virus is a tiny particle made of genetic material, DNA or RNA, wrapped in a coat of protein. Viruses are much smaller than bacteria, and most can be seen only with an electron microscope. A virus cannot grow or multiply on its own. It must get inside a living cell and take over the cell’s machinery to make copies of itself. Because of this, scientists debate whether viruses are truly alive. Viruses infect every kind of living thing, from people and plants to bacteria. They cause diseases such as the common cold, flu, measles, dengue, HIV and COVID-19, but the body can fight them, and vaccines protect against many.",
s:[["How a virus works","A virus attaches to a cell, slips its genetic material inside and tricks the cell into building new viruses. The new viruses burst out or bud off to infect more cells. Each kind of virus can usually infect only certain kinds of cells, which is why a plant virus cannot make a person sick."],["Shapes and sizes","Most viruses are about 20 to 300 nanometers across, hundreds of times smaller than a hair is wide. Some are shaped like balls with spikes, others like rods, and bacteriophages look like tiny landing spacecraft. Giant viruses such as Mimivirus, discovered in 2003, are big enough to see with an ordinary microscope."],["Fighting viruses","The immune system attacks viruses with antibodies and special white blood cells, and it remembers viruses it has met before. Vaccines train it without causing the disease. Antibiotics do not work on viruses, but antiviral medicines can slow some of them. Handwashing, masks and staying home when sick help stop viruses from spreading."],["How viruses spread","Different viruses spread in different ways: through droplets in the air when people cough or sneeze, through touch, through food and water, through blood, or through bites from mosquitoes and other animals. Some viruses that infect animals can jump to people, which is how many new diseases begin."]],
k:[["Made of","DNA or RNA inside a protein coat"],["Typical size","About 20–300 nanometers"],["Multiplies","Only inside living cells"],["First discovered","Tobacco mosaic virus, 1892–1898"],["Defense","Immune system, vaccines, antiviral drugs"]],
d:[["1796","Edward Jenner tests the first vaccine, against smallpox"],["1892","Dmitri Ivanovsky finds that a tobacco plant disease passes through filters that stop bacteria"],["1898","Martinus Beijerinck calls the cause a virus"],["1939","Scientists first see a virus with an electron microscope"],["1980","The World Health Organization declares smallpox eradicated"]],
s2:[["Viruses in our DNA","About 8 percent of human DNA comes from ancient viruses that infected our ancestors and left copies of their genes behind. Some of these leftover genes now do useful jobs, such as helping build the placenta that feeds a baby before birth."],["Helpful viruses","Viruses that infect bacteria, called bacteriophages or phages, are the most common biological things on Earth. Doctors are testing phages to treat infections caused by bacteria that resist antibiotics. Viruses in the ocean also help recycle nutrients by bursting tiny living things."]],
sp:["The 1918 flu","The influenza pandemic of 1918 to 1920 was one of the deadliest disease outbreaks in history. It infected about a third of the world’s population and killed tens of millions of people. Lessons from it still shape how countries prepare for new pandemics."],
g:[["Virus","A tiny particle that multiplies only inside living cells"],["Host","The living thing a virus infects"],["Vaccine","A preparation that trains the immune system to fight a disease"]],
f:["Antibiotics do not work against viruses.","About 8 percent of human DNA came from ancient viruses.","Smallpox was declared eradicated in 1980."],
fun:"There may be about 10 million times more viruses in the oceans than there are stars in the observable universe."});

ADDART({id:"mycorrhiza",t:"Mycorrhizal Fungi",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🍄",w:"Mycorrhiza",r:["fungi","trees","rainforests","orchids","symbiosis","lichens"],
p:[["Fungi on roots","Mycorrhizal fungi live on and inside plant roots."],["A fair trade","The fungi bring water and minerals; the plant gives them sugar."],["Most plants need them","Most land plants on Earth have these fungal partners."]],
l:"Mycorrhizal fungi are fungi that form a partnership with the roots of plants. Their threads, called hyphae, spread far through the soil, much farther and finer than roots can reach. The fungi gather water and minerals such as phosphorus and nitrogen and pass them to the plant. In return, the plant gives the fungi sugars it makes by photosynthesis. Most land plants, including most trees, crops and wildflowers, rely on these partners. The name comes from Greek words meaning fungus and root. The partnership is very old: fossils show that it helped the first plants move onto land more than 400 million years ago.",
s:[["Two main kinds","In the most common kind, arbuscular mycorrhizae, fungal threads grow right into root cells and form tiny tree-shaped structures where food is traded. In ectomycorrhizae, common in pine, oak and dipterocarp forests, the fungi wrap roots in a sheath and grow between the cells. Many mushrooms in forests are the fruiting bodies of ectomycorrhizal fungi."],["A huge network","Fungal threads are so thin and long that a single gram of healthy soil can contain many meters of them. They can make a plant’s root system effectively far larger, helping it survive drought and poor soil. The threads also hold soil grains together, which helps soil store water and resist erosion."],["The wood wide web","Fungi can link the roots of several plants at once, forming common mycorrhizal networks. Some studies suggest that trees can pass sugar or warning signals through these networks, an idea popularly called the wood wide web. Other scientists say the evidence for trees deliberately sharing is weaker than often claimed, and research continues."],["Orchids depend on them","Orchid seeds are as fine as dust and carry almost no food. They can sprout only when a suitable fungus enters them and feeds the young plant. Some orchids, and a few other plants without green leaves, take all their food from fungi throughout their lives."]],
k:[["Partners","Fungi and plant roots"],["Fungus gives","Water, phosphorus, nitrogen"],["Plant gives","Sugars from photosynthesis"],["How common","Most land plant species"],["Age","More than 400 million years"]],
d:[["c. 407 Ma","Fossils from the Rhynie chert in Scotland show early plants with fungal partners"],["1885","Albert Bernhard Frank coins the word mycorrhiza"],["1997","A study in the journal Nature suggests trees share carbon through fungal links"],["2023","Scientists review the evidence and urge caution about claims of tree sharing"]],
s2:[["Farming and forests","Farmers and foresters sometimes add mycorrhizal fungi to soils to help seedlings grow, especially when replanting damaged land. Heavy use of phosphorus fertilizer and frequent plowing can weaken these fungal networks."],["Storing carbon","Plants send a large share of the carbon they capture into the soil through their fungal partners. Scientists are studying how much of this carbon stays underground, because it could help slow climate change."]],
sp:["Plants that steal from fungi","A few plants, such as the ghostly white Indian pipe of North America and Asia, have no green chlorophyll and cannot make their own food. Instead, they tap into the fungal networks around tree roots and take sugar that ultimately comes from the trees."],
g:[["Mycorrhiza","A partnership between a fungus and plant roots"],["Hyphae","The thin threads that make up a fungus"],["Symbiosis","A close relationship between two different living things"]],
f:["Most land plants have fungal partners on their roots.","Orchid seeds cannot sprout without a fungus.","This partnership helped plants move onto land over 400 million years ago."],
fun:"Truffles, among the most expensive foods in the world, are the underground fruiting bodies of mycorrhizal fungi that live on the roots of oak and hazel trees."});

ADDART({id:"proteins",t:"Proteins",c:"Science and Technology",i:"🧪",w:"Protein",r:["cell","dna","digest","hormone","insulin","genome"],
p:[["Workers of the cell","Proteins do most of the work inside living things."],["Chains of building blocks","Each protein is a chain of amino acids folded into a shape."],["Shape is everything","A protein’s shape decides what it can do."]],
l:"Proteins are large molecules that do most of the work in living things. They build muscles, hair and skin, carry oxygen in the blood, fight germs, send signals and speed up chemical reactions. Each protein is a long chain of small building blocks called amino acids, and living things use 20 main kinds. The order of the amino acids is set by a gene in the cell’s DNA. After it is made, the chain folds into a precise three-dimensional shape, and that shape decides the protein’s job. The human body uses tens of thousands of different proteins. People get the amino acids they need by eating protein-rich foods such as fish, eggs, beans, meat and milk.",
s:[["From DNA to protein","A gene is a set of instructions for one protein. The cell copies the gene into a messenger molecule called RNA, which travels to tiny machines called ribosomes. Ribosomes read the RNA three letters at a time and join the matching amino acids into a chain, one by one."],["Enzymes","Many proteins are enzymes, which speed up chemical reactions. Enzymes in the gut break food into pieces the body can absorb, and others build new molecules or release energy. Without enzymes, many reactions in the body would be far too slow to keep us alive."],["Proteins with jobs","Hemoglobin carries oxygen in red blood cells. Collagen makes skin and bones strong, and keratin forms hair and nails. Antibodies recognize germs, insulin controls blood sugar, and actin and myosin slide past each other to make muscles move."],["Folding problems","If a protein folds wrongly, it may stop working or clump together. Misfolded proteins are linked to diseases such as Alzheimer’s disease and sickle cell disease. Heat can unfold proteins too, which is why an egg white turns from clear to white when it is cooked."]],
k:[["Made of","Chains of amino acids"],["Kinds of amino acids","20 main kinds"],["Instructions","Genes in DNA"],["Built by","Ribosomes"],["Food sources","Fish, eggs, beans, meat, milk"]],
d:[["1838","Gerardus Mulder and Jöns Jacob Berzelius name proteins"],["1955","Frederick Sanger completes the amino acid sequence of insulin"],["1958","John Kendrew reveals the first 3D structure of a protein, myoglobin"],["2020","The AI program AlphaFold predicts protein shapes with high accuracy"],["2024","The Nobel Prize in Chemistry honors protein design and AlphaFold"]],
s2:[["Predicting shapes with AI","Working out a protein’s shape in a laboratory can take years. In 2020 an artificial intelligence program called AlphaFold, from Google DeepMind, began predicting shapes from amino acid sequences with high accuracy. It has predicted structures for more than 200 million proteins, and its makers shared the 2024 Nobel Prize in Chemistry with protein designer David Baker."],["How much protein people need","Adults need roughly 0.8 grams of protein a day for each kilogram of body weight, and growing children need a little more. Most people who eat a varied diet get enough. Plant foods such as beans, lentils, peanuts and tofu can provide all the amino acids the body needs when eaten in variety."]],
sp:["Spider silk","Spider silk is made of proteins spun into threads. For its weight, some spider silk is stronger than steel and far stretchier than nylon. Scientists are trying to produce silk proteins with bacteria and yeast to make strong, lightweight materials."],
g:[["Amino acid","One of the building blocks that make up proteins"],["Enzyme","A protein that speeds up a chemical reaction"],["Ribosome","A tiny cell machine that builds proteins"]],
f:["Living things use 20 main kinds of amino acids.","Cooking an egg changes its proteins’ shapes.","AlphaFold has predicted the shapes of more than 200 million proteins."],
fun:"The largest known human protein, titin, found in muscles, is a chain of about 34,000 amino acids."});

ADDART({id:"naturalselection",t:"Natural Selection",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦋",w:"Natural selection",r:["evo","darwin","galapagos","adaptation","antibiotics","dna"],
p:[["Survival of the fittest","Living things best suited to their surroundings tend to survive and have more young."],["Small differences add up","Over many generations, helpful traits become more common."],["The engine of evolution","Natural selection is a main cause of evolution."]],
l:"Natural selection is the process by which living things that are better suited to their environment tend to survive and reproduce more than others. Individuals of a species are not all the same: they vary in size, color, speed and many other traits, and many of these differences are inherited. If a trait helps an animal or plant survive and have more offspring, that trait becomes more common over the generations. Over long periods, natural selection can change species and produce new ones. Charles Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace described the idea in 1858, and it remains a central idea of biology.",
s:[["Three ingredients","Natural selection needs three things: variation, so individuals differ; inheritance, so offspring resemble their parents; and competition, because more young are born than can survive. Whenever all three are present, the traits that help survival and reproduction spread through a population."],["The peppered moth","In 19th-century England, smoke from factories darkened tree trunks. Pale peppered moths became easier for birds to see, while rare dark moths were hidden. Dark moths soon became common near cities. After clean-air laws in the 20th century, pale moths became common again."],["Darwin’s finches","On the Galápagos Islands, Peter and Rosemary Grant have studied finches since 1973. During a drought in 1977, when only large, hard seeds were left, finches with bigger beaks survived better, and the next generation had larger beaks on average. Selection was measured happening in just a few years."],["Selection we cause","Bacteria that survive an antibiotic pass on their resistance, so overusing antibiotics breeds superbugs. Insects become resistant to pesticides in the same way. Fishing that removes the biggest fish has led some fish populations to mature at smaller sizes."]],
k:[["Described by","Charles Darwin and Alfred Russel Wallace"],["Announced","1 July 1858"],["Needs","Variation, inheritance, competition"],["Result","Helpful traits become more common"],["Famous examples","Peppered moth, Galápagos finches"]],
d:[["1838","Charles Darwin first sketches the idea of natural selection in his notebooks"],["1858","Darwin’s and Wallace’s papers are read in London"],["1859","Darwin publishes On the Origin of Species"],["1973","Peter and Rosemary Grant begin studying Galápagos finches"],["1977","A drought on Daphne Major favors finches with larger beaks"]],
s2:[["Other kinds of selection","In sexual selection, traits spread because they help win mates, like a peacock’s tail or a deer’s antlers, even when they make survival harder. In artificial selection, people choose which plants and animals breed, which is how dogs, rice and corn were shaped from wild ancestors."],["A common misunderstanding","Natural selection does not plan ahead or aim for perfection. It works only on the variation that already exists, and the “fittest” simply means best suited to a particular environment at a particular time. When the environment changes, the traits that are favored can change too."]],
sp:["Islands of evolution","Islands are natural laboratories of evolution. On the Hawaiian Islands, a single kind of finch that arrived millions of years ago evolved into more than 50 species of honeycreepers, with beaks shaped for sipping nectar, cracking seeds or probing bark for insects."],
g:[["Trait","A feature of a living thing, such as color or size"],["Population","A group of the same species living in one place"],["Fitness","How well a living thing survives and reproduces"]],
f:["Darwin and Wallace announced the idea together in 1858.","Galápagos finches changed beak size after a drought in 1977.","Overusing antibiotics selects for resistant bacteria."],
fun:"Wallace came up with natural selection while sick with fever in the Malay Archipelago and mailed his idea to Darwin, which pushed Darwin to finally publish."});

ADDART({id:"homeostasis",t:"Homeostasis",c:"Science and Technology",i:"🌡️",w:"Homeostasis",r:["blood","kidney","hormone","insulin","diabetes","heart"],
p:[["Keeping steady","Homeostasis is the way a body keeps conditions inside it stable."],["Constant checking","Sensors, the brain and hormones work like a thermostat."],["Health depends on it","When homeostasis fails, illness can follow."]],
l:"Homeostasis is the way living things keep conditions inside their bodies steady, even when the world outside changes. The human body keeps its temperature near 37 °C, holds the amount of sugar, water and salt in the blood within narrow limits, and balances oxygen and carbon dioxide. Sensors detect changes, the brain or other control centers decide what to do, and muscles, glands and organs respond. Most of this works through negative feedback, which pushes conditions back toward normal. The French scientist Claude Bernard described this stable inner environment in the 1800s, and the American physiologist Walter Cannon named it homeostasis in 1926.",
s:[["Like a thermostat","A home thermostat switches the heater on when a room gets cold and off when it warms up. The body works the same way. When something drifts away from its normal range, the body acts to bring it back. This is called negative feedback, and it controls temperature, blood sugar, blood pressure and much more."],["Staying cool and warm","When you get hot, blood vessels near the skin widen to release heat and you sweat; as sweat evaporates, it cools you. When you get cold, skin vessels narrow, you shiver to make heat and the hairs on your skin stand up, causing goose bumps. A part of the brain called the hypothalamus controls these responses."],["Blood sugar","After a meal, sugar from food enters the blood. The pancreas releases the hormone insulin, which helps cells take in sugar and the liver store it. When blood sugar falls, the pancreas releases glucagon, which tells the liver to release stored sugar. In diabetes, this system does not work properly."],["Water and salt","The kidneys filter the blood and adjust how much water and salt leave in urine. When you are short of water, a hormone tells the kidneys to save water, so urine becomes darker, and you feel thirsty. Drinking too little or losing too much water through sweat can cause dehydration."]],
k:[["Normal body temperature","About 37 °C"],["Main method","Negative feedback"],["Temperature control center","Hypothalamus in the brain"],["Blood sugar hormones","Insulin and glucagon"],["Named by","Walter Cannon, 1926"]],
d:[["1865","Claude Bernard describes the body’s stable inner environment"],["1921","Frederick Banting and Charles Best isolate insulin"],["1926","Walter Cannon coins the word homeostasis"],["1932","Cannon explains the idea in his book The Wisdom of the Body"]],
s2:[["Positive feedback","Sometimes the body uses positive feedback, which makes a change bigger instead of smaller. During childbirth, contractions trigger a hormone that causes stronger contractions until the baby is born. Blood clotting also speeds itself up once it starts."],["Fever","A fever is not a breakdown of homeostasis. When fighting an infection, the brain raises its temperature set point on purpose, which may help the immune system work. Very high body temperatures, however, can be dangerous."]],
sp:["Too hot to cool down","Sweating cools the body only if sweat can evaporate. In very hot and humid air, evaporation slows, and body temperature can climb dangerously. Heatstroke, when body temperature rises above about 40 °C, is a medical emergency. Health agencies advise drinking water, resting in the shade and checking on older people during heat waves."],
g:[["Homeostasis","Keeping conditions inside the body stable"],["Negative feedback","A response that reverses a change"],["Hypothalamus","A part of the brain that controls body temperature and thirst"]],
f:["Normal human body temperature is about 37 °C.","Sweating cools the body as the sweat evaporates.","The word homeostasis was coined in 1926."],
fun:"Some animals switch off homeostasis on purpose. Hibernating Arctic ground squirrels let their body temperature drop below freezing."});

ADDART({id:"nocturnal",t:"Nocturnal Animals",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🌙",w:"Nocturnality",r:["owl","bat","tarsier","eye","animalbehavior","sleep"],
p:[["Creatures of the night","Nocturnal animals are active at night and rest during the day."],["Special senses","Big eyes, keen ears and sensitive noses help them in the dark."],["Many reasons","Night brings cooler air, fewer predators and less competition."]],
l:"Nocturnal animals are active mainly at night and rest during the day. Owls, bats, moths, many frogs, cats, hedgehogs, raccoons, kiwis and tarsiers are all nocturnal. Many mammals are active at night, partly because early mammals lived alongside the dinosaurs and may have avoided them by coming out after dark. Night offers cooler temperatures, which helps desert animals save water, and darkness helps animals hide from predators or sneak up on prey. To find their way, nocturnal animals rely on large, sensitive eyes, sharp hearing, a keen sense of smell, whiskers, or echolocation. Animals active at dawn and dusk are called crepuscular, and those active by day are diurnal.",
s:[["Eyes for the dark","Many nocturnal animals have huge eyes with wide pupils that let in as much light as possible. Their eyes are packed with rod cells, which work in dim light. Many also have a mirror-like layer behind the retina, the tapetum lucidum, which bounces light back through the eye. This is why a cat’s or deer’s eyes shine in a flashlight beam."],["Hearing and smell","Owls can hunt in total darkness by sound alone. Barn owls have one ear set higher than the other, which helps them pinpoint where a sound comes from. Kiwis have nostrils at the tip of their long beaks and sniff out worms underground. Many moths find mates by smelling scents released from far away."],["Night lights","Some nocturnal animals make their own light. Fireflies flash coded signals to attract mates, and many deep-sea animals glow. In parts of Southeast Asia, thousands of fireflies in mangrove trees flash together in rhythm."],["Light pollution","Bright city lights confuse nocturnal animals. Moths circle streetlights until they are exhausted, newly hatched sea turtles crawl toward lit roads instead of the sea, and migrating birds crash into lit buildings. Turning off unnecessary lights at night and using shielded, warm-colored lamps help."]],
k:[["Active","At night"],["Rest","During the day"],["Examples","Owls, bats, moths, tarsiers, kiwis"],["Eyeshine comes from","The tapetum lucidum"],["Dawn and dusk animals","Crepuscular"]],
d:[["1938","Donald Griffin begins studying how bats find their way in the dark"],["2017","A study suggests most mammals stayed nocturnal until after the dinosaurs died out"],["2018","A study of 62 mammal species finds they become more nocturnal near people"]],
s2:[["Nocturnal because of us","A 2018 study that compared 62 mammal species found that many become more nocturnal near people, shifting their activity into the night to avoid humans. Animals from deer to tigers to coyotes have been seen doing this."],["Night in the home","Some pets are naturally nocturnal or crepuscular. Hamsters are most active at night, and cats are naturally most active at dawn and dusk, which is why they may run around the house in the early morning."]],
sp:["Tarsiers","Tarsiers, small primates of the islands of Southeast Asia, are among the most specialized nocturnal animals. Each of a tarsier’s eyes is about as big as its brain, and it can turn its head almost 180 degrees to spot insects in the dark. They leap between branches to catch prey."],
g:[["Nocturnal","Active at night"],["Diurnal","Active during the day"],["Tapetum lucidum","A reflective layer behind the eye that helps night vision"]],
f:["A tarsier’s eye is about as big as its brain.","Barn owls can catch prey in total darkness by sound.","Many mammals become more nocturnal when living near people."],
fun:"Kiwis are the only birds with nostrils at the very tip of their beaks, which they use to sniff out worms in the dark."});

ADDART({id:"echolocation",t:"Echolocation",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🔊",w:"Animal echolocation",r:["bat","whale","swiftlets","sonar","sound","nocturnal"],
p:[["Seeing with sound","Echolocation means finding things by listening to echoes."],["Bats and dolphins","Bats, toothed whales and a few birds use it."],["A sound picture","Echoes tell an animal the size, shape and distance of objects."]],
l:"Echolocation is a way of sensing the surroundings by making sounds and listening to the echoes that bounce back. By timing how long an echo takes to return and noticing how it changes, an animal can tell how far away something is, how big it is, what shape it has and which way it is moving. Most bats, toothed whales such as dolphins and sperm whales, some shrews and tenrecs, and a few cave-dwelling birds, including oilbirds and some swiftlets, use echolocation. It lets them hunt and travel in darkness or murky water. People copy the same idea with sonar.",
s:[["How bats do it","Most bats make high-pitched calls through their mouths or noses, often too high for people to hear. As a bat closes in on an insect, it calls faster and faster, up to about 200 calls a second, in a final burst called a feeding buzz. Its large, specially shaped ears catch the faint echoes. Some bats can avoid wires about as thin as a human hair."],["Dolphins and whales","Dolphins make rapid clicks inside their heads and focus them through a fatty organ in the forehead called the melon. Echoes are picked up through the lower jaw and carried to the ear. Dolphins can find fish buried in sand and tell different objects apart by echo alone. Sperm whales make the loudest sounds of any animal."],["Birds and others","Oilbirds of South America and some swiftlets of Asia and the Pacific make clicking sounds that people can hear, using them to navigate in dark caves. Some shrews and tenrecs use simple echolocation too."],["An arms race","Some moths can hear bat calls and dive or dodge to escape. Tiger moths even make their own clicks, which can jam bat echolocation or warn that they taste bad. Some bats have evolved quieter or different-pitched calls to sneak up on moths that can hear."]],
k:[["Used by","Bats, toothed whales, some birds and shrews"],["Bat calls","Up to about 200 per second"],["Dolphin sound focuser","The melon"],["Named by","Donald Griffin, 1944"],["Human version","Sonar"]],
d:[["1793","Lazzaro Spallanzani shows that blinded bats can still fly without bumping into things"],["1938","Donald Griffin and George Pierce record bats’ ultrasonic calls"],["1944","Griffin coins the word echolocation"],["1950s","Scientists show that dolphins use echolocation"]],
s2:[["People who echolocate","Some blind people have learned to echolocate by making tongue clicks and listening to the echoes. Brain scans show that skilled echolocators use parts of the brain normally used for sight. Teachers now train some blind children in click echolocation."],["Inspired technology","Sonar on ships and submarines works like a dolphin’s clicks, and ultrasound scanners use echoes to see inside the body. Engineers study bats to design better sensors for robots, drones and cars."]],
sp:["Oilbirds","Oilbirds of South America live in caves and fly out at night to eat fruit. In the darkness of their caves they make sharp clicking calls that people can hear, using the echoes to avoid walls and find their nests. They are the only nocturnal fruit-eating birds in the world."],
g:[["Echolocation","Finding objects by listening to echoes"],["Echo","A sound that bounces back"],["Ultrasound","Sound too high-pitched for people to hear"]],
f:["Some bats call up to about 200 times a second.","Dolphins hear echoes through their lower jaws.","Some blind people can learn to echolocate."],
fun:"Some bats call as loudly as a smoke alarm, more than 110 decibels, but their calls are too high-pitched for people to hear."});

ADDART({id:"parentalcare",t:"Animal Parents",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐣",w:"Parental care",r:["penguin","seahorse","croc","eagle","animalbehavior","lifecycles"],
p:[["Raising the young","Many animals feed, protect and teach their young."],["Different strategies","Some have many young and leave them; others have few and care for them."],["Mothers, fathers or both","Care can come from the mother, the father or both parents."]],
l:"Parental care is the way animals look after their young after they are born or hatched, or even before. It can mean guarding eggs, building nests, feeding, carrying, keeping babies warm and teaching skills. Animals follow different strategies. Many fish, frogs and insects produce thousands of eggs and give little or no care, so only a few survive. Mammals and birds usually have far fewer young but care for them for weeks, months or years. In most mammals the mother does most of the care, because she makes milk. In most birds both parents help, and in some fish and frogs it is the father who does the work.",
s:[["Devoted fathers","Male emperor penguins balance a single egg on their feet through the Antarctic winter for about two months, eating nothing. Male seahorses carry the eggs in a pouch and give birth to the young. Male giant water bugs carry eggs on their backs, and in many fish, such as some cichlids and catfish, males guard or carry eggs in their mouths."],["Long childhoods","Orangutan mothers nurse and carry their young for years, and a young orangutan stays with its mother for about seven to eight years while it learns which plants to eat and how to build nests. Elephants grow up in herds where aunts and older sisters help care for calves. Long childhoods give time to learn."],["Surprising carers","Crocodile and alligator mothers guard their nests and gently carry hatchlings to the water in their mouths. Some poison dart frogs carry tadpoles on their backs to tiny pools of water in plants and come back to feed them eggs. Some earwigs and spiders guard and feed their young too."],["Helpers and teamwork","In some species, older offspring or other relatives help raise the young. Florida scrub-jays, meerkats and naked mole-rats live in groups where helpers bring food and stand guard. In cuckoos and cowbirds the opposite happens: they lay their eggs in other birds’ nests and let others raise their chicks."]],
k:[["Low-care strategy","Many eggs, little care"],["High-care strategy","Few young, long care"],["Father-only care","Seahorses, many fish, emperor penguins (incubation)"],["Longest childhood among apes","Orangutans, about 7–8 years"],["Nest parasites","Cuckoos and cowbirds"]],
d:[["1964","W. D. Hamilton explains why animals help raise their relatives’ young"],["1972","Robert Trivers publishes his theory of parental investment"]],
s2:[["Why care at all?","Caring for young takes time and energy that a parent could use to survive or have more offspring. Natural selection favors care when it greatly improves the young’s chances, for example in harsh places or when young are born helpless. When young can look after themselves from birth, care is often brief or absent."],["Learning from parents","Some skills are learned from parents. Young chimpanzees learn to crack nuts with stones by watching their mothers, killer whale calves learn hunting methods from their family groups, and songbird chicks learn their songs by listening to adults."]],
sp:["Elephant families","Elephant calves grow up in close family herds led by an older female, the matriarch. Mothers, aunts, older sisters and cousins all help protect and guide the young. Calves drink their mothers’ milk for several years, and young females stay with the family for life."],
g:[["Parental care","Looking after young after they are born or hatched"],["Incubation","Keeping eggs warm so they develop"],["Brood parasite","An animal that tricks others into raising its young"]],
f:["Male seahorses give birth to the young.","Emperor penguin fathers keep an egg warm for about two months.","A young orangutan stays with its mother for about seven to eight years."],
fun:"Discus fish feed their babies a kind of milk: the parents’ skin makes a special mucus that the young nibble for their first weeks."});

;
/* data/prehist5.js */
// Prehistoric Life part 5 (27 November 2026): more rulers of sea and sky, and animals lost in the Ice Age and in recent times.
ADDART({id:"ichthyosaurs",t:"Ichthyosaurs",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐬",w:"Ichthyosaur",r:["marinereptiles","mosasaurus","elasmosaurus","pterosaurs","paleontology","massext","nothosaurus","pliosaurus"],
p:[["Fish lizards","Ichthyosaurs were marine reptiles shaped like dolphins."],["Ocean hunters","They swam fast and hunted fish and squid-like animals."],["Live birth","They gave birth to live young in the sea."]],
l:"Ichthyosaurs were marine reptiles that lived in the oceans from about 250 to 94 million years ago, during the age of dinosaurs, though they were not dinosaurs themselves. Their name means fish lizards. Their streamlined bodies, flippers, fins on their backs and crescent-shaped tails made them look remarkably like dolphins and sharks, an example of how different animals can evolve similar shapes for the same way of life. Most were about 2 to 4 meters long, but some were giants: Shonisaurus reached about 15 meters, and Ichthyotitan may have been about 25 meters long. They breathed air, hunted fish and squid-like animals, and gave birth to live young.",
s:[["Built for speed","Ichthyosaurs had smooth, torpedo-shaped bodies and powerful tails. Fossils with preserved outlines show a fleshy fin on the back and a tail with two lobes, like a shark’s. Studies suggest they were among the most efficient swimmers of all marine reptiles, cruising like modern dolphins."],["Giant eyes","Ichthyosaurs had the largest eyes, relative to their bodies, of any known animals with backbones. Temnodontosaurus had eyes about 25 centimeters across, as big as a dinner plate. Huge eyes let them see in dim, deep water and may have helped them hunt far below the surface."],["Babies at sea","Ichthyosaurs could not crawl onto land to lay eggs, so they gave birth in the water. Many fossils show unborn young inside their mothers, and some show babies being born tail first, as dolphins are, so they would not drown before taking their first breath."],["Mary Anning’s finds","In 1811 Joseph Anning found an ichthyosaur skull on the cliffs at Lyme Regis in southern England, and his sister Mary, then about 12, found the rest of the skeleton. Mary Anning went on to become one of the greatest fossil hunters in history."]],
k:[["Lived","About 250 to 94 million years ago"],["Typical length","About 2–4 m"],["Largest","Ichthyotitan, perhaps about 25 m"],["Food","Fish, squid-like animals, ammonites"],["Babies","Born live in the water"]],
d:[["c. 250 Ma","The first ichthyosaurs appear soon after the Great Dying"],["1811","Joseph and Mary Anning find an ichthyosaur at Lyme Regis"],["1977","Nevada names the giant ichthyosaur Shonisaurus its state fossil"],["2020","An 11-year-old, Ruby Reynolds, finds part of a giant jawbone in Somerset"],["2024","Scientists name the jawbone’s owner Ichthyotitan severnensis"]],
s2:[["Why they disappeared","Ichthyosaurs died out about 94 million years ago, nearly 30 million years before the asteroid that ended the age of dinosaurs. Changes in ocean temperature and chemistry, and competition from other predators, may have been to blame."],["A girl and her father","Ruby Reynolds and her father Justin were fossil hunting on a beach at Blue Anchor in Somerset, England, in 2020 when Ruby found a piece of a huge jawbone. They later found more pieces and worked with scientists to describe Ichthyotitan, possibly the largest marine reptile ever known."]],
sp:["Dolphins and ichthyosaurs","Dolphins are mammals and ichthyosaurs were reptiles, separated by hundreds of millions of years of evolution. Yet both evolved streamlined bodies, flippers, back fins and live birth because they faced the same challenges of fast swimming in open water. Scientists call this convergent evolution."],
g:[["Ichthyosaur","A dolphin-shaped marine reptile of the age of dinosaurs"],["Convergent evolution","When unrelated animals evolve similar features"],["Marine reptile","A reptile that lives mostly in the sea"]],
f:["Ichthyosaurs had the largest eyes, for their size, of any known backbone animals.","They gave birth to live young, tail first.","Ichthyotitan may have been about 25 meters long."],
fun:"Some ichthyosaur fossils are so well preserved that scientists have found traces of skin pigment and blubber, suggesting they were dark on top and lighter underneath."});

ADDART({id:"rhamphorhynchus",t:"Rhamphorhynchus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🪽",w:"Rhamphorhynchus",r:["pterosaurs","pteranodon","quetzalcoatlus","archaeopteryx","paleontology","dimorphodon"],
p:[["A long-tailed pterosaur","Rhamphorhynchus was a flying reptile with a long tail."],["Fish catcher","Its sharp, forward-pointing teeth gripped slippery fish."],["Perfect fossils","Fine limestone in Germany preserved even its wings and tail vane."]],
l:"Rhamphorhynchus was a pterosaur, a flying reptile, that lived about 150 million years ago in the Late Jurassic Period, in what is now southern Germany. It had a wingspan of up to about 1.8 meters, a long, stiff tail ending in a diamond-shaped vane, and a beak full of needle-like teeth that pointed forward to spear and grip fish. Its name means beak snout. Many beautifully preserved skeletons have been found in the fine limestone of Solnhofen in Bavaria, the same rocks that preserved Archaeopteryx. Some even show the outline of the wing membranes, the tail vane and the last meal in the stomach.",
s:[["Long tails and short tails","Early pterosaurs such as Rhamphorhynchus had long tails, short necks and narrow wings. Later pterosaurs, called pterodactyloids, including Pteranodon and Quetzalcoatlus, had short tails and long necks. The tail vane of Rhamphorhynchus may have acted like a rudder, helping it steer."],["A fishing flyer","Rhamphorhynchus probably skimmed low over lagoons, snatching fish from the water. Fossils show fish bones and remains of squid-like animals in its stomach. Its teeth meshed together like the bars of a cage, ideal for holding wriggling prey."],["Growing up","Most known specimens are young animals. By comparing sizes, scientists found that Rhamphorhynchus grew fairly slowly and that the shape of its tail vane changed as it grew, from oval in the young to triangular in large adults."],["Caught by a fish","One remarkable fossil shows a Rhamphorhynchus with its wing caught in the jaws of a large fish called Aspidorhynchus. The pterosaur had just swallowed a small fish of its own. The two may have died together and sunk into the lagoon’s still, oxygen-poor water."]],
k:[["Lived","About 150 million years ago"],["Period","Late Jurassic"],["Wingspan","Up to about 1.8 m"],["Food","Fish and squid-like animals"],["Best fossils","Solnhofen limestone, Germany"]],
d:[["c. 150 Ma","Rhamphorhynchus flies over lagoons in what is now Germany"],["1831","Georg August Goldfuss describes the first known specimen"],["1846","Hermann von Meyer names the animal Rhamphorhynchus"],["2012","Scientists describe fossils of Rhamphorhynchus caught by Aspidorhynchus fish"]],
s2:[["The Solnhofen lagoons","About 150 million years ago, southern Germany was dotted with warm, salty lagoons near the sea. Their bottom waters were so poor in oxygen that few scavengers lived there, so animals that sank were buried in fine mud and preserved in amazing detail, becoming the Solnhofen limestone."],["Seen with ultraviolet light","Shining ultraviolet light on Solnhofen fossils makes soft tissues glow. This has revealed the shape of Rhamphorhynchus wings, the attachment of the wing membrane to the legs and details of its head crest that are invisible in normal light."]],
sp:["Not a dinosaur, not a bird","Pterosaurs like Rhamphorhynchus were close relatives of dinosaurs but not dinosaurs themselves, and they were not ancestors of birds. They flew on wings of skin stretched mainly from a hugely long fourth finger, while birds fly on feathers and bats on skin stretched between several fingers."],
g:[["Pterosaur","A flying reptile of the age of dinosaurs"],["Wing membrane","The skin that formed a pterosaur’s wing"],["Lagoon","A shallow body of water separated from the sea"]],
f:["Rhamphorhynchus means beak snout.","Its tail ended in a diamond-shaped vane.","One fossil was caught in the jaws of a fish."],
fun:"Rhamphorhynchus fossils are so common in the Solnhofen limestone that more than a hundred specimens are known, from hatchling-sized young to large adults."});

ADDART({id:"basilosaurus",t:"Basilosaurus",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐋",w:"Basilosaurus",r:["whale","livyatan","evo","fossils","marinereptiles","egyptcountry"],
p:[["An ancient whale","Basilosaurus was an early whale that lived about 40 million years ago."],["Long and slender","It was shaped like a huge eel, up to about 18 meters long."],["Tiny legs","It still had small hind legs, a clue to whales’ land-living ancestors."]],
l:"Basilosaurus was an early whale that lived about 41 to 34 million years ago, during the Eocene Epoch, in warm seas that covered parts of today’s United States, Egypt and Jordan. It was long and slender, up to about 17 to 20 meters, with a small head, strong jaws and sharp, saw-edged teeth. Its name means king lizard, because the first scientist to study its bones in 1834 thought it was a giant reptile. Basilosaurus still had tiny hind legs, complete with knees and toes, which were far too small to walk on. They show that whales descended from four-legged land mammals.",
s:[["A mistaken name","In 1834 the American naturalist Richard Harlan studied huge backbones found in Louisiana and named the animal Basilosaurus, believing it was a sea reptile. A few years later the British anatomist Richard Owen saw that its teeth had two roots, like a mammal’s, and renamed it Zeuglodon. By the rules of naming, the first name stayed, even though it is wrong."],["Legs that tell a story","In 1990, scientists described well-preserved hind legs of Basilosaurus from Egypt. Each leg was shorter than a person’s arm, on an animal more than 15 meters long. The legs were probably useless for walking but may have helped during mating. Fossils of older whales, such as Pakicetus and Ambulocetus, show the steps from land to sea."],["Top predator","Basilosaurus was a top predator of Eocene seas. Bite marks on the skulls of a smaller whale, Dorudon, show that Basilosaurus attacked them, including young ones. Its stomach contents also include fish and sharks."],["The Valley of the Whales","At Wadi Al-Hitan in Egypt’s Western Desert, hundreds of skeletons of early whales lie in sandstone that was once the seafloor. The site, now a UNESCO World Heritage Site, contains many Basilosaurus and Dorudon skeletons, some lying exposed on the desert sand."]],
k:[["Lived","About 41 to 34 million years ago"],["Length","About 15–20 m"],["Group","Early whales (archaeocetes)"],["Name meaning","King lizard"],["Hind legs","Shorter than a person’s arm, too small to walk on"]],
d:[["c. 40 Ma","Basilosaurus hunts in warm seas"],["1834","Richard Harlan names Basilosaurus, thinking it is a reptile"],["1839","Richard Owen shows it is a mammal"],["1990","Scientists describe its tiny hind legs from Egypt"],["2005","Wadi Al-Hitan becomes a World Heritage Site"]],
s2:[["A fake sea serpent","In 1845 a showman named Albert Koch joined Basilosaurus bones from several animals into a 35-meter skeleton and exhibited it in New York as a sea serpent called Hydrarchos. Scientists soon exposed the hoax, but crowds paid to see it."],["State fossil","Basilosaurus cetoides is the state fossil of both Alabama and Mississippi, where many of its bones have been found. In parts of the American South, early settlers even used its large vertebrae as fireplace supports."]],
sp:["From land to sea","Whales evolved from small, hoofed land mammals related to today’s hippos. Over about 10 million years, their descendants grew more aquatic, their front legs became flippers and their hind legs shrank. Basilosaurus, with its tiny legs, is a late step in that journey; modern whales still have small leftover hip bones inside their bodies."],
g:[["Archaeocete","An early, extinct kind of whale"],["Eocene","The epoch from about 56 to 34 million years ago"],["Vestigial","A body part that has lost most of its original use"]],
f:["Basilosaurus means king lizard, though it was a mammal.","Its hind legs had knees and toes but were shorter than a person’s arm.","It is the state fossil of Alabama and Mississippi."],
fun:"A showman once built a fake 35-meter sea serpent out of Basilosaurus bones and charged people to see it."});

ADDART({id:"glyptodon",t:"Glyptodon",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐢",w:"Glyptodon",r:["armadillos","iceagemammals","groundsloths","extinction","humanorigins"],
p:[["A giant armadillo relative","Glyptodon was a huge, armored mammal of South America."],["A shell like a dome","Its body was covered by a shell made of hundreds of bony plates."],["Lost in the Ice Age","It died out about 12,000 to 11,000 years ago."]],
l:"Glyptodon was a large armored mammal that lived in South America from about 3 million years ago until the end of the last Ice Age, about 12,000 to 11,000 years ago. It was a relative of armadillos, and studies of ancient DNA show that glyptodonts belong within the armadillo family tree. Glyptodon was about the size of a small car, up to about 3.5 meters long, and the largest kinds may have weighed around 2 tonnes. Its rounded shell was made of more than a thousand bony plates locked together, and it had a bony cap on its head and armored rings around its tail. It ate grasses and other plants. Early people in South America lived alongside glyptodonts and hunted them.",
s:[["A walking fortress","Glyptodon’s shell, called a carapace, was made of small bony plates called osteoderms, fused into a solid dome up to about 1.7 meters long. Unlike armadillos, it could not roll up. Its legs were short and thick to carry the heavy shell, and its backbone was partly fused to support it."],["Clubbed relatives","Glyptodon’s relative Doedicurus had a tail ending in a spiked club, which it may have swung at rivals or predators. Damage found on some glyptodont shells suggests they fought each other with their tails, much as some deer fight with antlers."],["Grazing the pampas","Glyptodon lived on the grasslands and open woodlands of South America, from the Argentine pampas to the high Andes. Its deep jaws and ever-growing teeth were suited to grinding tough grasses."],["People and glyptodonts","Bones of glyptodonts with cut marks show that early humans butchered them. In 2024 scientists reported cut marks on the bones of a glyptodont called Neosclerocalyptus in Argentina, about 21,000 years old, which, if confirmed, would be among the oldest signs of people in the Americas. Stories that people sheltered under empty shells are popular, but there is little evidence for them."]],
k:[["Lived","About 3 million to 11,000 years ago"],["Where","South America"],["Length","Up to about 3.5 m"],["Weight","Up to about 2 tonnes in the largest species"],["Closest living relatives","Armadillos"]],
d:[["c. 3 Ma","Glyptodon appears in South America"],["1839","Richard Owen formally names Glyptodon"],["2016","Ancient DNA shows glyptodonts were giant armadillos"],["2024","Cut-marked glyptodont bones about 21,000 years old are reported from Argentina"],["c. 11,000 years ago","The last glyptodonts die out"]],
s2:[["The Great American Interchange","About 3 million years ago, the Isthmus of Panama joined North and South America. Animals crossed in both directions. Glyptodonts and ground sloths spread north, some reaching the southern United States, while cats, dogs and horses spread south."],["Why they vanished","Glyptodon died out at the end of the Ice Age along with mammoths, ground sloths and other giant animals. Scientists debate how much was caused by hunting by newly arrived humans and how much by the warming climate. Many think both played a part."]],
sp:["Darwin and the glyptodont","On his voyage on HMS Beagle, Charles Darwin collected pieces of glyptodont shell in Argentina and Uruguay in the 1830s. Noticing that the fossil giants resembled the small armadillos still living there helped lead him to his ideas about how species change over time."],
g:[["Carapace","The hard upper shell of an animal"],["Osteoderm","A bony plate in the skin"],["Megafauna","Large animals, especially those of the Ice Age"]],
f:["Glyptodon’s shell was made of more than a thousand bony plates.","It could not roll up like an armadillo.","Darwin collected its fossils on the Beagle voyage."],
fun:"Glyptodon’s relative Doedicurus had a spiked tail club that could deliver a blow strong enough to crack another glyptodont’s shell."});

ADDART({id:"dodo",t:"The Dodo",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🦤",w:"Dodo",r:["extinction","endangered","birds","conservation","direwolf"],
p:[["A flightless bird","The dodo was a large bird that could not fly."],["Only on Mauritius","It lived only on the island of Mauritius in the Indian Ocean."],["Gone in a century","It died out less than a hundred years after sailors arrived."]],
l:"The dodo was a large, flightless bird that lived only on Mauritius, an island in the Indian Ocean east of Madagascar. It stood about 1 meter tall and probably weighed around 10 to 18 kilograms, with gray or brownish feathers, a big hooked beak, stubby wings and a tuft of tail feathers. Its closest living relative is the Nicobar pigeon. Dutch sailors first described the dodo in 1598. Within less than a century it was gone: the last widely accepted sighting was in 1662. Hunting by sailors played a part, but rats, pigs, monkeys and other animals brought by people, which ate eggs and destroyed forests, were probably even more harmful. The dodo became a worldwide symbol of extinction.",
s:[["A giant pigeon","The dodo’s ancestors were pigeons that flew to Mauritius millions of years ago. With plenty of fallen fruit to eat and no predators on the island, they grew larger and lost the ability to fly. Ancient DNA shows the dodo’s closest relatives include the Nicobar pigeon and the extinct Rodrigues solitaire of a nearby island."],["Not so foolish","The dodo is often described as fat and stupid, but this picture is unfair. It had no reason to fear people because it had evolved without predators. Many old paintings showed overfed birds kept in captivity. Studies of its skeleton suggest it was a fairly agile bird adapted to its forest home."],["How it disappeared","Dutch settlers arrived on Mauritius in 1638. They brought rats, pigs, goats, monkeys and dogs, which ate dodo eggs and chicks from nests on the ground and competed for food. Forests were cut down. Within a few decades the dodo was gone, though the exact date of the last bird is uncertain."],["Few remains","No complete stuffed dodo survives. The best remains are a head and foot at Oxford University, which still contain some soft tissue, and skeletons assembled from bones found in a marsh called Mare aux Songes, first dug up in 1865. Paintings and sailors’ descriptions fill in the rest."]],
k:[["Home","Mauritius, Indian Ocean"],["Height","About 1 m"],["Closest living relative","Nicobar pigeon"],["First described","1598, by Dutch sailors"],["Last widely accepted sighting","1662"]],
d:[["1598","Dutch sailors land on Mauritius and describe the dodo"],["1638","The Dutch begin settling Mauritius"],["1662","The last widely accepted sighting of a dodo"],["1865","Bones are dug up at the Mare aux Songes marsh"],["2023","A company announces a project to try to recreate a dodo-like bird"]],
s2:[["As dead as a dodo","The phrase dead as a dodo means completely gone. In 1865, the same year dodo bones were found, Lewis Carroll included a Dodo in Alice’s Adventures in Wonderland, and the bird became famous around the world. It appears on the coat of arms of Mauritius."],["Bringing it back?","In 2023 the US company Colossal Biosciences announced a plan to create a dodo-like bird by editing the genes of the Nicobar pigeon, and in 2025 and 2026 it reported progress such as growing pigeon germ cells in the laboratory. Many scientists say any such bird would be a modified pigeon, not a true dodo, and that protecting living birds matters more."]],
sp:["Mauritius today","Mauritius has lost many species since people arrived, but it is also known for conservation successes. The Mauritius kestrel fell to only four known wild birds in 1974, and the pink pigeon was reduced to about ten in the 1990s. Careful breeding and habitat protection brought both back to hundreds of birds."],
g:[["Flightless","Unable to fly"],["Extinct","No longer existing anywhere"],["Invasive species","An animal or plant brought to a new place where it causes harm"]],
f:["The dodo was a kind of giant pigeon.","It was gone less than a century after it was first described.","The best dodo remains are a head and foot at Oxford."],
fun:"The dodo’s scientific name, Raphus cucullatus, means hooded bustard-like bird, because early naturalists did not realize it was a pigeon."});

ADDART({id:"thylacine",t:"The Thylacine",c:"Animals and Nature",i:"🐅",w:"Thylacine",r:["tasmania","extinction","kangaroo","australiacountry","direwolf","endangered","megalania"],
p:[["The Tasmanian tiger","The thylacine was a striped, dog-like marsupial."],["A pouched hunter","It was the largest meat-eating marsupial of modern times."],["Lost in 1936","The last known thylacine died in a zoo in Hobart in 1936."]],
l:"The thylacine, also called the Tasmanian tiger or Tasmanian wolf, was a carnivorous marsupial that looked like a large dog with dark stripes across its back and tail. Like kangaroos, the mother carried her young in a pouch. It was the largest meat-eating marsupial to survive into modern times, about 1 to 1.3 meters long plus a stiff tail. Thylacines once lived across Australia and New Guinea but disappeared from the mainland about 3,000 years ago, surviving only on the island of Tasmania. After Europeans settled there, thylacines were hunted as threats to sheep, and a government bounty paid for more than 2,000 killed. The last known thylacine died at Hobart’s Beaumaris Zoo on 7 September 1936.",
s:[["Like a dog, but not a dog","Thylacines were not related to dogs or wolves. Their resemblance is a famous example of convergent evolution: as hunters of similar prey, they evolved similar skulls, teeth and bodies. A thylacine could open its jaws extremely wide, and it may have hunted kangaroos, wallabies and birds, mostly at night."],["A pouch for both","Female thylacines carried up to four young in a backward-facing pouch. Unusually, males also had a pouch-like flap of skin that protected their body when running through thick bush."],["The bounty years","Sheep farmers blamed thylacines for killing their flocks, though wild dogs and poor farming caused many losses. From 1888 to 1909 the Tasmanian government paid a bounty for each thylacine killed, and more than 2,000 were paid for. Disease, habitat loss and competition with dogs also took a toll."],["Too late","Thylacines became protected by law in July 1936, just 59 days before the last known animal died in captivity. Searches and reported sightings have continued ever since, but none has been confirmed. The thylacine was officially declared extinct in 1982 by the IUCN and in 1986 by the Tasmanian government."]],
k:[["Also called","Tasmanian tiger, Tasmanian wolf"],["Type","Carnivorous marsupial"],["Length","About 1–1.3 m plus tail"],["Last known animal died","7 September 1936, Hobart"],["Declared extinct","1982 (IUCN), 1986 (Tasmania)"]],
d:[["c. 3,000 years ago","Thylacines disappear from mainland Australia"],["1888","Tasmania begins paying a bounty for killed thylacines"],["1933","The last known thylacine is captured and taken to Beaumaris Zoo"],["1936","The last known thylacine dies on 7 September"],["1986","Tasmania declares the thylacine extinct"]],
s2:[["Films of the last tiger","Black-and-white films taken at Beaumaris Zoo show the last known thylacine pacing, yawning and resting. In 2021 a restored colorized version was released, and in 2020 newly found film from 1935 was shared. These short clips are among the only moving images of the animal."],["De-extinction efforts","In 2022 the company Colossal Biosciences and the University of Melbourne announced a project to recreate a thylacine-like animal by editing the genes of its living relative, the fat-tailed dunnart. In 2024 they reported a nearly complete thylacine genome. Many scientists doubt such an animal would be a true thylacine and urge protecting living species first."]],
sp:["Threatened Species Day","Australia marks National Threatened Species Day each year on 7 September, the anniversary of the death of the last known thylacine. The day reminds people of animals still at risk, such as the Tasmanian devil, the thylacine’s living relative, which has been badly hit by a contagious face cancer."],
g:[["Marsupial","A mammal whose young develop in a pouch"],["Bounty","A reward paid for killing an animal considered a pest"],["De-extinction","Trying to bring back an extinct species or something like it"]],
f:["The last known thylacine died on 7 September 1936.","Male thylacines had a pouch-like flap too.","Thylacines were protected only 59 days before the last one died."],
fun:"A thylacine could open its jaws to an angle of about 80 degrees, far wider than a dog can."});

;
